(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e3a.a5aab50f02.js"]={"31":[{"id":"sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which bacterium is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries?","choices":{"A":"Vibrio cholerae","B":"Salmonella typhi","C":"Yersinia enterocolitica","D":"Campylobacter jejuni","E":"Shigella dysenteriae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Campylobacter jejuni is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-resource countries and an important cause of traveler's diarrhea and food poisoning, most often from improperly cooked chicken. Vibrio cholerae is endemic in India and Africa, not the leading bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries. Salmonella typhi is endemic in countries such as India, Mexico, and the Philippines and mainly affects travelers, not high-income populations at large. Yersinia enterocolitica causes clustered winter cases in northern and central Europe but is far less common than Campylobacter. Shigella is highly endemic in under-resourced countries. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Campylobacter are comma-shaped, flagellated, gram-negative organisms diagnosed mainly by stool culture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Campylobacter infection can be followed by reactive arthritis, mainly in HLA-B27–positive patients (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vibrio cholerae is endemic in India and Africa, not the leading bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries.","B":"Salmonella typhi is endemic in countries such as India, Mexico, and the Philippines and mainly affects travelers, not high-income populations at large.","C":"Yersinia enterocolitica causes clustered winter cases in northern and central Europe but is far less common than Campylobacter.","E":"Shigella is highly endemic in under-resourced countries."}},{"id":"sdl31_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the known reservoir of Shigella?","choices":{"A":"Poultry","B":"Humans","C":"Shellfish","D":"Cattle","E":"Swine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Humans are the known reservoir of Shigella. Up to 10^9 organisms per gram of stool and an infective dose of several hundred organisms make fecal-oral spread by water and food very efficient. Poultry is a reservoir for Campylobacter and nontyphoid Salmonella, whereas humans are the only known Shigella reservoir. Shellfish and plankton are the reservoir of Vibrio cholerae, not Shigella. Cattle are the reservoir of EHEC O157:H7, whereas Shigella has no animal reservoir. Pigs are a reservoir for Yersinia and Campylobacter, but Shigella infects only humans. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shigella are gram-negative, unencapsulated, nonmotile facultative anaerobes closely related to enteroinvasive E. coli (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the United States, children in daycare, migrant workers, travelers, and nursing home residents are most affected (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Poultry is a reservoir for Campylobacter and nontyphoid Salmonella, whereas humans are the only known Shigella reservoir.","C":"Shellfish and plankton are the reservoir of Vibrio cholerae, not Shigella.","D":"Cattle are the reservoir of EHEC O157:H7, whereas Shigella has no animal reservoir.","E":"Pigs are a reservoir for Yersinia and Campylobacter, but Shigella infects only humans."}},{"id":"sdl31_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal sites does Yersinia infection preferentially involve?","choices":{"A":"Rectum, sigmoid, and left colon","B":"Stomach, duodenum, and jejunum","C":"Ileum, appendix, and right colon","D":"Esophagus, stomach, and duodenum","E":"Jejunum, transverse colon, and rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Yersinia infections preferentially involve the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Organisms proliferate in lymphoid tissue, causing Peyer patch and regional node hyperplasia. The rectum, sigmoid, and left colon are the favored sites of Shigella. Yersinia spares the stomach and proximal small bowel. The esophagus and stomach are not sites of this infection. It does not favor the jejunum, transverse colon, or rectum. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Yersinia infections are linked to pork, raw milk, and contaminated water and tend to cluster in winter (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Peyer patch and lymphatic involvement can mimic acute appendicitis in teenagers and young adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The rectum, sigmoid, and left colon are the favored sites of Shigella.","B":"Yersinia spares the stomach and proximal small bowel.","D":"The esophagus and stomach are not sites of this infection.","E":"It does not favor the jejunum, transverse colon, or rectum."}},{"id":"sdl31_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which E. coli group is the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC) are the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea and spread via contaminated food or water. In under-resourced countries, children younger than 2 years are particularly susceptible. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes sporadic and outbreak bloody diarrhea from undercooked beef and HUS, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteroinvasive E. coli causes Shigella-like dysentery in young children in under-resourced countries, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteropathogenic E. coli causes endemic infant diarrhea and nursery outbreaks in children under 2, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteroaggregative E. coli causes prolonged nonbloody diarrhea via biofilm, but ETEC remains the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ETEC are noninvasive and produce heat-labile and heat-stable toxins (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: like cholera, ETEC infection produces mild histologic changes (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes sporadic and outbreak bloody diarrhea from undercooked beef and HUS, not traveler's diarrhea.","C":"Enteroinvasive E. coli causes Shigella-like dysentery in young children in under-resourced countries, not traveler's diarrhea.","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli causes endemic infant diarrhea and nursery outbreaks in children under 2, not traveler's diarrhea.","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli causes prolonged nonbloody diarrhea via biofilm, but ETEC remains the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea."}},{"id":"sdl31_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":5,"stem":"Which E. coli group is bacteriologically similar to Shigella and invades epithelial cells without producing toxins?","choices":{"A":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","B":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","C":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteroinvasive E. coli (EIEC) resemble Shigella bacteriologically. They do not produce toxins but invade epithelial cells and cause a nonspecific acute self-limited colitis. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli produces Shiga-like toxin and is not the toxin-free, Shigella-like invader. Enterotoxigenic E. coli is noninvasive and acts through heat-labile and heat-stable toxins. Enteropathogenic E. coli attaches and effaces microvilli without invading epithelial cells. Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilms on the mucosa rather than invading epithelial cells. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EIEC infections are most common among young children in under-resourced countries (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: EIEC are transmitted via food, water, or person-to-person contact (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli produces Shiga-like toxin and is not the toxin-free, Shigella-like invader.","C":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli is noninvasive and acts through heat-labile and heat-stable toxins.","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli attaches and effaces microvilli without invading epithelial cells.","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilms on the mucosa rather than invading epithelial cells."}},{"id":"sdl31_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":5,"stem":"What does the heat-stable toxin of ETEC bind on enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Adenylate cyclase","B":"GM1 ganglioside receptor","C":"Rho family GTPases","D":"Guanylate cyclase","E":"Beta-1 integrin receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ETEC heat-stable toxin (ST) binds guanylate cyclase and raises intracellular cGMP; its effects on epithelial ion transport resemble those of the heat-labile toxin. Adenylate cyclase activation and raised cAMP is the action of the heat-labile toxin, not the heat-stable toxin. GM1 ganglioside is the binding receptor for cholera-like heat-labile toxin. Rho GTPases are targets of C. difficile toxins, not ETEC heat-stable toxin, which raises cGMP via guanylate cyclase. Beta-1 integrins are bound by Yersinia adhesins during M-cell invasion, not by ETEC heat-stable toxin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ETEC heat-labile toxin resembles cholera toxin and activates adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP and chloride secretion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: ETEC are noninvasive, so mucosal biopsies show mild change (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Adenylate cyclase activation and raised cAMP is the action of the heat-labile toxin, not the heat-stable toxin.","B":"GM1 ganglioside is the binding receptor for cholera-like heat-labile toxin.","C":"Rho GTPases are targets of C. difficile toxins, not ETEC heat-stable toxin, which raises cGMP via guanylate cyclase.","E":"Beta-1 integrins are bound by Yersinia adhesins during M-cell invasion, not by ETEC heat-stable toxin."}},{"id":"sdl31_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":5,"stem":"What type of organism is Tropheryma whipplei?","choices":{"A":"Gram-negative curved spirochete","B":"Gram-positive actinomycete","C":"Acid-fast mycobacterial rod","D":"Flagellated intestinal protozoan","E":"Gram-negative comma-shaped rod"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Whipple's original postmortem found foamy macrophages filled with gram-positive microbes; the organism was identified as an actinomycete in 1992 and named Tropheryma whipplei. T. whipplei is not a spirochete; it is a gram-positive actinomycete. T. whipplei is a bacterium (actinomycete), not a flagellated protozoan like Giardia. A gram-negative comma-shaped rod describes Vibrio or Campylobacter, not the gram-positive actinomycete T. whipplei. The acid-fast stain separates the two: mycobacteria stain positively, while T. whipplei does not. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tropheryma whipplei is an intracellular gram-positive organism (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: intact rod-shaped bacilli can be seen within macrophages by electron microscopy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"T. whipplei is not a spirochete; it is a gram-positive actinomycete.","D":"T. whipplei is a bacterium (actinomycete), not a flagellated protozoan like Giardia.","E":"A gram-negative comma-shaped rod describes Vibrio or Campylobacter, not the gram-positive actinomycete T. whipplei.","C":"The acid-fast stain separates the two: mycobacteria stain positively, while T. whipplei does not."}},{"id":"sdl31_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which stain result best separates Whipple disease from intestinal mycobacterial infection?","choices":{"A":"Positive mucicarmine stain","B":"Positive Gram stain result","C":"Negative acid-fast stain","D":"Positive Congo red stain","E":"Negative Giemsa stain result"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both Whipple disease and intestinal mycobacterial infection show foamy macrophages with PAS-positive organisms. Mycobacteria are acid-fast positive, while T. whipplei is not. Mucicarmine highlights capsules such as Cryptococcus and does not separate Whipple disease from mycobacterial infection. Congo red stains amyloid, not intracellular bacteria, so it cannot distinguish Whipple disease from mycobacteria. Giemsa stains organisms such as H. pylori, Chlamydia, Borrelia, Rickettsia, trypanosomes, and Plasmodium; Whipple disease is separated by PAS positivity and mycobacteria by acid-fast staining. Robbins notes that Whipple and mycobacterial organisms are both PAS-positive, and the acid-fast stain separates them because mycobacteria stain while T. whipplei does not; a Gram stain is not the discriminator given. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease shows PAS-positive foamy macrophages in the intestinal lamina propria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: mycobacterial infection can mimic Whipple disease, particularly in an immunocompromised host (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucicarmine highlights capsules such as Cryptococcus and does not separate Whipple disease from mycobacterial infection.","D":"Congo red stains amyloid, not intracellular bacteria, so it cannot distinguish Whipple disease from mycobacteria.","E":"Giemsa stains organisms such as H. pylori, Chlamydia, Borrelia, Rickettsia, trypanosomes, and Plasmodium; Whipple disease is separated by PAS positivity and mycobacteria by acid-fast staining.","B":"Robbins notes that Whipple and mycobacterial organisms are both PAS-positive, and the acid-fast stain separates them because mycobacteria stain while T. whipplei does not; a Gram stain is not the discriminator given."}},{"id":"sdl31_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which demographic group is Whipple disease most common in?","choices":{"A":"East Asian women","B":"West African children","C":"Native American teens","D":"South Asian older women","E":"Northern European men"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Whipple disease is most common in males of Northern European descent, particularly farmers and others with occupational exposure to soil or animals. East Asian women, West African children, Native American teens, and South Asian older women are outside that group. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease is most common in older males (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: extraintestinal symptoms may precede malabsorption by months or years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"East Asian women are outside the described group.","B":"West African children are outside the described group.","C":"Native American teenagers are outside the described group.","D":"South Asian older women are outside the described group."}},{"id":"sdl31_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Which virus has become the most common cause of diarrhea worldwide as rotavirus vaccine use has grown?","choices":{"A":"Norovirus","B":"Rotavirus","C":"Adenovirus","D":"Astrovirus","E":"Sapovirus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With increasing rotavirus vaccine use, norovirus has become the most common cause of diarrhea worldwide, responsible for about 20% of acute gastroenteritis. Rotavirus incidence has fallen with vaccination, which is exactly why norovirus now leads as the cause of diarrhea worldwide. Adenovirus causes endemic pediatric diarrhea and opportunistic infection but is not the leading cause worldwide. First Aid names norovirus the most common cause of viral diarrhea in developed countries as rotavirus falls with vaccination, not astrovirus. First Aid names norovirus the most common cause of viral diarrhea in developed countries as rotavirus falls with vaccination, not sapovirus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norovirus is an icosahedral virus with a single-stranded RNA genome (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: infectious particles can be shed before symptoms and for up to 8 weeks afterward in healthy individuals (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Rotavirus incidence has fallen with vaccination, which is exactly why norovirus now leads as the cause of diarrhea worldwide.","C":"Adenovirus causes endemic pediatric diarrhea and opportunistic infection but is not the leading cause worldwide.","D":"First Aid names norovirus the most common cause of viral diarrhea in developed countries as rotavirus falls with vaccination, not astrovirus.","E":"First Aid names norovirus the most common cause of viral diarrhea in developed countries as rotavirus falls with vaccination, not sapovirus."}},{"id":"sdl31_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Which cells does rotavirus selectively infect and destroy?","choices":{"A":"Colonic crypt stem cells","B":"Gastric parietal cells","C":"Paneth cells at crypt bases","D":"Microfold cells of Peyer patches","E":"Mature villus enterocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rotavirus selectively infects and destroys mature small intestinal enterocytes, and immature cells repopulate the villi, so absorption falls and net secretion plus an osmotic component produce watery diarrhea. Colonic crypt stem cells are not the target; rotavirus attacks the small intestine. Gastric parietal cells are not infected by rotavirus, which causes enteritis rather than gastritis. Paneth cells at crypt bases secrete antimicrobial products and are not the cells rotavirus destroys. Microfold cells of Peyer patches are the entry route for organisms such as Salmonella, not rotavirus targets. Sources: Robbins ch 17, rotavirus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rotavirus destroys mature small intestinal enterocytes and causes malabsorptive and secretory diarrhea (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Oral rehydration is the mainstay, and vaccination prevents severe infant disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colonic crypt stem cells are not the","B":"Gastric parietal cells are not infected by","C":"Paneth cells at crypt bases secrete antimicrobial","D":"Microfold cells of Peyer patches are the"}},{"id":"sdl31_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Enteric adenovirus is a common cause of diarrhea in children. Which adult group does it also commonly affect?","choices":{"A":"Healthy pregnant women","B":"Cruise ship passengers","C":"Young adult travelers","D":"Immunocompromised adults","E":"Healthy daycare center staff"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes adenovirus as a common cause of pediatric diarrhea that also affects older immunocompromised patients, in whom biopsies often show villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia. Healthy pregnant women are not described as a group commonly affected by enteric adenovirus. Cruise ship passengers are the classic victims of norovirus outbreaks rather than adenovirus. Young adult travelers most often get traveler's diarrhea from enterotoxigenic E. coli. Healthy daycare center staff are linked to rotavirus outbreaks in daycare settings more than to adenovirus. Sources: Robbins ch 17, viral gastroenteritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enteric adenovirus causes pediatric diarrhea and disease in immunocompromised adults, with villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Consider adenovirus in a transplant recipient with prolonged diarrhea and vomiting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Healthy pregnant women are not described as","B":"Cruise ship passengers are the classic victims","C":"Young adult travelers most often get traveler's","E":"Healthy daycare center staff are linked to"}},{"id":"sdl31_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"How is giardiasis usually documented?","choices":{"A":"Modified acid-fast stool stain","B":"Colonic biopsy for trophozoites","C":"Stool cyst immunofluorescence","D":"Serum antiparasite IgG titers","E":"Stool culture on selective agar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Giardia infection is usually documented by immunofluorescent detection of cysts in stool samples. Oral antimicrobial therapy is effective, but recurrence is common. A modified acid-fast stain detects Cryptosporidium oocysts, not Giardia. Giardia trophozoites attach to the duodenal brush border, so colonic biopsy is not how giardiasis is documented. Giardia is a protozoan diagnosed by stool cyst immunofluorescence, not by bacterial culture on selective agar. Giardiasis is usually documented by immunofluorescent detection of cysts in stool samples, not by serology. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giardia trophozoites in duodenal biopsies are pear-shaped with two equally sized nuclei (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: infants, travelers, and immunocompromised patients have increased risk of severe giardiasis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A modified acid-fast stain detects Cryptosporidium oocysts, not Giardia.","B":"Giardia trophozoites attach to the duodenal brush border, so colonic biopsy is not how giardiasis is documented.","E":"Giardia is a protozoan diagnosed by stool cyst immunofluorescence, not by bacterial culture on selective agar.","D":"Giardiasis is usually documented by immunofluorescent detection of cysts in stool samples, not by serology."}},{"id":"sdl31_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug is the most effective treatment for systemic amebiasis?","choices":{"A":"Oral vancomycin","B":"Albendazole","C":"Praziquantel","D":"Nitazoxanide","E":"Metronidazole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica lacks mitochondria and Krebs cycle enzymes and depends on fermentation. Metronidazole inhibits pyruvate oxidoreductase, an enzyme required for fermentation, and is the most effective treatment for systemic disease. Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not amebiasis. Albendazole is an antihelminthic for worms, not a treatment for the protozoan Entamoeba. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not the protozoan Entamoeba histolytica. Nitazoxanide is used for Cryptosporidium and Giardia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. histolytica cysts have a chitin wall that resists gastric acid (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: amebiasis most often affects the cecum and ascending colon (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not amebiasis.","B":"Albendazole is an antihelminthic for worms, not a treatment for the protozoan Entamoeba.","C":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not the protozoan Entamoeba histolytica.","D":"Nitazoxanide is used for Cryptosporidium and Giardia."}},{"id":"sdl31_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"What property lets Cryptosporidium oocysts persist in treated but unfiltered water?","choices":{"A":"Chlorine resistance","B":"Heat resistance","C":"Chitin acid shield","D":"Surface antigen variation","E":"Bile acid tolerance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cryptosporidium oocysts are resistant to chlorine and persist in treated but unfiltered water; contaminated drinking water is the most common means of transmission. A chitin wall gives Entamoeba cysts acid resistance, but Cryptosporidium persists in water because its oocysts resist chlorine. Surface antigen variation is how Giardia evades immunity, not how Cryptosporidium survives water treatment. Heat resistance does not explain this. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diagnosis of cryptosporidiosis is based on finding oocysts in the stool (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: the 1993 Milwaukee outbreak from inadequate water purification affected more than 400,000 people (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A chitin wall gives Entamoeba cysts acid resistance, but Cryptosporidium persists in water because its oocysts resist chlorine.","D":"Surface antigen variation is how Giardia evades immunity, not how Cryptosporidium survives water treatment.","B":"Heat resistance does not explain this."}},{"id":"sdl31_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man develops ascending flaccid leg weakness and absent reflexes 2 weeks after diarrhea from undercooked chicken. What process most likely links the two illnesses?","choices":{"A":"Molecular mimicry of gangliosides","B":"Direct invasion of nerve roots","C":"Toxin block of acetylcholine release","D":"Immune complexes in muscle fibers","E":"HLA-B27 linked joint inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guillain-Barré syndrome after Campylobacter infection reflects molecular mimicry: antibodies to C. jejuni lipopolysaccharide cross-react with peripheral and central nervous system gangliosides. It is not HLA-linked. Campylobacter does not invade nerve roots; Guillain-Barré follows antibody cross-reaction with gangliosides. Toxin block of acetylcholine release is botulism, which causes descending paralysis, not post-Campylobacter ascending weakness. Immune complexes in muscle do not explain areflexic ascending weakness, which is a ganglioside-mimicry neuropathy. Robbins notes that Campylobacter reactive arthritis occurs mainly with HLA-B27. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: up to 40% of Guillain-Barré cases have documented Campylobacter infection in the prior 2 weeks (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Campylobacter infection is associated with postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Campylobacter does not invade nerve roots; Guillain-Barré follows antibody cross-reaction with gangliosides.","C":"Toxin block of acetylcholine release is botulism, which causes descending paralysis, not post-Campylobacter ascending weakness.","D":"Immune complexes in muscle do not explain areflexic ascending weakness, which is a ganglioside-mimicry neuropathy.","E":"Robbins notes that Campylobacter reactive arthritis occurs mainly with HLA-B27."}},{"id":"sdl31_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":5,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman returns from the Philippines with fever, abdominal pain, and small salmon-colored macules on her chest. Blood cultures grow gram-negative rods. What will her terminal ileum most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck","B":"Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions","C":"Transmural granulomas with fissures","D":"Raised Peyer patches with oval ulcers","E":"Flattened villi with lymphocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Typhoid fever (S. typhi) invades small intestinal M cells. Peyer patches in the terminal ileum enlarge into plateau-like elevations, and mucosal damage creates oval ulcers oriented along the ileal axis that may perforate. Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck are typical of Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not typhoid. Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions are C. difficile colitis, not typhoid ileitis. Transmural granulomas with fissures suggest Crohn disease, not acute typhoid fever. Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytosis is celiac disease, not Peyer patch hyperplasia from typhoid. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7) (Typhoid fever). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rose spots on the chest and abdomen are a feature of typhoid fever (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: gallbladder colonization with S. typhi may be associated with gallstones and a chronic carrier state (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck are typical of Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not typhoid.","B":"Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions are C. difficile colitis, not typhoid ileitis.","C":"Transmural granulomas with fissures suggest Crohn disease, not acute typhoid fever.","E":"Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytosis is celiac disease, not Peyer patch hyperplasia from typhoid."}},{"id":"sdl31_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy in daycare has fever, cramps, and bloody diarrhea, and stool culture grows Shigella sonnei. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Oral rehydration","B":"Culture-guided antibiotics","C":"Antimotility agents","D":"Acetaminophen","E":"Intravenous saline"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antidiarrheal medications can prolong symptoms and delay Shigella clearance, so they are contraindicated. Antibiotic treatment shortens both the clinical course and the duration of bacterial shedding. Oral rehydration is safe, standard supportive care for dysentery and should not be withheld. Culture-guided antibiotics shorten the course and shedding of Shigella, so they are appropriate. Intravenous saline is appropriate rehydration for a dehydrated child and is not contraindicated. Robbins states that antidiarrheal medications prolong symptoms and delay Shigella clearance and are therefore contraindicated, which is a warning about antimotility agents, not acetaminophen. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7) (Shigellosis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shigella infections are most prominent in the left colon, and M-cell tropism can produce aphthous ulcers resembling Crohn disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Shigella dysenteriae serotype 1 releases Shiga toxin, which inhibits protein synthesis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — antidiarrheal drug classes and their adverse effects","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral rehydration is safe, standard supportive care for dysentery and should not be withheld.","B":"Culture-guided antibiotics shorten the course and shedding of Shigella, so they are appropriate.","E":"Intravenous saline is appropriate rehydration for a dehydrated child and is not contraindicated.","D":"Robbins states that antidiarrheal medications prolong symptoms and delay Shigella clearance and are therefore contraindicated, which is a warning about antimotility agents, not acetaminophen."}},{"id":"sdl31_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has bloody diarrhea after a cookout with undercooked hamburgers. A week later she is pale, platelets are 40,000/µL (reference range 150,000–450,000/µL), and creatinine is rising. Which bacterial product most directly causes these complications?","choices":{"A":"Heat-labile toxin","B":"Heat-stable toxin","C":"Dispersin protein","D":"Shiga-like toxin","E":"Lipopolysaccharide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"EHEC O157:H7 from undercooked ground beef produces Shiga-like toxins and is more likely than non-O157 serotypes to cause bloody diarrhea, hemolytic uremic syndrome, and ischemic colitis. Heat-labile toxin is an ETEC product that raises cAMP and causes watery diarrhea. Heat-stable toxin is an ETEC product that raises cGMP and causes watery diarrhea. Dispersin is an enteroaggregative E. coli surface protein that aids adherence. Lipopolysaccharide is not that toxin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EHEC lesions show an ischemia-like morphology with surface atrophy and erosion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: antibiotic treatment can release Shiga-like toxins and raise hemolytic uremic syndrome risk, especially in children (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Heat-labile toxin is an ETEC product that raises cAMP and causes watery diarrhea, not HUS.","B":"Heat-stable toxin is an ETEC product that raises cGMP and causes watery diarrhea, not HUS.","C":"Dispersin is an enteroaggregative E. coli surface protein that aids adherence.","E":"Lipopolysaccharide is not the toxin that produces this syndrome."}},{"id":"sdl31_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-month-old boy in a crowded urban daycare has 5 days of watery diarrhea. Research biopsy shows bacteria tightly attached to enterocyte apical membranes with local effacement of microvilli. Which group is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","D":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteropathogenic E. coli (EPEC) produce attaching and effacing lesions: bacteria attach tightly to small intestinal enterocyte apical membranes and cause local effacement of microvilli. EPEC are a major cause of diarrhea in children under 2 years. Enterotoxigenic E. coli acts through toxins without attaching and effacing microvilli. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli can form A/E lesions but affects the colon with bloody diarrhea, not small-intestinal watery diarrhea in a nursery. Enteroinvasive E. coli invades colonic epithelium and causes dysentery rather than attaching and effacing. Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilm in the colon rather than effacing small-intestinal microvilli. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: assays for the bacterial proteins that insert into enterocyte membranes allow molecular diagnosis of EPEC (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: EPEC are prevalent worldwide and cause endemic diarrhea and outbreaks (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli acts through toxins without attaching and effacing microvilli.","C":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli can form A/E lesions but affects the colon with bloody diarrhea, not small-intestinal watery diarrhea in a nursery.","D":"Enteroinvasive E. coli invades colonic epithelium and causes dysentery rather than attaching and effacing.","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilm in the colon rather than effacing small-intestinal microvilli."}},{"id":"sdl31_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man develops watery diarrhea 3 days after arriving in Mexico. Stool testing identifies an E. coli strain producing heat-labile and heat-stable toxins. What would a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses","B":"Ischemic surface erosion","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers","D":"Foamy lamina macrophages","E":"Minimal mucosal changes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ETEC are noninvasive and act through toxins that raise cAMP and cGMP. Like cholera, ETEC infection results in mild histologic changes. Neutrophilic crypt abscesses imply invasive colitis, but ETEC is noninvasive and leaves little histologic change. Ischemic surface erosion fits EHEC Shiga-like toxin injury, not toxin-mediated ETEC secretory diarrhea. Flask-shaped ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not ETEC. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not ETEC. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ETEC heat-labile toxin activates adenylate cyclase, while the heat-stable toxin raises cGMP (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: ETEC spread through contaminated food or water (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses imply invasive colitis, but ETEC is noninvasive and leaves little histologic change.","B":"Ischemic surface erosion fits EHEC Shiga-like toxin injury, not toxin-mediated ETEC secretory diarrhea.","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not ETEC.","D":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not ETEC."}},{"id":"sdl31_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man who farms in rural Wisconsin has diarrhea, weight loss, joint pain, and memory problems. Duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria packed with distended foamy macrophages. What will special staining most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Acid-fast bacilli in macrophages","B":"PAS-positive lysosomal granules","C":"Congo red apple-green deposits","D":"Prussian blue iron granules","E":"Mucicarmine-staining capsules"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The macrophages of Whipple disease contain PAS-positive, diastase-resistant granules representing partially digested bacteria within lysosomes. T. whipplei is acid-fast negative, unlike mycobacteria. Congo red apple-green birefringence identifies amyloid, not the bacteria-laden macrophages of Whipple disease. Prussian blue stains iron, such as in hemochromatosis, not T. whipplei within macrophages. Mucicarmine stains capsules such as Cryptococcus, whereas Whipple macrophages are PAS-positive. Robbins notes mycobacteria stain positively with the acid-fast stain while T. whipplei does not, which is how the stain separates the two foamy macrophage infiltrates. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac symptoms, arthralgias, and neurologic symptoms are common in Whipple disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: the usual triad is diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Congo red apple-green birefringence identifies amyloid, not the bacteria-laden macrophages of Whipple disease.","D":"Prussian blue stains iron, such as in hemochromatosis, not T. whipplei within macrophages.","E":"Mucicarmine stains capsules such as Cryptococcus, whereas Whipple macrophages are PAS-positive.","A":"Robbins notes mycobacteria stain positively with the acid-fast stain while T. whipplei does not, which is how the stain separates the two foamy macrophage infiltrates."}},{"id":"sdl31_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man working on a dairy farm has steatorrhea, mesenteric lymphadenopathy, and arthralgias. Duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria filled with foamy macrophages, and tissue PCR detects Tropheryma whipplei. What best explains his malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Impaired lymph drainage","B":"Brush-border enzyme loss","C":"Pancreatic enzyme deficiency","D":"Bile salt deconjugation","E":"Toxin-driven chloride secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In Whipple disease, bacteria-laden macrophages accumulate in the small intestinal lamina propria and mesenteric lymph nodes and obstruct lymphatics. The malabsorptive diarrhea results from impaired lymph drainage. Brush-border enzyme loss fits celiac disease or viral villous damage, but Whipple malabsorption comes from lymphatic obstruction. Pancreatic enzyme deficiency causes maldigestion in pancreatic disease, not in Whipple disease. Bile salt deconjugation results from bacterial overgrowth, not from macrophage-mediated lymphatic obstruction. Toxin-driven chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera or ETEC, not Whipple disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea and steatorrhea occur later in the course of Whipple disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: the rarity of infection despite many healthy carriers suggests genetic risk factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Brush-border enzyme loss fits celiac disease or viral villous damage, but Whipple malabsorption comes from lymphatic obstruction.","C":"Pancreatic enzyme deficiency causes maldigestion in pancreatic disease, not in Whipple disease.","D":"Bile salt deconjugation results from bacterial overgrowth, not from macrophage-mediated lymphatic obstruction.","E":"Toxin-driven chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera or ETEC, not Whipple disease."}},{"id":"sdl31_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has had migratory joint pain for 2 years and now has diarrhea, weight loss, and a new heart murmur. Biopsy shows foamy macrophages expanding duodenal villi. What will endoscopy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Gray adherent pseudomembranes","B":"Cobblestoning with skip lesions","C":"Yellow-white mucosal plaques","D":"Flask-shaped undermined ulcers","E":"Scalloped duodenal folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dense macrophage infiltrates expand villi and give a shaggy surface. Lymphatic dilation and mucosal lipid deposition produce endoscopically visible white to yellow plaques. Macrophages can also accumulate in cardiac valves, synovium, and brain. Gray adherent pseudomembranes are C. difficile colitis, not Whipple disease. Cobblestoning with skip lesions is Crohn disease, not Whipple disease. Flask-shaped undermined ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica, not Whipple disease. Scalloped duodenal folds suggest celiac disease, whereas Whipple disease shows yellow-white plaques. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the shaggy mucosal surface reflects villous expansion by dense macrophage infiltrates (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: extraintestinal symptoms such as arthritis may precede malabsorption by months or years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gray adherent pseudomembranes are C. difficile colitis, not Whipple disease.","B":"Cobblestoning with skip lesions is Crohn disease, not Whipple disease.","D":"Flask-shaped undermined ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica, not Whipple disease.","E":"Scalloped duodenal folds suggest celiac disease, whereas Whipple disease shows yellow-white plaques."}},{"id":"sdl31_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman on a cruise ship develops vomiting and watery diarrhea during a shipwide outbreak and recovers in 2 days. Her 70-year-old husband, equally exposed, never becomes ill, and his saliva lacks blood group antigens. Which trait most likely protects him?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27 positive genotype","B":"Heterozygous CFTR mutation","C":"Sickle hemoglobin trait carrier","D":"HLA-DQ2 haplotype expression","E":"Homozygous FUT2 inactivation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mutations inactivating FUT2 prevent fucosylation of blood group antigens that serve as norovirus binding sites. About 20% of people of European and African descent carry this genotype. HLA-B27 predisposes to reactive arthritis after Shigella and other enteric infections, not norovirus resistance. Sickle cell trait is linked to Salmonella osteomyelitis risk, not norovirus resistance. HLA-DQ2 is the celiac disease susceptibility haplotype, not a norovirus resistance factor. Natural resistance to norovirus comes from homozygous FUT2 inactivation (nonsecretor status), which removes the blood group antigens the virus binds; CFTR heterozygosity does not do this. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Norovirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norovirus outbreaks spread in schools, hospitals, nursing homes, and cruise ships through droplets, surfaces, and fomites (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: an infective dose of fewer than 20 viral particles makes prevention difficult (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — small-intestine epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"HLA-B27 predisposes to reactive arthritis after Shigella and other enteric infections, not norovirus resistance.","C":"Sickle cell trait is linked to Salmonella osteomyelitis risk, not norovirus resistance.","D":"HLA-DQ2 is the celiac disease susceptibility haplotype, not a norovirus resistance factor.","B":"Natural resistance to norovirus comes from homozygous FUT2 inactivation (nonsecretor status), which removes the blood group antigens the virus binds; CFTR heterozygosity does not do this."}},{"id":"sdl31_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":5,"stem":"A 14-month-old unvaccinated boy has vomiting and watery diarrhea during a daycare outbreak. The virus has a segmented, double-stranded RNA genome. Which viral factor may mediate the enterocyte damage?","choices":{"A":"Hemagglutinin-neuraminidase","B":"Nonstructural protein 4","C":"Reverse transcriptase","D":"Spike glycoprotein","E":"Envelope fusion protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rotavirus selectively infects and destroys mature small intestinal enterocytes; damage may be mediated by nonstructural protein 4 (NSP4), which can induce apoptosis. Immature cells then repopulate the villus surface. Hemagglutinin and neuraminidase are envelope antigens of influenza and parainfluenza viruses; rotavirus is a nonenveloped reovirus. Reverse transcriptase belongs to retroviruses and hepadnavirus (HBV), not to rotavirus, a double-stranded RNA reovirus. Rotavirus is a nonenveloped reovirus, so it has no envelope fusion protein. Robbins attributes rotavirus enterocyte damage to nonstructural protein 4 (NSP4), which can induce apoptosis, not to a spike glycoprotein. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Rotavirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the minimal infective inoculum of rotavirus is about 10 viral particles (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: rotavirus vaccines are incompletely effective in under-resourced regions, possibly due to malnutrition (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Hemagglutinin and neuraminidase are envelope antigens of influenza and parainfluenza viruses; rotavirus is a nonenveloped reovirus.","C":"Reverse transcriptase belongs to retroviruses and hepadnavirus (HBV), not to rotavirus, a double-stranded RNA reovirus.","E":"Rotavirus is a nonenveloped reovirus, so it has no envelope fusion protein.","D":"Robbins attributes rotavirus enterocyte damage to nonstructural protein 4 (NSP4), which can induce apoptosis, not to a spike glycoprotein."}},{"id":"sdl31_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man on immunosuppression after a heart transplant has 6 days of diarrhea, vomiting, and fever. Stool PCR detects adenovirus. What would a small intestinal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Prominent intranuclear viral inclusions","B":"Foamy macrophages in lamina propria","C":"Villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia","D":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites","E":"Transmural granulomas with fissures"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adenovirus biopsies can show epithelial degeneration but more often show nonspecific villous atrophy and compensatory crypt hyperplasia. Viral nuclear inclusions are uncommon. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not adenovirus enteritis. Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites are amebiasis, not adenovirus. Transmural granulomas with fissures are Crohn disease, not adenovirus. Robbins states that adenovirus small intestinal biopsies more often show nonspecific villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia and that viral nuclear inclusions are uncommon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Adenovirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenovirus is a common cause of pediatric diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: adenovirus symptoms generally resolve within 10 days (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — villi and microvilli as absorptive surface area","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not adenovirus enteritis.","D":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites are amebiasis, not adenovirus.","E":"Transmural granulomas with fissures are Crohn disease, not adenovirus.","A":"Robbins states that adenovirus small intestinal biopsies more often show nonspecific villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia and that viral nuclear inclusions are uncommon."}},{"id":"sdl31_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man from Mexico had bloody diarrhea a month ago and now has fever and right upper quadrant pain. CT shows a 9-cm liver abscess, and colon biopsy showed organisms ingesting red cells. How did the parasite most likely reach the liver?","choices":{"A":"Ascent through bile ducts","B":"Lymphatic spread to liver","C":"Direct transmural extension","D":"Portal venous embolization","E":"Hepatic artery seeding"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica invades crypts and burrows into the lamina propria, forming flask-shaped ulcers. Parasites penetrate splanchnic vessels and embolize to the liver, producing abscesses in about 40% of patients with amebic dysentery. Amebae do not ascend the bile ducts; they invade colonic splanchnic vessels and embolize through the portal vein. Entamoeba reaches the liver through the portal venous system, not lymphatics. Direct transmural extension from the colon does not explain a liver abscess, which arises from portal embolization. Colonic venous blood drains to the liver through the portal vein, not the hepatic artery. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Entamoeba histolytica). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebic liver abscesses can exceed 10 cm and have a shaggy fibrinous lining with scant marginal inflammation (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: metronidazole is the most effective treatment for systemic amebiasis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Amebae do not ascend the bile ducts; they invade colonic splanchnic vessels and embolize through the portal vein.","B":"Entamoeba reaches the liver through the portal venous system, not lymphatics.","C":"Direct transmural extension from the colon does not explain a liver abscess, which arises from portal embolization.","E":"Colonic venous blood drains to the liver through the portal vein, not the hepatic artery."}},{"id":"sdl31_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":5,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who drank stream water while camping has had intermittent bloating and greasy diarrhea for 4 months. Duodenal biopsy shows pear-shaped organisms with two nuclei on the brush border. Which parasite feature best explains her months of symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Invasion into the lamina propria","B":"Chitin wall resisting acid","C":"Intracellular vacuole residence","D":"Red cell ingestion by trophozoites","E":"Variant surface protein switching"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Giardia evade immune clearance through continuous modification of their major surface antigen, variant surface protein, and can persist for months or years with intermittent symptoms. They do not invade. Giardia does not invade the lamina propria; it stays attached to the brush border. A chitin acid-resistant wall describes Entamoeba cysts and does not explain prolonged Giardia infection. Intracellular vacuole residence describes Cryptosporidium, not Giardia. Red cell ingestion by trophozoites is diagnostic of Entamoeba histolytica, not Giardia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Giardia lamblia). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giardia decrease brush-border enzyme expression and cause microvillous damage and epithelial apoptosis (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Giardia cysts resist chlorine, so infection is endemic in unfiltered public water supplies (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Giardia does not invade the lamina propria; it stays attached to the brush border.","B":"A chitin acid-resistant wall describes Entamoeba cysts and does not explain prolonged Giardia infection.","C":"Intracellular vacuole residence describes Cryptosporidium, not Giardia.","D":"Red cell ingestion by trophozoites is diagnostic of Entamoeba histolytica, not Giardia."}},{"id":"sdl31_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":5,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with advanced HIV has weeks of profuse watery diarrhea. Ileal biopsy shows small blue spheres that appear to sit on the brush border, and stool shows oocysts. Where does the organism actually reside?","choices":{"A":"Free in lumen over villi","B":"Vacuole within enterocytes","C":"Inside lamina propria macrophages","D":"Within submucosal lymphatics","E":"Inside flask-shaped ulcers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cryptosporidium sporozoites attach to the brush border and induce enterocytes to engulf them into an endocytic vacuole, so the intracellular parasite appears to sit on the apical membrane. Sodium malabsorption, chloride secretion, and tight junction leak cause watery diarrhea. Cryptosporidium is not free in the lumen. Organisms inside lamina propria macrophages describe Whipple disease or MAC, not Cryptosporidium. Flask-shaped ulcers belong to Entamoeba histolytica, not Cryptosporidium. Robbins explains that Cryptosporidium organisms only appear to sit on the brush border and are actually enveloped by a thin layer of host cell cytoplasm, not within submucosal lymphatics. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Cryptosporidium). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cryptosporidium causes sodium malabsorption, chloride secretion, and increased tight junction permeability, producing watery diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: in immunodeficient hosts, organisms can involve the biliary and respiratory tracts (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Cryptosporidium is not free in the lumen.","C":"Organisms inside lamina propria macrophages describe Whipple disease or MAC, not Cryptosporidium.","E":"Flask-shaped ulcers belong to Entamoeba histolytica, not Cryptosporidium.","D":"Robbins explains that Cryptosporidium organisms only appear to sit on the brush border and are actually enveloped by a thin layer of host cell cytoplasm, not within submucosal lymphatics."}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy in Germany has 2 days of fever and right lower quadrant pain after eating undercooked pork. At laparoscopy the appendix is mildly inflamed, and the mesenteric lymph nodes and terminal ileal lymphoid tissue are enlarged. Culture on selective agar grows a gram-negative rod. Which coexisting condition would most increase his risk of sepsis from this organism?","choices":{"A":"Iron-deficiency anemia","B":"Hemochromatosis","C":"Lactase nonpersistence","D":"Vitamin B12 deficiency","E":"Primary hypothyroidism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pork, northern European setting, and pseudoappendicitis with mesenteric adenitis point to Yersinia enterocolitica. A pathogenicity island gives Yersinia iron capture systems, and iron enhances its virulence and dissemination, so people with increased nonheme iron, as in hemochromatosis, are at greater risk of sepsis. Iron deficiency lowers available iron, which would not enhance this organism's virulence. Lactase nonpersistence causes osmotic symptoms with milk and does not affect bacterial invasion. Vitamin B12 deficiency causes anemia and neuropathy but is not a risk factor for this sepsis. Hypothyroidism slows motility but is not linked to dissemination of this organism. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Yersinia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Yersinia invades M cells, localizes to the ileum, appendix, and right colon, and is more virulent in iron overload (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Yersinia mesenteric adenitis can mimic appendicitis in teenagers and young adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Iron deficiency lowers available iron, which would not","C":"Lactase nonpersistence causes osmotic symptoms with milk and","D":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causes anemia and neuropathy but","E":"Hypothyroidism slows motility but is not linked to"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy in a rural, under-resourced community has fever, cramps, and small-volume bloody stools with mucus. Stool culture grows E. coli that lacks Shiga-like toxin, heat-labile toxin, and heat-stable toxin genes. Colon biopsy shows a nonspecific acute self-limited colitis. Which pathogenic feature does this strain share with Shigella?","choices":{"A":"Effacement of small bowel microvilli","B":"Toxin-driven cAMP rise in enterocytes","C":"Aggregative fimbriae aided by dispersin","D":"Invasion of colonic epithelial cells","E":"Shiga toxin injury to glomerular vessels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A toxin-negative E. coli causing bloody colitis in a young child in an under-resourced setting is enteroinvasive E. coli. EIEC is bacteriologically similar to Shigella and, without producing toxins, invades epithelial cells to cause acute self-limited colitis. Effacement of microvilli in attaching and effacing lesions defines enteropathogenic E. coli. A toxin-driven rise in cAMP is the mechanism of the heat-labile toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli. Adherence fimbriae aided by dispersin define enteroaggregative E. coli. Shiga-like toxin genes are absent from this strain, so it cannot cause toxin-mediated injury. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EIEC resembles Shigella and invades colonic epithelium without toxins, whereas ETEC, EPEC, EHEC, and EAEC act through toxins or adherence (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Bloody diarrhea in a child prompts testing for Shiga toxin before choosing therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Effacement of microvilli in attaching and effacing lesions","B":"A toxin-driven rise in cAMP is the mechanism","C":"Adherence fimbriae aided by dispersin define enteroaggregative E.","E":"Shiga-like toxin genes are absent from this strain,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgias, and duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria packed with distended foamy macrophages. Besides the gut and lymph nodes, where else can these macrophages accumulate?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle","B":"Thyroid gland","C":"Cardiac valves","D":"Renal glomeruli","E":"Adrenal cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgias with foamy macrophages filling the lamina propria indicate Whipple disease. Robbins describes accumulation of bacteria-laden macrophages in mesenteric lymph nodes, synovial membranes, cardiac valves, brain, and other sites. Skeletal muscle is not among the sites Robbins lists for these macrophages. The thyroid gland is not a described site of macrophage accumulation in Whipple disease. Renal glomeruli are not among the extraintestinal sites, which center on joints, heart, and brain. The adrenal cortex is not a recognized site of these macrophages. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease fills the lamina propria with PAS-positive foamy macrophages and can involve joints, heart valves, and brain (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Cardiac, joint, and neurologic symptoms in a patient with malabsorption should prompt consideration of Whipple disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Skeletal muscle is not among the sites","B":"The thyroid gland is not a described","D":"Renal glomeruli are not among the extraintestinal","E":"The adrenal cortex is not a recognized"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-month-old girl develops vomiting and watery diarrhea from rotavirus, but her exclusively breastfed 3-month-old brother stays well. What most likely protects the infant?","choices":{"A":"Immature villus receptors","B":"Higher gastric acidity","C":"Lower infective inoculum","D":"Faster enterocyte turnover","E":"Antibodies in breast milk"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins notes that children between 6 and 24 months are most vulnerable to rotavirus, probably because antibodies in breast milk protect during the first 6 months of life. The breastfed 3-month-old shares the household exposure but receives this passive protection. Robbins attributes early protection to breast milk antibodies, not to immature receptors or faster enterocyte turnover. Higher gastric acidity is not the protective factor Robbins describes for young infants. The minimal infective inoculum of rotavirus is only about 10 particles, so household exposure easily exceeds it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Rotavirus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rotavirus mainly affects children 6 to 24 months old, probably because breast milk antibodies protect younger infants, and its infective inoculum is very small (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Breastfeeding and rotavirus vaccination both reduce severe rotavirus disease in infants.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins attributes early protection to breast milk antibodies,","B":"Higher gastric acidity is not the protective factor","C":"The minimal infective inoculum of rotavirus is only","D":"Robbins attributes early protection to breast milk antibodies,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman who recently moved from Colombia has 3 weeks of bloody diarrhea and lower abdominal pain. Colon biopsy shows flask-shaped ulcers containing trophozoites with ingested red blood cells. Which mechanism of action characterizes the first-line drug for her invasive disease?","choices":{"A":"Binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit","B":"Inhibition of DNA gyrase supercoiling","C":"Blockade of cell wall peptidoglycan","D":"Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis","E":"Toxic free radicals damaging DNA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites that have eaten red cells identify Entamoeba histolytica, and invasive amebiasis is treated with metronidazole. Metronidazole forms toxic free radical metabolites inside the organism that damage DNA, and paromomycin is added for asymptomatic cyst passers. Binding to the 50S subunit describes macrolides and clindamycin, which are not first-line for amebiasis. Inhibition of DNA gyrase is the action of fluoroquinolones. Blockade of peptidoglycan synthesis is the action of β-lactams, and amebae have no bacterial cell wall. Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis is the action of azole antifungals. Sources: First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Entamoeba histolytica; Metronidazole).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Entamoeba histolytica causes flask-shaped ulcers and liver abscesses and is treated with metronidazole, with paromomycin for cyst passers (First Aid 2025 Microbiology). COMLEX: Amebic colitis should be considered in bloody diarrhea after residence in endemic regions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Binding to the 50S subunit describes macrolides and","B":"Inhibition of DNA gyrase is the action of","C":"Blockade of peptidoglycan synthesis is the action of","D":"Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis is the action of"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"Eight people develop fever, cramps, and diarrhea a day after a potluck where egg salad sat unrefrigerated for hours. A 60-year-old woman who takes omeprazole daily for reflux ate a single bite yet became the most ill. Stool cultures grow a motile, H₂S-producing, non-lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod. Which factor best explains her illness after such a small exposure?","choices":{"A":"A preformed toxin ingested with the food","B":"Omeprazole-induced loss of mucosal IgA","C":"Low acid lowered the needed inoculum","D":"Raised gastric pH activating pepsinogen","E":"Omeprazole-induced loss of Th1 immunity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eggs left unrefrigerated and a motile, H₂S-producing gram-negative rod point to nontyphoidal Salmonella. Very few organisms are needed to cause infection, and the absence of gastric acid, as with acid-suppressive therapy, reduces the required inoculum further. Preformed toxin illness causes rapid vomiting without fever and would not grow an invasive rod in stool. Omeprazole lowers gastric acid and is not described as suppressing mucosal IgA. Pepsinogen is activated by low pH, so a raised pH would reduce pepsin activity. Defects in Th1 or Th17 immunity raise the risk of disseminated disease, but omeprazole does not cause them. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Salmonella); First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Salmonella).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nontyphoidal Salmonella needs a small inoculum, which is lowered further by achlorhydria or acid suppression, and antibiotics are generally avoided because they prolong carriage (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Patients on long-term proton pump inhibitors are more susceptible to enteric infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Preformed toxin illness causes rapid vomiting without fever","B":"Omeprazole lowers gastric acid and is not described","D":"Pepsinogen is activated by low pH, so a","E":"Defects in Th1 or Th17 immunity raise the"}},{"id":"grid_sdl31_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":4,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has 3 days of crampy abdominal pain and frequent stools that began 2 days after she ate a hamburger at a county fair. Temperature is 37.2°C (99.0°F). Five days later she is pale and has petechiae and decreased urine output; a peripheral smear shows schistocytes, and the platelet count is 42,000/mm³ (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm³). Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the organism causing this patient's illness?","grid":{"headers":["Bloody diarrhea","Epithelial invasion","Main site"]},"choices":{"A":["Absent","Present","Small bowel"],"B":["Present","Absent","Colon"],"C":["Absent","Absent","Small bowel"],"D":["Present","Present","Small bowel"],"E":["Absent","Absent","Colon"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (bloody diarrhea present, epithelial invasion absent, colon) is correct. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli O157:H7 comes from cattle and is spread by undercooked ground beef; the notes list severe bloody diarrhea, localization to the colon, and hemolytic uremic syndrome as its key complication. It injures the colon through Shiga-like toxin rather than by invading the epithelium. Choice A (bloody diarrhea absent, epithelial invasion present, small bowel) is Yersinia enterocolitica, which invades the ileum and its lymphoid tissue and causes fever, pain, and diarrhea with mesenteric adenitis that can mimic appendicitis. Choice C (bloody diarrhea absent, epithelial invasion absent, small bowel) is Vibrio cholerae, which stays in the small-intestinal lumen and causes rice-water stool. Choice D (bloody diarrhea present, epithelial invasion present, small bowel) is Salmonella typhi, which invades Peyer patches in the terminal ileum and causes enteric fever with rose spots. Choice E (bloody diarrhea absent, epithelial invasion absent, colon) is Clostridioides difficile, which causes toxin-mediated watery diarrhea after antibiotics. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 31, sections 1 and 2: gastroenteritis-causing bacteria and E. coli subgroups); Robbins Ch. 17 (Table 17.7, infectious enterocolitis). The absent invasion for EHEC and C. difficile and the invasive, usually nonbloody ileal course of Yersinia are from Robbins, since the notes do not grade them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EHEC O157:H7 releases Shiga-like toxin, does not ferment sorbitol, and causes HUS; antibiotics can increase toxin release (Exam 3 notes, SDL 31). COMLEX: bloody diarrhea after undercooked beef followed by anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal failure is HUS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — bloody versus watery bacterial diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q31","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which E. coli group forms a biofilm and causes prolonged nonbloody diarrhea, especially in immunodeficient people?","choices":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","B":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","C":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","D":"Enteroaggregative E. coli","E":"The Shiga-toxin strain that causes hemolytic uremic syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Enteroaggregative E. coli forms a biofilm and causes nonbloody watery diarrhea that can become chronic, especially in immunodeficient hosts. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome, not a nonbloody biofilm illness. Enteroinvasive E. coli invades the colon and causes dysentery with fever. Enteropathogenic E. coli makes attaching-and-effacing lesions in the small intestine of infants, not this biofilm picture. The Shiga-toxin strain is enterohemorrhagic E. coli, which causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome, not a nonbloody biofilm illness","B":"Enteroinvasive E. coli invades the colon and causes dysentery with fever","C":"Enteropathogenic E. coli makes attaching-and-effacing lesions in the small intestine of infants, not this biofilm picture","E":"The Shiga-toxin strain is enterohemorrhagic E. coli, which causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome"}},{"id":"sdl31_q32","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli produces its watery diarrhea in which part of the bowel?","choices":{"A":"Stomach","B":"Left colon only","C":"Rectum only","D":"Appendix","E":"Small intestine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli acts in the small intestine, where its toxins drive watery diarrhea. The stomach is not the segment where enterotoxigenic E. coli causes traveler's diarrhea. Left-colon invasion is the pattern of Shigella and enteroinvasive E. coli, not enterotoxigenic E. coli. The rectum is not the site of enterotoxigenic E. coli toxin action. The appendix is not where enterotoxigenic E. coli localizes.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The stomach is not the segment where enterotoxigenic E. coli causes traveler's diarrhea","B":"Left-colon invasion is the pattern of Shigella and enteroinvasive E. coli, not enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"The rectum is not the site of enterotoxigenic E. coli toxin action","D":"The appendix is not where enterotoxigenic E. coli localizes"}},{"id":"sdl31_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which set of extraintestinal problems is grouped with Whipple disease?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac, joint, and neurologic disease","B":"Only a lung cavity with acid-fast bacilli","C":"Flask-shaped colonic ulcers and a liver abscess","D":"Migrating skin tracks after beach exposure","E":"Bloody diarrhea limited to the left colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Whipple disease is remembered for cardiac involvement, arthralgias, and neurologic symptoms, along with malabsorptive diarrhea and weight loss. Acid-fast bacilli in a lung cavity point to mycobacteria, and Whipple macrophages are not acid-fast. Flask-shaped ulcers and a liver abscess are the invasive ameba pattern. Migrating skin tracks are cutaneous larva migrans, not Whipple disease. Bloody left-colon diarrhea is dysentery, not the Whipple pattern.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Acid-fast bacilli in a lung cavity point to mycobacteria, and Whipple macrophages are not acid-fast","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers and a liver abscess are the invasive ameba pattern","D":"Migrating skin tracks are cutaneous larva migrans, not Whipple disease","E":"Bloody left-colon diarrhea is dysentery, not the Whipple pattern"}},{"id":"sdl31_q34","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man develops watery diarrhea, cramps, and fever on day 6 of broad-spectrum antibiotics in the hospital. He is tender over the colon without peritonitis. The leukocyte count is 18,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL) and albumin is 2.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Stool is not grossly bloody. Which complication is this overgrowth particularly known for?","choices":{"A":"Toxic megacolon","B":"Guillain-Barré syndrome","C":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome from undercooked beef","D":"A liver abscess full of anchovy-paste material","E":"Rose spots and ileal perforation from a human-only bloodstream infection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Clostridioides difficile overgrowth after antibiotics can progress to toxic megacolon, perforation, and a high chance of recurrence. Guillain-Barré syndrome is the post-infectious complication of Campylobacter, not this antibiotic-associated colitis. Hemolytic uremic syndrome after undercooked beef is the enterohemorrhagic E. coli pattern. An anchovy-paste liver abscess follows invasive amebiasis. Rose spots and ileal perforation are typhoid fever, which is not triggered by a few days of broad-spectrum antibiotics.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Guillain-Barré syndrome is the post-infectious complication of Campylobacter, not this antibiotic-associated colitis","C":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome after undercooked beef is the enterohemorrhagic E. coli pattern","D":"An anchovy-paste liver abscess follows invasive amebiasis","E":"Rose spots and ileal perforation are typhoid fever, which is not triggered by a few days of broad-spectrum antibiotics"}},{"id":"sdl31_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old nursing student develops vomiting and watery diarrhea one day after a single sick classmate used the same bathroom. Dozens of students are ill within 48 hours, including some who never shared a meal. She recovers in two days. Sodium is 137 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and she stays afebrile after the first day. Which property best explains an outbreak this explosive?","choices":{"A":"A segmented double-stranded RNA genome that infects only mature enterocytes","B":"An extremely small infective dose with spread on surfaces and fomites","C":"Acid-fast oocysts that must be swallowed in thousands","D":"Invasion of Peyer patches with rose spots","E":"A toxin that is made only in undercooked beef"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Norovirus spreads by the fecal-oral route, droplets, and fomites, and fewer than 20 particles are enough to infect. A segmented double-stranded RNA genome that hits mature enterocytes describes rotavirus, not this fomite outbreak. Acid-fast oocysts are Cryptosporidium, and this illness did not require a huge swallowed dose. Peyer-patch invasion with rose spots is typhoid fever. A beef-associated toxin is enterohemorrhagic E. coli, which causes bloody diarrhea rather than a two-day vomiting outbreak.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A segmented double-stranded RNA genome that hits mature enterocytes describes rotavirus, not this fomite outbreak","C":"Acid-fast oocysts are Cryptosporidium, and this illness did not require a huge swallowed dose","D":"Peyer-patch invasion with rose spots is typhoid fever","E":"A beef-associated toxin is enterohemorrhagic E. coli, which causes bloody diarrhea rather than a two-day vomiting outbreak"}},{"id":"sdl31_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old immunocompetent girl has five days of watery diarrhea after swimming in a community pool. She is drinking and has no blood in the stool. Temperature is 37.4°C and the abdomen is soft. Sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and creatinine is 0.5 mg/dL (reference range: 0.3–0.7 mg/dL). Stool shows acid-fast oocysts. Which drug is appropriate for this host?","choices":{"A":"Albendazole","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Nitazoxanide","D":"Praziquantel","E":"A luminal agent used only after metronidazole for amebic abscess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In an immunocompetent host, nitazoxanide is the treatment for cryptosporidiosis, along with fluid replacement. Albendazole is a benzimidazole used for intestinal nematodes and tissue cestodes, not this coccidian. Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Onchocerca. Praziquantel paralyzes trematodes and cestodes and is not the treatment for these acid-fast oocysts. A luminal amebicide after metronidazole is the sequence for invasive amebiasis, not for cryptosporidiosis.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Albendazole is a benzimidazole used for intestinal nematodes and tissue cestodes, not this coccidian","B":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Onchocerca","D":"Praziquantel paralyzes trematodes and cestodes and is not the treatment for these acid-fast oocysts","E":"A luminal amebicide after metronidazole is the sequence for invasive amebiasis, not for cryptosporidiosis"}},{"id":"sdl31_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has had greasy stools and bloating for two months after drinking from a stream. He has no fever and no blood in the stool. The abdomen is soft. Sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Duodenal biopsy shows pear-shaped, binucleate organisms on the brush border. Which drug treats this infection?","choices":{"A":"Tinidazole","B":"Albendazole as a microtubule inhibitor for roundworms","C":"Praziquantel","D":"Ivermectin","E":"Pyrantel pamoate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Giardiasis is treated with metronidazole, tinidazole, or nitazoxanide. Albendazole's microtubule block is aimed at nematodes, not this flagellate. Praziquantel is for tapeworms and flukes. Ivermectin paralyzes nematodes and is not the treatment for this brush-border flagellate. Pyrantel pamoate is an over-the-counter option for pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Albendazole's microtubule block is aimed at nematodes, not this flagellate","C":"Praziquantel is for tapeworms and flukes","D":"Ivermectin paralyzes nematodes and is not the treatment for this brush-border flagellate","E":"Pyrantel pamoate is an over-the-counter option for pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm"}}],"32":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"What do endoscopic and microscopic evaluations show in irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Normal mucosal findings","B":"Patchy crypt abscesses","C":"Thickened collagen band","D":"Diffuse villous blunting","E":"Scattered aphthous ulcers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Despite real symptoms, endoscopic and microscopic evaluations are normal in IBS, so the diagnosis rests on clinical symptoms and functional testing. Patchy crypt abscesses indicate inflammatory bowel disease, which must be excluded because IBS biopsies are normal. A thickened subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, not IBS, whose mucosa is normal. Diffuse villous blunting suggests celiac disease, an organic cause excluded before diagnosing IBS. Scattered aphthous ulcers point to Crohn disease, whereas IBS shows no mucosal abnormality. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS has no structural abnormalities and is most common in middle-aged females (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: IBS is divided into diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, and mixed subtypes (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Patchy crypt abscesses indicate inflammatory bowel disease, which must be excluded because IBS biopsies are normal.","C":"A thickened subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, not IBS, whose mucosa is normal.","D":"Diffuse villous blunting suggests celiac disease, an organic cause excluded before diagnosing IBS.","E":"Scattered aphthous ulcers point to Crohn disease, whereas IBS shows no mucosal abnormality."}},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":5,"stem":"Which pair of conditions is associated with irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Sacroiliitis and uveitis","B":"Gallstones and kidney stones","C":"Erythema nodosum and arthritis","D":"Fibromyalgia and depression","E":"Pyoderma and episcleritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Elentra objective lists fibromyalgia and mood disorders as IBS associations, and First Aid notes IBS may be associated with fibromyalgia and mood disorders such as anxiety and depression. The distractors are extraintestinal or metabolic complications of inflammatory bowel disease. Sources: SDL 32 Elentra Objective 1; First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (FA) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS may be associated with fibromyalgia and with anxiety or depression (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: candidate IBS genes include serotonin reuptake transporters and cannabinoid receptors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"FA"},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first-line treatment for irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Oral glucocorticoid course","B":"Lifestyle and diet changes","C":"Long-term opioid therapy","D":"Empiric antibiotic course","E":"Elective subtotal colectomy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Elentra objective and First Aid both give lifestyle modification and dietary changes as first-line IBS treatment. Glucocorticoids target inflammatory bowel disease, and colectomy is not indicated for a disorder without structural disease. Long-term opioids and empiric antibiotics are not first-line. Sources: SDL 32 Elentra Objective 1; First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: first-line IBS treatment is lifestyle modification and dietary change (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: 5-HT3 receptor antagonists are effective in many cases of diarrhea-predominant IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Long-term opioids are not IBS therapy.","D":"Empiric antibiotics are not first-line therapy.","A":"Glucocorticoids target inflammatory bowel disease.","E":"Colectomy is not indicated for a disorder without structural disease."}},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene encoding an intracellular sensor of bacterial muramyl dipeptide is most strongly associated with Crohn disease?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"CFTR","C":"NOD2","D":"HFE","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NOD2 encodes an intracellular sensor of muramyl dipeptide and is the gene most strongly associated with Crohn disease in European populations, though risk alleles occur in about 30% of patients. NOD2 variants are protective in ulcerative colitis. APC mutations drive familial adenomatous polyposis and sporadic colon cancer, not Crohn disease. CFTR mutations cause cystic fibrosis, not Crohn disease, which is linked to the bacterial sensor NOD2. HFE mutations cause hereditary hemochromatosis, not Crohn disease. KRAS is an oncogene in colorectal adenoma progression, not a sensor of muramyl dipeptide. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ATG16L1 and IRGM, which act in autophagosome formation, are additional Crohn disease risk genes (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: very early onset IBD can be monogenic, most often from IL10 or IL-10 receptor mutations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC mutations drive familial adenomatous polyposis and sporadic colon cancer, not Crohn disease.","B":"CFTR mutations cause cystic fibrosis, not Crohn disease, which is linked to the bacterial sensor NOD2.","D":"HFE mutations cause hereditary hemochromatosis, not Crohn disease.","E":"KRAS is an oncogene in colorectal adenoma progression, not a sensor of muramyl dipeptide."}},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":5,"stem":"Which form of microscopic colitis shows a strong association with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Collagenous colitis","B":"Diversion colitis","C":"Ischemic colitis","D":"Ulcerative proctitis","E":"Lymphocytic colitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lymphocytic colitis is strongly associated with celiac disease and autoimmune diseases including Graves disease, rheumatoid arthritis, and autoimmune gastritis. Its collagen layer is of normal thickness, and intraepithelial lymphocytes often exceed 20 per 100 colonocytes. Collagenous colitis is defined by a thick subepithelial collagen band. Ischemic colitis is a vascular injury with abnormal endoscopy, not a form of microscopic colitis. Ulcerative proctitis is a limited form of ulcerative colitis with visible mucosal disease, not microscopic colitis. Diversion colitis arises in a surgically defunctioned colon segment, shows prominent mucosal lymphoid follicles, and resolves when fecal flow is restored. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Microscopic colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: microscopic colitis is most common in older females and has a normal endoscopic appearance (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Microscopic colitis). COMLEX: both forms of microscopic colitis cause chronic, nonbloody, watery diarrhea without weight loss (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Collagenous colitis is defined by a thick subepithelial collagen band.","C":"Ischemic colitis is a vascular injury with abnormal endoscopy, not a form of microscopic colitis.","D":"Ulcerative proctitis is a limited form of ulcerative colitis with visible mucosal disease, not microscopic colitis.","B":"Diversion colitis arises in a surgically defunctioned colon segment, shows prominent mucosal lymphoid follicles, and resolves when fecal flow is restored."}},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":5,"stem":"Which antibodies are often present in Crohn disease but typically absent in ulcerative colitis?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Saccharomyces antibodies","B":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","C":"Anti-smooth muscle antibodies","D":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA","E":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crohn disease patients often have anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies, which are typically absent in ulcerative colitis. In contrast, up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients have p-ANCA. Antimitochondrial antibodies mark primary biliary cholangitis, not Crohn disease. Anti-smooth muscle antibodies mark autoimmune hepatitis, not Crohn disease. Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA is the serologic test for celiac disease, not Crohn disease. Anti-parietal cell antibodies mark autoimmune gastritis, not Crohn disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease may be positive for anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies (ASCA) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: fecal calprotectin helps distinguish IBD activity from noninflammatory disease such as IBS (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Antimitochondrial antibodies mark primary biliary cholangitis, not Crohn disease.","C":"Anti-smooth muscle antibodies mark autoimmune hepatitis, not Crohn disease.","D":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA is the serologic test for celiac disease, not Crohn disease.","E":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies mark autoimmune gastritis, not Crohn disease."}},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the colonic wall do sigmoid diverticula form?","choices":{"A":"Bases of colonic crypts","B":"Lymphoid aggregate sites","C":"Vasa recta entry points","D":"Mesenteric fat attachments","E":"Haustral fold crests"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Focal gaps in the inner circular muscle occur where nerves and arterial vasa recta penetrate it. In the colon these gaps are unreinforced, so raised intraluminal pressure herniates mucosa through them. Diverticula are not at crypt bases; they herniate through muscle gaps where vasa recta penetrate. Lymphoid aggregates are not the weak points; diverticula form at vasa recta entry gaps in the circular muscle. Haustral fold crests are not the weak points; diverticula herniate where vasa recta penetrate the inner circular muscle. Robbins places colonic diverticula at unreinforced sites where nerves and vasa recta cross the inner circular muscle layer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur especially where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: colonic diverticula are 0.5 to 1 cm flask-like outpouchings that are uniformly spaced (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diverticula are not at crypt bases; they herniate through muscle gaps where vasa recta penetrate.","B":"Lymphoid aggregates are not the weak points; diverticula form at vasa recta entry gaps in the circular muscle.","E":"Haustral fold crests are not the weak points; diverticula herniate where vasa recta penetrate the inner circular muscle.","D":"Robbins places colonic diverticula at unreinforced sites where nerves and vasa recta cross the inner circular muscle layer."}},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which dietary pattern promotes sigmoid diverticular disease by raising intraluminal pressure?","choices":{"A":"High intake of dairy foods","B":"Frequent spicy food intake","C":"Heavy daily caffeine intake","D":"Gluten-containing grains","E":"Low fiber, low stool bulk"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Low-fiber diets reduce stool bulk, so the sigmoid colon generates exaggerated peristaltic contractions and high intraluminal pressure that push mucosa through weak points where vasa recta cross the muscle. A high intake of dairy foods is not the dietary factor Robbins or First Aid ties to diverticula. Frequent spicy food intake is not linked to diverticula in these sources. Heavy daily caffeine intake is not described as a cause of colonic diverticula. Gluten-containing grains trigger celiac disease in susceptible people, not diverticular disease. Sources: Robbins ch 17, sigmoid diverticular disease; First Aid 2025 GI, diverticulosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low-fiber diets raise sigmoid intraluminal pressure and drive pseudodiverticula at vasa recta entry points (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Fiber supplementation may ease symptoms of diverticular disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A high intake of dairy foods is","B":"Frequent spicy food intake is not linked","C":"Heavy daily caffeine intake is not described","D":"Gluten-containing grains trigger celiac disease in susceptible"}},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the sex ratio of Meckel diverticula?","choices":{"A":"Twice as common in males","B":"Twice as common in females","C":"Equal in males and females","D":"Four times more in females","E":"Ten times more in males"},"correct":"A","explanation":"By the rule of 2s, Meckel diverticula have a male-to-female ratio of 2:1, occur in about 2% of people, lie within 2 feet of the ileocecal valve, are about 2 inches long, and are most often symptomatic by age 2. Meckel diverticula are twice as common in males, not females, per the rule of 2s. Meckel diverticula are not equal by sex; the rule of 2s gives a 2:1 male predominance. Meckel diverticula favor males 2:1, so a fourfold female excess is wrong. A tenfold male excess overstates the 2:1 male-to-female ratio of Meckel diverticula. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a Meckel diverticulum extends from the antimesenteric side of the ileum and may contain ectopic pancreatic tissue (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: about 4% of Meckel diverticula ever become symptomatic (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Meckel diverticula are twice as common in males, not females, per the rule of 2s.","C":"Meckel diverticula are not equal by sex; the rule of 2s gives a 2:1 male predominance.","D":"Meckel diverticula favor males 2:1, so a fourfold female excess is wrong.","E":"A tenfold male excess overstates the 2:1 male-to-female ratio of Meckel diverticula."}},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the colon are hyperplastic polyps most commonly found?","choices":{"A":"Right colon","B":"Transverse colon","C":"Cecum and appendix","D":"Left colon","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are most common in the left colon, are typically less than 5 mm, and are frequently multiple in the sigmoid colon and rectum. The right colon is the usual site of sessile serrated polyps, whereas hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon. The transverse colon is not the typical site. Hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon, sigmoid, and rectum rather than the cecum and appendix. The terminal ileum is small bowel, while hyperplastic polyps are colonic lesions mostly of the left colon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are the most common colonic polyps and are predominantly located in the rectosigmoid region (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: colonic hyperplastic polyps are typically discovered in the sixth and seventh decades (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The right colon is the usual site of sessile serrated polyps, whereas hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon.","B":"The transverse colon is not the typical site; hyperplastic polyps cluster.","C":"Hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon, sigmoid, and rectum rather than the cecum and appendix.","E":"The terminal ileum is small bowel, while hyperplastic polyps are colonic lesions mostly of the left colon."}},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the proposed pathogenesis of colonic hyperplastic polyps?","choices":{"A":"Biallelic APC gene inactivation","B":"Germline STK11 gene mutation","C":"Repeated mucosal prolapse injury","D":"Mismatch repair gene silencing","E":"Delayed epithelial shedding"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are thought to result from decreased epithelial turnover and delayed shedding of surface epithelial cells, leading to a piling up of goblet and absorptive cells. Mucosal prolapse drives solitary rectal ulcer polyps instead. Biallelic APC inactivation starts adenomas, but hyperplastic polyps are nonneoplastic and come from delayed epithelial shedding. Germline STK11 mutation causes Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not hyperplastic polyps. Mismatch repair silencing drives microsatellite-unstable neoplasia, not the nonneoplastic hyperplastic polyp. Robbins attributes repeated cycles of injury and healing with mucosal prolapse to inflammatory polyps of solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, not hyperplastic polyps. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are composed of mature goblet and absorptive cells with a serrated surface architecture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: epithelial hyperplasia can occur as a nonspecific reaction overlying any mass and may signal an adjacent lesion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Biallelic APC inactivation starts adenomas, but hyperplastic polyps are nonneoplastic and come from delayed epithelial shedding.","B":"Germline STK11 mutation causes Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not hyperplastic polyps.","D":"Mismatch repair silencing drives microsatellite-unstable neoplasia, not the nonneoplastic hyperplastic polyp.","C":"Robbins attributes repeated cycles of injury and healing with mucosal prolapse to inflammatory polyps of solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, not hyperplastic polyps."}},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"What underlying abnormality causes solitary rectal ulcer syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Prior pelvic radiation for carcinoma","B":"Poor anorectal sphincter relaxation","C":"Hypoperfusion of the rectal watershed","D":"Recurrent enteric bacterial infection","E":"Ectopic gastric mucosa in the rectum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Impaired relaxation of the anorectal sphincter creates a sharp angle at the anterior rectal shelf, causing recurrent abrasion and ulceration of the overlying mucosa. Entrapment of the resulting polyp in the fecal stream leads to mucosal prolapse. Hypoperfusion of a watershed causes ischemic colitis, not solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, which comes from poor sphincter relaxation. Recurrent infection is not the cause. Ectopic gastric mucosa is the ulcer-causing feature of Meckel diverticulum, not of solitary rectal ulcer. Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome comes from impaired anorectal sphincter relaxation and recurrent abrasion of the anterior rectal shelf, not from radiation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary rectal ulcer syndrome presents with rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, and an inflammatory lesion of the anterior rectal wall (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: inflammatory polyps form through repeated cycles of injury and healing (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Hypoperfusion of a watershed causes ischemic colitis, not solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, which comes from poor sphincter relaxation.","D":"Recurrent infection is not the cause.","E":"Ectopic gastric mucosa is the ulcer-causing feature of Meckel diverticulum, not of solitary rectal ulcer.","A":"Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome comes from impaired anorectal sphincter relaxation and recurrent abrasion of the anterior rectal shelf, not from radiation."}},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":5,"stem":"Which gene is the most common mutation identified in autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis?","choices":{"A":"STK11","B":"KRAS","C":"MUTYH","D":"SMAD4","E":"PTEN"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SMAD4, which encodes a signaling intermediate in the TGF-beta pathway, is the most common mutation in autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis. BMPR1A mutations account for other cases, and together they explain fewer than half of patients. STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, not juvenile polyposis. KRAS is an oncogene in sporadic adenomas, not the germline cause of juvenile polyposis. MUTYH mutations cause an autosomal recessive adenomatous polyposis, not autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis. PTEN mutations cause Cowden syndrome hamartomas, whereas juvenile polyposis most often involves SMAD4. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: juvenile polyposis is an autosomal dominant syndrome of numerous hamartomatous polyps in the colon, stomach, and small bowel (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: patients with SMAD4 mutations often have both juvenile polyposis and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, not juvenile polyposis.","B":"KRAS is an oncogene in sporadic adenomas, not the germline cause of juvenile polyposis.","C":"MUTYH mutations cause an autosomal recessive adenomatous polyposis, not autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis.","E":"PTEN mutations cause Cowden syndrome hamartomas, whereas juvenile polyposis most often involves SMAD4."}},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Where are the polyps of Peutz-Jeghers syndrome most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Small intestine","C":"Rectosigmoid colon","D":"Distal esophagus","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps are most common in the small intestine, although they may occur in the stomach and colon and rarely in the bladder and lungs. Grossly they are large, pedunculated, and lobulated. Peutz-Jeghers polyps can occur in the stomach but are most common in the small intestine. Peutz-Jeghers polyps are less common in the colon than in the small intestine. The distal esophagus is not a typical site of Peutz-Jeghers polyps, which favor the small intestine. The ascending colon is a less common site. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome features numerous hamartomatous polyps throughout the GI tract with hyperpigmented macules (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome presents at a median age of 11 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps can occur in the stomach but are most common in the small intestine.","C":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps are less common in the colon than in the small intestine.","D":"The distal esophagus is not a typical site of Peutz-Jeghers polyps, which favor the small intestine.","E":"The ascending colon is a less common site."}},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which pathway does the Peutz-Jeghers tumor suppressor STK11 regulate?","choices":{"A":"BMP receptor signaling","B":"Wnt signaling","C":"AMPK signaling","D":"DNA mismatch repair","E":"Hedgehog signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"STK11 is a tumor suppressor that regulates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) pathways, which control cell polarization and brake growth and anabolic metabolism. Germline heterozygous loss-of-function STK11 mutations occur in about half of familial cases. BMP receptor signaling is the TGF-beta pathway mutated in juvenile polyposis (BMPR1A), not the STK11 pathway. Wnt signaling is disrupted by APC loss in adenomas, not by STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers. DNA mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome, not Peutz-Jeghers, whose STK11 regulates AMPK. Hedgehog signaling is the pathway activated in medulloblastoma, not the AMPK pathway that STK11 regulates. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is autosomal dominant (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: the second normal STK11 copy is often lost through somatic mutation in Peutz-Jeghers-associated cancers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"BMP receptor signaling is the TGF-beta pathway mutated in juvenile polyposis (BMPR1A), not the STK11 pathway.","B":"Wnt signaling is disrupted by APC loss in adenomas, not by STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers.","D":"DNA mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome, not Peutz-Jeghers, whose STK11 regulates AMPK.","E":"Hedgehog signaling is the pathway activated in medulloblastoma, not the AMPK pathway that STK11 regulates."}},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has crampy lower abdominal pain on about 5 days each month for 6 months, with bloating and alternating loose and hard stools. Colonoscopy and biopsies are normal. Which additional feature best supports the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Nocturnal bloody stools","B":"Weight loss over 10%","C":"Elevated stool calprotectin","D":"Pain eased by defecation","E":"Symptom onset after age 60"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins lists IBS criteria as abdominal pain or discomfort at least 3 days per month over 3 months with improvement after defecation and a change in stool frequency or form, after other causes are excluded. An elevated fecal calprotectin points toward organic disease. Nocturnal bloody stools are an alarm feature of organic disease such as IBD, not support for IBS. Weight loss over 10% suggests organic disease that must be excluded, not IBS. Onset after age 60 raises concern for organic disease, whereas IBS typically begins in younger adults. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome, Inflammatory bowel diseases). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is recurrent abdominal pain with at least 2 of: related to defecation, change in stool frequency, change in stool form, so under FA no single feature is mandatory (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: IBS prevalence in high-income countries is about 5% to 10%, peaking between 20 and 40 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility, haustral contractions, and mass movements","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Nocturnal bloody stools are an alarm feature of organic disease such as IBD, not support for IBS.","B":"Weight loss over 10% suggests organic disease that must be excluded, not IBS.","E":"Onset after age 60 raises concern for organic disease, whereas IBS typically begins in younger adults."}},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":5,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has 2 years of urgent loose stools and intermittent abdominal cramping. Colonoscopy is normal, and he does not have celiac disease. A test shows excess fecal bile acids. Which mechanism best explains this subtype of his disorder?","choices":{"A":"Gluten-induced villous injury","B":"Subepithelial collagen band","C":"Bile acid malabsorption","D":"Bacterial invasion of crypts","E":"Granulomatous ileal disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excess bile acid synthesis or bile acid malabsorption is one identified cause of diarrhea-predominant IBS, likely through effects of bile acids on intestinal motility and epithelial ion transport. Gluten-induced villous injury is celiac disease, which has already been excluded. A subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, which a normal colonoscopy with biopsy would reveal, not IBS. Bacterial invasion of crypts would cause inflammatory colitis with abnormal biopsies, not IBS. Granulomatous ileal disease is Crohn disease, which would be visible on colonoscopy, unlike IBS. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea-predominant IBS shows increased colonic contractions and transit rates (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: opioids and anticholinergic psychoactive drugs are also used for diarrhea-predominant IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Gluten-induced villous injury is celiac disease, which has already been excluded.","B":"A subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, which a normal colonoscopy with biopsy would reveal, not IBS.","D":"Bacterial invasion of crypts would cause inflammatory colitis with abnormal biopsies, not IBS.","E":"Granulomatous ileal disease is Crohn disease, which would be visible on colonoscopy, unlike IBS."}},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman returns from Mexico after a week of fever and watery diarrhea. Six months later she has recurring cramps, bloating, and variable stool form with normal colonoscopy and biopsies. Which mechanism is proposed for this onset pattern?","choices":{"A":"Shift in the gut microbiome","B":"Antibodies to enteric neurons","C":"Persistent mucosal granulomas","D":"Chronic bacterial tissue invasion","E":"Subepithelial collagen deposits"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Some IBS patients relate onset to a bout of infectious gastroenteritis, suggesting that immune activation or a shift in the gut microbiome triggers disease. Granulomas, invasion, and collagen deposition would produce abnormal biopsies. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is chronic and may be diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, or mixed (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: defective brain-gut axis signaling is proposed in IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has 8 months of right lower quadrant pain, nonbloody diarrhea, and a draining perianal tract. Resection of a strictured terminal ileum shows a thick rubbery wall with deep fissures. Which additional gross finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Continuous disease of mucosa","B":"Thin wall with smooth serosa","C":"Superficial broad ulcers","D":"Flat atrophic mucosal surface","E":"Creeping fat over the serosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In transmural Crohn disease, mesenteric adipose tissue frequently extends over the serosal surface as creeping fat. Wall thickening from edema, fibrosis, and muscular hypertrophy drives strictures. Continuous mucosal disease with a normal serosa fits ulcerative colitis. A thin wall with smooth serosa contradicts Crohn disease, whose transmural inflammation thickens the wall and draws creeping fat. Superficial broad-based ulcers are typical of ulcerative colitis, whereas Crohn disease has deep linear fissuring ulcers. A flat atrophic surface does not fit Crohn disease, which shows cobblestoning from deep ulcers between spared mucosa. Robbins describes ulcerative colitis as continuous disease without skip lesions, while Crohn disease can be discontinuous with skip lesions. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease shows cobblestone mucosa, creeping fat, and a string sign on small bowel follow-through (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: disease often recurs at the anastomosis, and up to 40% need more resection within 10 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A thin wall with smooth serosa contradicts Crohn disease, whose transmural inflammation thickens the wall and draws creeping fat.","C":"Superficial broad-based ulcers are typical of ulcerative colitis, whereas Crohn disease has deep linear fissuring ulcers.","D":"A flat atrophic surface does not fit Crohn disease, which shows cobblestoning from deep ulcers between spared mucosa.","A":"Robbins describes ulcerative colitis as continuous disease without skip lesions, while Crohn disease can be discontinuous with skip lesions."}},{"id":"sdl32_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":5,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with newly diagnosed ulcerative colitis has pruritus and an alkaline phosphatase of 420 U/L (reference range 44–147 U/L). MRCP shows beaded intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts. When should colonoscopic dysplasia surveillance begin?","choices":{"A":"About 8 years after diagnosis","B":"At the time of diagnosis","C":"Beginning at 50 years of age","D":"After the first bloody relapse","E":"Upon confirmation of pancolitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patients with IBD typically enter surveillance about 8 years after diagnosis. The major exception is IBD with primary sclerosing cholangitis, which carries greater cancer risk, so surveillance starts at diagnosis. Age 50 is the average-risk screening start, but PSC-associated IBD requires surveillance from diagnosis. Surveillance does not wait for a relapse; PSC with IBD warrants surveillance from diagnosis. Surveillance in PSC-associated colitis begins at diagnosis regardless of disease extent. Robbins notes that surveillance usually begins about 8 years after IBD diagnosis, but patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis are enrolled at diagnosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease) (Colitis-associated neoplasia). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is associated with primary sclerosing cholangitis and MPO-ANCA/p-ANCA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: colitis-associated cancer risk rises sharply 8 to 10 years after onset (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary sclerosing cholangitis and cholestatic disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Age 50 is the average-risk screening start, but PSC-associated IBD requires surveillance from diagnosis.","D":"Surveillance does not wait for a relapse; PSC with IBD warrants surveillance from diagnosis.","E":"Surveillance in PSC-associated colitis begins at diagnosis regardless of disease extent.","A":"Robbins notes that surveillance usually begins about 8 years after IBD diagnosis, but patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis are enrolled at diagnosis."}},{"id":"sdl32_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has 4 months of watery, nonbloody diarrhea without weight loss. Colonoscopy is normal, but biopsies show more than 20 intraepithelial lymphocytes per 100 colonocytes with a collagen layer of normal thickness. Her medications are listed. Which is most likely contributing?","choices":{"A":"Metformin","B":"Lisinopril","C":"Levothyroxine","D":"Naproxen","E":"Amlodipine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These biopsies show lymphocytic microscopic colitis. Similar histologic changes can be induced by NSAIDs and possibly proton pump inhibitors. Microscopic colitis changes like these can be induced by NSAIDs and possibly proton pump inhibitors; metformin is not among the drugs implicated. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Microscopic colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microscopic colitis histology shows lamina propria lymphocytes with intraepithelial lymphocytosis or a thickened subepithelial collagen band (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Microscopic colitis). COMLEX: collagenous colitis has a subepithelial collagen layer thicker than 10 µm (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — large-intestine lamina propria and the collagen layer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Microscopic colitis changes like these can be induced by NSAIDs and possibly proton pump inhibitors; metformin is not among the drugs implicated.","B":"Lisinopril is not part of that answer.","C":"Levothyroxine is not part of that answer.","E":"Amlodipine is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl32_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man had left lower quadrant pain and fever with a WBC of 16,000/µL (reference range 4,500–11,000/µL) 2 months ago. He now has recurrent urinary tract infections and air in his urine. Which complication best explains his current findings?","choices":{"A":"Diverticular bleeding","B":"Sigmoid volvulus","C":"Ischemic stricture","D":"Free perforation","E":"Colovesical fistula"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inflammation in sigmoid diverticula can extend through the thin wall to cause serositis, abscesses, sinus tracts, and perforation. A tract into the bladder produces pneumaturia and recurrent infection. Diverticular bleeding causes hematochezia, not pneumaturia and recurrent urinary infections. Sigmoid volvulus causes obstruction and distention, not air in the urine. An ischemic stricture causes obstruction, not a tract into the bladder producing pneumaturia. Free perforation causes acute peritonitis, not the chronic pneumaturia of a colovesical fistula. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulitis complications include abscess, colovesical fistula with pneumaturia, obstruction, and perforation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulitis). COMLEX: surgery is generally reserved for severe or recurrent diverticulitis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 5 — high-fat, low-fiber diets in nutritional disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diverticular bleeding causes hematochezia, not pneumaturia and recurrent urinary infections.","B":"Sigmoid volvulus causes obstruction and distention, not air in the urine.","C":"An ischemic stricture causes obstruction, not a tract into the bladder producing pneumaturia.","D":"Free perforation causes acute peritonitis, not the chronic pneumaturia of a colovesical fistula."}},{"id":"sdl32_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a lifelong low-fiber diet has dozens of sigmoid outpouchings on colonoscopy, but small bowel imaging shows no outpouchings. Which structural difference best explains the sparing of her small bowel?","choices":{"A":"Lower intraluminal small bowel pressure","B":"Absence of penetrating vasa recta","C":"Denser submucosal lymphoid tissue","D":"Continuous longitudinal muscle coat","E":"Thicker muscularis mucosae layer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Both bowels have gaps in the inner circular muscle where nerves and vasa recta penetrate. In the small intestine these gaps are reinforced by a continuous outer longitudinal layer, whereas in the colon that layer is gathered into taeniae coli. Small bowel pressure is not the key difference; the small bowel's continuous longitudinal coat reinforces the muscle gaps. Vasa recta penetrate both small bowel and colon, so their absence does not explain small bowel sparing. The muscularis mucosae is not what reinforces the gaps; the continuous outer longitudinal layer does. The small bowel is spared because a continuous outer longitudinal muscle layer reinforces the gaps where vasa recta penetrate; lymphoid density is not the reason. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is caused by intraluminal pressure and focal weakness in the colonic wall (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulosis). COMLEX: hypertrophy of the inner circular muscle is commonly associated with diverticulosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Small bowel pressure is not the key difference; the small bowel's continuous longitudinal coat reinforces the muscle gaps.","B":"Vasa recta penetrate both small bowel and colon, so their absence does not explain small bowel sparing.","E":"The muscularis mucosae is not what reinforces the gaps.","C":"The small bowel is spared because a continuous outer longitudinal muscle layer reinforces the gaps where vasa recta penetrate; lymphoid density is not the reason."}},{"id":"sdl32_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy has episodes of screaming with legs drawn up, vomiting, and a stool of blood and mucus. A sausage-shaped right abdominal mass is palpated. At surgery, a congenital ileal pouch lined by gastric mucosa is found at the leading point. Which complication of this pouch explains his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Diverticulitis","B":"Intussusception","C":"Small bowel volvulus","D":"Peptic perforation","E":"Adhesive obstruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Meckel diverticulum can act as a lead point for intussusception and can also cause hematochezia, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum. Meckel diverticulitis mimics appendicitis. Volvulus causes twisting with obstruction. Peptic perforation from ectopic gastric mucosa causes peritonitis. Adhesive obstruction follows surgery. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (Meckel diverticulum); Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum can cause painless hematochezia, RLQ pain, intussusception, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: symptomatic Meckel cases can mimic acute appendicitis or obstruction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Meckel diverticulitis mimics appendicitis.","C":"Volvulus causes twisting with obstruction.","D":"Peptic perforation from ectopic gastric mucosa causes peritonitis.","E":"Adhesive obstruction follows surgery."}},{"id":"sdl32_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has several small smooth colonic polyps. Histology shows mature goblet and absorptive cells with a saw-tooth outline limited to the upper third of the crypts. Which premalignant lesion must be distinguished from these?","choices":{"A":"Tubular adenoma","B":"Juvenile hamartoma","C":"Sessile serrated polyp","D":"Inflammatory pseudopolyp","E":"Lymphoid aggregate polyp"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, but they must be distinguished from sessile serrated polyps, which are histologically similar and premalignant. A tubular adenoma is dysplastic from the start and does not mimic the saw-tooth pattern of a hyperplastic polyp. A juvenile hamartoma has cystic mucin-filled glands, not serrated crypt tops, and is not the key mimic. An inflammatory pseudopolyp is nonneoplastic regenerating mucosa, not a premalignant serrated mimic. First Aid classifies serrated polyps as neoplastic lesions with CpG island methylator phenotype that must be separated from hyperplastic polyps, unlike a lymphoid aggregate polyp. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serrated polyps show a saw-tooth crypt pattern, BRAF mutations, and CpG island methylation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps may occur singly but are frequently multiple (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A tubular adenoma is dysplastic from the start and does not mimic the saw-tooth pattern of a hyperplastic polyp.","B":"A juvenile hamartoma has cystic mucin-filled glands, not serrated crypt tops, and is not the key mimic.","D":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp is nonneoplastic regenerating mucosa, not a premalignant serrated mimic.","E":"First Aid classifies serrated polyps as neoplastic lesions with CpG island methylator phenotype that must be separated from hyperplastic polyps, unlike a lymphoid aggregate polyp."}},{"id":"sdl32_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with 15 years of ulcerative pancolitis has colonoscopy showing many mucosal projections, some joined at their tips into bridges. Biopsies show no dysplasia. How do these projections form?","choices":{"A":"Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth","B":"Clonal adenomatous epithelial growth","C":"Submucosal lymphoid hyperplasia","D":"Mucin retention in cystic glands","E":"Regrowth of mucosa between ulcers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In ulcerative colitis, isolated islands of regenerating mucosa bulge into the lumen to create pseudopolyps, and their tips may fuse into mucosal bridges. Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth describes Peutz-Jeghers or juvenile polyps, not UC pseudopolyps. Clonal adenomatous growth would be dysplastic, but these biopsies show none; pseudopolyps are regenerating mucosa. Mucin retention in cystic glands describes juvenile retention polyps, not UC pseudopolyps. Robbins explains that pseudopolyps are isolated islands of regenerating mucosa whose tips may fuse into mucosal bridges, not lymphoid hyperplasia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease); Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inflammatory pseudopolyps are due to mucosal erosion in inflammatory bowel disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: pseudopolyps are marked in ulcerative colitis and moderate in Crohn disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth describes Peutz-Jeghers or juvenile polyps, not UC pseudopolyps.","B":"Clonal adenomatous growth would be dysplastic, but these biopsies show none; pseudopolyps are regenerating mucosa.","D":"Mucin retention in cystic glands describes juvenile retention polyps, not UC pseudopolyps.","C":"Robbins explains that pseudopolyps are isolated islands of regenerating mucosa whose tips may fuse into mucosal bridges, not lymphoid hyperplasia."}},{"id":"sdl32_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, and chronic straining. A polypoid lesion is on the anterior rectal wall. Biopsy shows erosion and epithelial hyperplasia. Which additional histologic feature indicates mucosal prolapse?","choices":{"A":"Lamina propria smooth muscle","B":"Noncaseating submucosal granulomas","C":"Thickened subepithelial collagen","D":"Apoptotic bodies in crypt bases","E":"Foamy macrophages in lamina"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome polyps show mixed inflammation, erosion, and epithelial hyperplasia with prolapse-induced fibromuscular hyperplasia of the lamina propria. Noncaseating granulomas suggest Crohn disease, not mucosal prolapse. Thickened subepithelial collagen is collagenous colitis, not prolapse. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not mucosal prolapse. Prolapse in solitary rectal ulcer syndrome is marked by fibromuscular hyperplasia (smooth muscle) in the lamina propria, not by apoptotic crypt bodies. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary rectal ulcer polyps are an example of an inflammatory lesion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: granulation tissue-like capillary proliferation in the lamina propria reflects repeated erosion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Noncaseating granulomas suggest Crohn disease, not mucosal prolapse.","C":"Thickened subepithelial collagen is collagenous colitis, not prolapse.","E":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not mucosal prolapse.","D":"Prolapse in solitary rectal ulcer syndrome is marked by fibromuscular hyperplasia (smooth muscle) in the lamina propria, not by apoptotic crypt bodies."}},{"id":"sdl32_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has painless rectal bleeding, and a red mass prolapses through the anus. The resected pedunculated polyp is smooth, with cystic glands filled with mucin and inflammatory debris in an expanded lamina propria. He has no family history of polyps. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory pseudopolyp","B":"Juvenile retention polyp","C":"Tubulovillous adenoma","D":"Sessile hyperplastic polyp","E":"Submucosal leiomyoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sporadic juvenile polyps, also called retention polyps, are usually solitary, often rectal, and occur mostly before age 5. They show cystic mucin-filled glands in inflamed lamina propria and can prolapse through the anal sphincter. An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises in chronic IBD, which this child lacks. A tubulovillous adenoma is a dysplastic lesion of adults, not a cystic mucin-filled polyp in a 4-year-old. A hyperplastic polyp is small with serrated crypt tops, not cystic mucin-filled glands. First Aid classifies juvenile polyps as hamartomatous polyps of growths of normal colonic tissue with distorted architecture, not a submucosal leiomyoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary hamartomatous polyps do not have significant risk of transformation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: dysplasia is rare in sporadic juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises in chronic IBD, which this child lacks.","C":"A tubulovillous adenoma is a dysplastic lesion of adults, not a cystic mucin-filled polyp in a 4-year-old.","D":"A hyperplastic polyp is small with serrated crypt tops, not cystic mucin-filled glands.","E":"First Aid classifies juvenile polyps as hamartomatous polyps of growths of normal colonic tissue with distorted architecture, not a submucosal leiomyoma."}},{"id":"sdl32_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has dozens of hamartomatous polyps in the colon, recurrent nosebleeds, lip telangiectasias, and a pulmonary arteriovenous malformation. Her father had similar polyps. Which malignancy is she at greatest risk of developing by her mid-40s?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma","B":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma","C":"Colonic adenocarcinoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","E":"Pancreatic islet tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Juvenile polyposis syndrome is associated with dysplasia in polyps and separate adenomas, and 30% to 50% of patients develop colonic adenocarcinoma by age 45. Pulmonary arteriovenous malformations are recognized extraintestinal features. Hepatocellular carcinoma, pancreatic islet tumors, medulloblastoma, and papillary thyroid carcinoma are not that cancer risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: juvenile polyposis syndrome is associated with increased risk of colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colectomy may be needed to limit chronic hemorrhage from ulcerated juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is not a juvenile polyposis risk.","E":"Pancreatic islet tumors are not juvenile polyposis features.","A":"Medulloblastoma is not the cancer linked to this syndrome.","B":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma is not the cancer linked to this syndrome."}},{"id":"sdl32_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":5,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy has dark blue-brown macules on his lips, nostrils, and palms, and imaging shows multiple GI polyps. A resected polyp has an arborizing network of smooth muscle and normal epithelium. Which neoplasm warrants surveillance starting at birth?","choices":{"A":"Sex cord stromal tumor","B":"Gastric adenocarcinoma","C":"Small intestinal carcinoma","D":"Pancreatic carcinoma","E":"Lung adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome carries a lifetime cancer risk near 40%. Surveillance begins at birth for testicular sex cord tumors, in late childhood for gastric and small intestinal cancers, and in the second and third decades for colon, pancreatic, breast, lung, ovarian, and uterine cancers. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome carries increased risk of breast and GI cancers, including colorectal, gastric, small bowel, and pancreatic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: smooth muscle mixed with lamina propria distinguishes Peutz-Jeghers polyps from juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer and extracolonic cancers","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with fibromyalgia has 8 months of recurrent lower abdominal pain that eases after defecation, bloating that worsens through the day, and alternating hard and loose stools. CBC, tissue transglutaminase IgA, and colonoscopy with biopsies are normal. She wants to avoid medication. Which dietary change most directly targets the mechanism of her bloating?","choices":{"A":"Strict gluten avoidance to let villi recover","B":"Limiting poorly absorbed short carbohydrates","C":"High-protein meals to slow colonic transit","D":"Low-fat meals to reduce bile acid secretion","E":"Prolonged fasting to rest the colonic mucosa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Recurrent pain relieved by defecation with altered stool form, normal colonoscopy and biopsies, and an associated fibromyalgia point to irritable bowel syndrome, where poorly absorbed short-chain carbohydrates reach the colon, undergo bacterial fermentation, and produce the gas that drives bloating, so limiting them is a first-line diet change. Strict gluten avoidance treats celiac disease, which her normal transglutaminase serology and normal biopsies argue against. High-protein meals are not an IBS therapy and do not address colonic fermentation of carbohydrates. Low-fat meals target bile acid or fat malabsorption, which is not the mechanism of bloating in IBS. Prolonged fasting is not a recommended IBS measure because the mucosa is structurally normal in this disorder. Sources: Course master PDF (Irritable Bowel Syndrome); Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is a clinical diagnosis of pain related to defecation with altered bowel habits and normal mucosa; fermentation of poorly absorbed short-chain carbohydrates causes gas and bloating (course master PDF, IBS). COMLEX: start with diet and lifestyle changes and screen for fibromyalgia, depression, and anxiety.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Strict gluten avoidance treats celiac disease, which","C":"High-protein meals are not an IBS therapy","D":"Low-fat meals target bile acid or fat","E":"Prolonged fasting is not a recommended IBS"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man develops bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus. Colonoscopy shows continuous mucosal inflammation from the distal colon upward. Which recent habit change most likely preceded his onset?","choices":{"A":"Quitting cigarette smoking","B":"Starting oral contraceptives","C":"Starting daily NSAID use","D":"Adopting a high fiber diet","E":"Beginning heavy alcohol use"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Unlike Crohn disease, onset of ulcerative colitis can be associated with smoking cessation, and smoking may give partial symptomatic relief. Smoking is a strong risk factor for Crohn disease. NSAIDs are a trigger for microscopic colitis, which does not produce continuous grossly visible inflammation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ulcerative colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is treated with 5-aminosalicylic acid preparations, 6-mercaptopurine, infliximab, or colectomy (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: most ulcerative colitis patients have at least one relapse within 10 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"NSAIDs are a trigger for microscopic colitis, which does not produce continuous grossly visible inflammation."}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has passed large painless maroon stools for 2 days. He is pale, and his abdomen is soft and nontender. Hemoglobin is 7.9 g/dL (reference range: 10.5–13.5 g/dL). Upper endoscopy and colonoscopy are normal, and a technetium-99m pertechnetate scan shows a focus of uptake in the right lower quadrant. Which mechanism best explains his bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of a dilated submucosal vein","B":"Telescoping of ileum with venous infarction","C":"Erosion of a vasa recta at a colonic outpouching","D":"Acid from ectopic mucosa ulcerating nearby ileum","E":"Mucosal inflammation from an obstructed lumen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Painless brisk bleeding in a toddler with a right lower quadrant focus on a pertechnetate scan indicates a Meckel diverticulum containing ectopic gastric mucosa, which secretes acid that ulcerates the adjacent ileal mucosa and bleeds. Rupture of a dilated submucosal vein describes variceal or hemorrhoidal bleeding, and the pertechnetate scan marks gastric mucosa, not veins. Telescoping of ileum is intussusception, which causes colicky pain, vomiting, and currant jelly stool rather than painless bleeding in a calm child. Erosion of a vasa recta at a colonic outpouching is sigmoid diverticular bleeding in older adults, and his colonoscopy was normal. Mucosal inflammation from an obstructed lumen describes appendicitis, which presents with pain and fever rather than painless hemorrhage. Sources: Course master PDF (Meckel Diverticulum); Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum is a true diverticulum from a persistent vitelline duct; ectopic gastric mucosa secretes acid that ulcerates nearby ileum, and a pertechnetate scan detects it (course master PDF, Meckel). COMLEX: painless rectal bleeding in a young child calls for a Meckel scan and surgical resection if symptomatic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 5 — high-fat, low-fiber diets in nutritional disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rupture of a dilated submucosal vein describes","B":"Telescoping of ileum is intussusception, which causes","C":"Erosion of a vasa recta at a","E":"Mucosal inflammation from an obstructed lumen describes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has fatigue and 5 kg of weight loss. Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) with low ferritin. Colonoscopy shows a 4 cm ulcerated, friable sigmoid mass. Superficial biopsies from the edge of the mass show mature goblet and absorptive cells with serration limited to the upper crypts and no dysplasia. What is the best interpretation of these biopsies?","choices":{"A":"A benign hyperplastic polyp needing no follow-up","B":"A sessile serrated lesion that explains the mass","C":"Reactive change bordering an unsampled lesion","D":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp from chronic colitis","E":"A juvenile hamartoma with cystic dilated glands"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperplastic epithelium can form as a nonspecific reaction next to or over any mass, so bland serrated epithelium from the rim of an ulcerated mass in a man with weight loss and iron deficiency is a clue to an adjacent lesion that has not been sampled and needs deeper biopsies. A benign hyperplastic polyp has no malignant potential, but it would not explain a 4 cm ulcerated mass with anemia and weight loss. A sessile serrated lesion shows serration down to the crypt base with lateral crypt growth, which these upper-crypt changes lack. An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises from cycles of injury and healing in colitis, and he has no history of inflammatory bowel disease. A juvenile hamartoma is a childhood lesion with cystic glands and inflamed stroma, not mature serrated epithelium. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic polyps; Sessile serrated polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperplastic polyps show serration confined to the upper crypt and have no malignant potential, but hyperplasia also occurs as a reaction beside a mass (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: a biopsy that does not explain the endoscopic lesion should prompt repeat, deeper sampling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A benign hyperplastic polyp has no malignant","B":"A sessile serrated lesion shows serration down","D":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises from cycles of","E":"A juvenile hamartoma is a childhood lesion"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":3,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl has had recurrent episodes of colicky abdominal pain; the last one was a jejunal intussusception, and the resected lead point was a hamartomatous polyp. She has dark blue-brown macules on her lips, buccal mucosa, and palms. Her father had similar spots. For which malignancy should she have surveillance as an adult?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","B":"Cerebellar medulloblastoma","C":"Abdominal desmoid tumor","D":"Clear cell renal carcinoma","E":"Pancreatic adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hamartomatous small-bowel polyps causing intussusception plus mucocutaneous pigmentation that involves the buccal mucosa in an autosomal dominant pattern indicate Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, which carries a markedly increased lifetime cancer risk that includes pancreatic adenocarcinoma, along with colon, breast, lung, and gynecologic cancers. Hepatocellular carcinoma is linked to cirrhosis and chronic viral hepatitis, not to hamartomatous polyposis. Cerebellar medulloblastoma is the CNS tumor of Turcot syndrome, a variant of adenomatous polyposis or Lynch syndrome. Abdominal desmoid tumor is an extracolonic lesion of Gardner syndrome, a form of familial adenomatous polyposis. Clear cell renal carcinoma is associated with von Hippel-Lindau disease rather than Peutz-Jeghers syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Peutz-Jeghers syndrome); First Aid 2025 GI (hereditary polyposis syndromes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is autosomal dominant STK11 loss with hamartomatous polyps, buccal pigmentation, intussusception, and increased breast, GI, and pancreatic cancer risk (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: arrange lifelong multi-organ cancer surveillance and screen first-degree relatives.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is linked to cirrhosis and","B":"Cerebellar medulloblastoma is the CNS tumor of","C":"Abdominal desmoid tumor is an extracolonic lesion","D":"Clear cell renal carcinoma is associated with"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":5,"stem":"A 22-year-old man with Crohn disease has resection of a fibrotic terminal ileal stricture. The surgeon plans to remove as little bowel as possible. Which outcome makes this bowel preservation important?","choices":{"A":"Postoperative toxic megacolon","B":"Loss of colonic water absorption","C":"Adhesive small bowel obstruction","D":"Recurrence at the anastomosis","E":"Postsurgical bile reflux gastritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crohn disease often recurs at the site of anastomosis, and up to 40% of patients need additional resections within 10 years, so bowel preservation is an important part of surgical planning. Toxic megacolon is a complication of ulcerative colitis and is unrelated to how much ileum is preserved. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Crohn disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease is treated with glucocorticoids, azathioprine, antibiotics, and biologics such as infliximab and adalimumab (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: extensive small bowel Crohn disease can cause hypoalbuminemia and malabsorption of vitamin B12 and bile salts (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Toxic megacolon is a complication of ulcerative colitis and is unrelated to how much ileum is preserved."}},{"id":"grid_sdl32_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":4,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman who has never smoked has had 3 months of recurrent bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus, tenesmus, and lower abdominal cramps that ease after defecation. Stool studies for enteric pathogens are negative. Colonoscopy is performed, and biopsies of the involved segment are examined. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Gross lesions","Trans­mural","Collagen band","Many IELs"]},"choices":{"A":["+","+","−","−"],"B":["−","−","+","+"],"C":["+","−","−","−"],"D":["−","−","−","+"],"E":["−","−","−","−"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (+ gross lesions, − transmural, − collagen band, − many IELs) is correct. Ulcerative colitis begins in the rectum and extends proximally without skip lesions; nonsmokers are at higher risk, and attacks of bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus, tenesmus, and cramps relieved by defecation are typical. The mucosa is diffusely granular and friable with broad-based ulcers and pseudopolyps, but inflammation is limited to the mucosa and superficial submucosa, with neutrophilic crypt abscesses and crypt distortion. Choice A (+ gross lesions, + transmural, − collagen band, − many IELs) is Crohn disease, with transmural inflammation, skip lesions, cobblestoning, strictures, and fistulae, usually in the terminal ileum and favored by smoking. Choice B (− gross lesions, − transmural, + collagen band, + many IELs) is collagenous colitis, a microscopic colitis of older women with a normal-looking colon and a dense subepithelial collagen band >10 µm. Choice D (− gross lesions, − transmural, − collagen band, + many IELs) is lymphocytic colitis, with normal collagen thickness but >20 intraepithelial T lymphocytes per 100 colonocytes; both microscopic colitides cause nonbloody watery diarrhea. Choice E (all −) is irritable bowel syndrome, in which endoscopy and biopsies are characteristically normal. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 32, sections 1–2: IBS, Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis); Robbins Ch. 17 (inflammatory bowel disease) [Robbins]. The absence of an intraepithelial lymphocytosis and collagen band in Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis is inferred, as the notes list these features only for microscopic colitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: continuous rectal-up mucosal inflammation in a nonsmoker with bloody diarrhea is ulcerative colitis; transmural disease with skip lesions is Crohn (Exam 3 notes, SDL 32). COMLEX: chronic watery diarrhea in an older woman with a normal colonoscopy calls for biopsy to find microscopic colitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 — IBD drugs (5-ASA, budesonide, anti-TNF)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinical diagnosis of irritable bowel syndrome requires abdominal pain on at least how many days, and over what span?","choices":{"A":"One day in a year","B":"Daily pain for one week only","C":"Pain only at night for ten years","D":"At least 3 days per month over 3 months","E":"A single episode of bloody diarrhea"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Irritable bowel syndrome requires pain or discomfort on at least 3 days per month over a 3-month period, plus two of the stool-related features, after organic disease is excluded. A single day in a year does not meet the duration required for this diagnosis. One week of daily pain is too short for the 3-month requirement. Nocturnal pain for years is a warning of organic disease, not the stated threshold. A single episode of bloody diarrhea is an organic alarm, not the symptom count for irritable bowel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A single day in a year does not meet the duration required for this diagnosis","B":"One week of daily pain is too short for the 3-month requirement","C":"Nocturnal pain for years is a warning of organic disease, not the stated threshold","E":"A single episode of bloody diarrhea is an organic alarm, not the symptom count for irritable bowel syndrome"}},{"id":"sdl32_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has had loose urgent stools and crampy lower abdominal pain for eight months. Colonoscopy and biopsies are normal, celiac serology is negative, and the pain eases a little after she stools. She is afebrile. Hemoglobin is 13.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and C-reactive protein is 1 mg/L (reference range: <10 mg/L). Diet changes have failed. Which drug class both eases the visceral pain and slows colonic transit?","choices":{"A":"A low-dose tricyclic antidepressant","B":"Lubiprostone","C":"Linaclotide","D":"Polyethylene glycol","E":"Infliximab"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome, a low-dose tricyclic such as amitriptyline reduces visceral pain and slows colonic transit. Lubiprostone opens chloride channels and is used when constipation predominates. Linaclotide is a guanylate cyclase-C agonist for constipation-predominant disease. Polyethylene glycol is an osmotic laxative and would not be chosen to slow transit. Infliximab is an anti-TNF antibody for inflammatory bowel disease, and this mucosa is normal.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lubiprostone opens chloride channels and is used when constipation predominates","C":"Linaclotide is a guanylate cyclase-C agonist for constipation-predominant disease","D":"Polyethylene glycol is an osmotic laxative and would not be chosen to slow transit","E":"Infliximab is an anti-TNF antibody for inflammatory bowel disease, and this mucosa is normal"}},{"id":"sdl32_q33","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Crohn disease is driven mainly by which T-cell pattern?","choices":{"A":"Th1 and Th17 activation","B":"A mucosal Th2-like response","C":"Isolated IgE against a food antigen","D":"Only regulatory T cells with no effector response","E":"A pure B-cell response without T cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crohn disease is an immune dysregulation with Th1 and Th17 activation. A mucosal Th2-like response is the pattern described for ulcerative colitis. IgE against a food antigen is allergy, not the Crohn T-cell pattern. Loss of regulation may contribute, but the effector pattern named for Crohn disease is Th1 and Th17. Crohn disease is not a pure B-cell illness without T cells.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A mucosal Th2-like response is the pattern described for ulcerative colitis","C":"IgE against a food antigen is allergy, not the Crohn T-cell pattern","D":"Loss of regulation may contribute, but the effector pattern named for Crohn disease is Th1 and Th17","E":"Crohn disease is not a pure B-cell illness without T cells"}},{"id":"sdl32_q34","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Collagenous colitis is identified by which finding under the surface epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Caseating granulomas through the full wall","B":"Flask-shaped ulcers","C":"A normal collagen layer with no lymphocytes","D":"Hundreds of adenomas","E":"A dense collagen band thicker than 10 µm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Collagenous colitis has a dense subepithelial collagen band thicker than 10 µm, with a normal endoscopic appearance. Caseating granulomas are not the lesion of collagenous colitis, and Crohn granulomas are noncaseating. Flask-shaped ulcers are amebic. A normal collagen thickness is the contrast with lymphocytic colitis, which is defined by intraepithelial lymphocytes instead. Hundreds of adenomas are polyposis, not microscopic colitis.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Caseating granulomas are not the lesion of collagenous colitis, and Crohn granulomas are noncaseating","B":"Flask-shaped ulcers are amebic","C":"A normal collagen thickness is the contrast with lymphocytic colitis, which is defined by intraepithelial lymphocytes instead","D":"Hundreds of adenomas are polyposis, not microscopic colitis"}},{"id":"sdl32_q35","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has had bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus for four months. Colonoscopy shows continuous granular mucosa from the rectum proximally, without skip areas, and biopsies stay in the mucosa. He does not smoke. Hemoglobin is 11.0 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) and albumin is 3.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Medicines have failed. Which operation can cure the colonic disease?","choices":{"A":"A short stricturoplasty that must be repeated at the old join","B":"Colectomy","C":"Removal of only the terminal ileum","D":"Drainage of a perianal fistula with no bowel resection","E":"Ligation of the superior mesenteric artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Colectomy cures ulcerative colitis, whose inflammation is limited to the colon and rectum. Repeated resection at an anastomosis is the surgical problem in Crohn disease, which colectomy does not cure. The terminal ileum is the usual Crohn site, and this continuous rectal-based colitis spares that pattern. A perianal fistula is a Crohn complication, not the lesion that colectomy is used to remove here. Ligating the superior mesenteric artery would infarct midgut and is not treatment of this colitis.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Repeated resection at an anastomosis is the surgical problem in Crohn disease, which colectomy does not cure","C":"The terminal ileum is the usual Crohn site, and this continuous rectal-based colitis spares that pattern","D":"A perianal fistula is a Crohn complication, not the lesion that colectomy is used to remove here","E":"Ligating the superior mesenteric artery would infarct midgut and is not treatment of this colitis"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has right-lower-quadrant pain, nonbloody diarrhea, and a perianal fistula. He smokes a pack a day. Resection shows skip lesions, creeping fat, and a noncaseating granuloma. Hemoglobin is 12.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) and C-reactive protein is 28 mg/L (reference range: <10 mg/L). Which exposure is most likely to keep this disease active?","choices":{"A":"Quitting cigarettes","B":"A high-fiber breakfast","C":"Oral iron for a low ferritin","D":"Continued cigarette smoking","E":"Drinking pasteurized milk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cigarette smoking is a strong risk factor that worsens Crohn disease activity. Stopping cigarettes is not what worsens Crohn disease; smoking itself does. Fiber is a diet measure in irritable bowel syndrome and is not the exposure that drives this transmural disease. Oral iron treats deficiency and is not the exposure that keeps Crohn disease active. Pasteurized milk is not the risk that worsens Crohn disease.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Stopping cigarettes is not what worsens Crohn disease; smoking itself does","B":"Fiber is a diet measure in irritable bowel syndrome and is not the exposure that drives this transmural disease","C":"Oral iron treats deficiency and is not the exposure that keeps Crohn disease active","E":"Pasteurized milk is not the risk that worsens Crohn disease"}},{"id":"sdl32_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"A single juvenile polyp in a toddler is usually found in which site?","choices":{"A":"The stomach, by the dozens","B":"The rectum","C":"The esophagus","D":"The gallbladder","E":"The pancreas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sporadic juvenile polyps are solitary rectal lesions in children younger than 5 years and have no malignant potential. Dozens of polyps throughout the gut are juvenile polyposis, not the usual solitary toddler polyp. The esophagus is not the usual site of a sporadic juvenile polyp. The gallbladder does not harbor juvenile retention polyps. The pancreas is not the site of a solitary juvenile polyp.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dozens of polyps throughout the gut are juvenile polyposis, not the usual solitary toddler polyp","C":"The esophagus is not the usual site of a sporadic juvenile polyp","D":"The gallbladder does not harbor juvenile retention polyps","E":"The pancreas is not the site of a solitary juvenile polyp"}},{"id":"sdl32_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy has crampy pain, and surgery reduces a small-bowel intussusception led by a lobulated polyp. The polyp shows an arborizing network of smooth muscle invested by normal epithelium. He has no rectal bleeding and no telangiectasias. Hemoglobin is 13.0 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which skin finding belongs with this syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Dark blue-brown macules on the lips and palms","B":"Flaky-paint desquamation from protein deficiency","C":"Rose spots on the chest","D":"A serpiginous track on the foot","E":"Bronze skin from iron overload"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome pairs hamartomatous polyps that have arborizing smooth muscle with dark blue-brown macules on the lips, nostrils, buccal mucosa, and palms. Flaky-paint skin is kwashiorkor, not this hamartoma syndrome. Rose spots are the rash of typhoid fever. A serpiginous foot track is cutaneous larva migrans. Bronze skin is the pigment of hereditary hemochromatosis.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Flaky-paint skin is kwashiorkor, not this hamartoma syndrome","C":"Rose spots are the rash of typhoid fever","D":"A serpiginous foot track is cutaneous larva migrans","E":"Bronze skin is the pigment of hereditary hemochromatosis"}}],"33":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic hallmark defines colorectal adenomas?","choices":{"A":"Crypt base serration","B":"Mucosal prolapse fibrosis","C":"Epithelial dysplasia","D":"Granulomatous inflammation","E":"Hamartomatous smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hallmark of colorectal adenomas is epithelial dysplasia, seen as nuclear hyperchromasia, elongation, and stratification, often with prominent nucleoli and fewer goblet cells. Serration is the architecture of hyperplastic and sessile serrated polyps. Prolapse-induced fibromuscular change marks solitary rectal ulcer polyps, not adenomas. Granulomatous inflammation suggests Crohn disease, not an adenoma. Arborizing smooth muscle defines Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps are neoplastic and arise via the chromosomal instability pathway with APC and KRAS mutations (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: colorectal adenomas range from 0.3 to 10 cm and have a velvet or raspberry-like surface (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Serration is the architecture of hyperplastic and sessile serrated polyps.","B":"Prolapse-induced fibromuscular change marks solitary rectal ulcer polyps, not adenomas.","D":"Granulomatous inflammation suggests Crohn disease, not an adenoma.","E":"Arborizing smooth muscle defines Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas."}},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which colorectal polyp type carries the greatest malignant potential?","choices":{"A":"Tubular adenoma","B":"Villous adenoma","C":"Hyperplastic polyp","D":"Hamartomatous polyp","E":"Inflammatory polyp"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Villous adenomas harbor cancer more often than tubular adenomas, partly because they tend to be larger, and size remains the strongest predictor of malignancy. Tubular adenomas are usually small and have less malignant potential than villous adenomas. Hyperplastic polyps are generally nonneoplastic, small, and found in the rectosigmoid. A solitary hamartomatous polyp has no significant risk of transformation. Inflammatory polyps form from mucosal erosion and regrowth in IBD and are not neoplastic. Sources: Robbins ch 17, colonic adenomas; First Aid 2025 GI, colonic polyps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Villous histology carries more malignant potential than tubular, and size is the key predictor (Robbins ch 17; First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Large villous adenomas warrant complete removal and closer surveillance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Tubular adenomas are usually small and have","C":"Hyperplastic polyps are generally nonneoplastic, small, and","D":"A solitary hamartomatous polyp has no significant","E":"Inflammatory polyps form from mucosal erosion and"}},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":5,"stem":"Why does intramucosal carcinoma within an adenoma carry little metastatic potential?","choices":{"A":"Absent mucosal lymphatics","B":"Intact muscularis propria","C":"Low tumor mitotic activity","D":"Peritumoral lymphocyte barrier","E":"Rapid surface cell shedding"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Functional lymphatic channels are absent in the colonic mucosa, so intramucosal carcinoma has little or no metastatic potential and complete polypectomy is generally curative. Invasion beyond the muscularis mucosae carries a risk of spread. The muscularis propria is not the barrier; lack of mucosal lymphatics limits spread, and invasion past the muscularis mucosae already carries risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomas are usually asymptomatic but may present with occult bleeding (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: invasion into the submucosal stalk of a pedunculated polyp constitutes invasive adenocarcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"The muscularis propria is not the barrier; lack of mucosal lymphatics limits spread, and invasion past the muscularis mucosae already carries risk.","C":"Low tumor mitotic activity is not part of that answer.","D":"Peritumoral lymphocyte barrier is not part of that answer.","E":"Rapid surface cell shedding is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"How many adenomas are needed for a diagnosis of classic familial adenomatous polyposis?","choices":{"A":"At least 10","B":"At least 25","C":"At least 100","D":"At least 50","E":"At least 1000"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At least 100 polyps are necessary for a diagnosis of classic FAP, though thousands may be present. Apart from their number, FAP polyps look like sporadic adenomas. Ten adenomas is far below the FAP threshold of at least 100. Twenty-five adenomas is below the classic FAP threshold of at least 100. Fifty adenomas falls short of the at-least-100 threshold for classic FAP. Thousands may be present, but the diagnostic threshold is at least 100, not 1000. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP polyps arise after puberty, are pancolonic, and involve the rectum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: about 75% of FAP cases are inherited and the remainder arise from de novo mutations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ten adenomas is far below the FAP threshold of at least 100.","B":"Twenty-five adenomas is below the classic FAP threshold of at least 100.","D":"Fifty adenomas falls short of the at-least-100 threshold for classic FAP.","E":"Thousands may be present, but the diagnostic threshold is at least 100, not 1000."}},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the protein product of the gene mutated in familial adenomatous polyposis normally do?","choices":{"A":"Repairs base mismatches","B":"Activates RAS signaling","C":"Inhibits mTOR signaling","D":"Blocks TGF beta signals","E":"Degrades beta catenin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"APC is a key negative regulator of Wnt signaling. It binds and promotes degradation of beta catenin, so its loss lets beta catenin enter the nucleus and drive MYC and cyclin D1 transcription. Repairing base mismatches is the job of mismatch repair genes such as MSH2 and MLH1, not APC. APC does not activate RAS; it degrades beta-catenin, while KRAS activation is a separate later step in the adenoma-carcinoma sequence. Inhibiting mTOR via AMPK is the role of STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers, not APC. APC does not block TGF-beta; TGF-beta pathway genes such as SMAD4 are lost later in the sequence or in juvenile polyposis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis); Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP is an autosomal dominant mutation of the APC tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q21-q22 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colon cancers without APC mutations often carry beta catenin mutations that resist APC-dependent degradation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Repairing base mismatches is the job of mismatch repair genes such as MSH2 and MLH1, not APC.","B":"APC does not activate RAS; it degrades beta-catenin, while KRAS activation is a separate later step in the adenoma-carcinoma sequence.","C":"Inhibiting mTOR via AMPK is the role of STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers, not APC.","D":"APC does not block TGF-beta; TGF-beta pathway genes such as SMAD4 are lost later in the sequence or in juvenile polyposis."}},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which extraintestinal manifestation of familial adenomatous polyposis can be detected at birth?","choices":{"A":"Multiple mandibular osteomas","B":"Abdominal wall desmoid tumors","C":"Multiple epidermoid skin cysts","D":"Retinal pigment hypertrophy","E":"Impacted supernumerary teeth"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium can be detected at birth and may be an adjunct to early FAP screening. Osteomas, desmoids, skin cysts, and dental anomalies appear later in variants such as Gardner syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gardner syndrome adds osseous and soft tissue tumors and impacted or supernumerary teeth to FAP (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: specific APC mutations partly explain FAP variants (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which genes are mutated in most patients with Lynch syndrome?","choices":{"A":"APC or CTNNB1","B":"BRCA1 or BRCA2","C":"SMAD4 or BMPR1A","D":"KRAS or BRAF","E":"MSH2 or MLH1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"There are at least five mismatch repair genes, but most HNPCC patients have mutations in MSH2 or MLH1. Patients inherit one mutant and one normal allele. APC mutations cause FAP, not Lynch syndrome, which involves mismatch repair genes. SMAD4 or BMPR1A mutations cause juvenile polyposis, not Lynch syndrome. KRAS and BRAF are somatic oncogene mutations in sporadic tumors, not the germline defect in Lynch syndrome. Most patients with Lynch syndrome carry mutations in the mismatch repair genes MSH2 or MLH1; BRCA1 and BRCA2 are not the genes involved. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome is an autosomal dominant mismatch repair defect with about 80% progression to colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: the human genome has about 50,000 to 100,000 microsatellites (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC mutations cause FAP, not Lynch syndrome, which involves mismatch repair genes.","C":"SMAD4 or BMPR1A mutations cause juvenile polyposis, not Lynch syndrome.","D":"KRAS and BRAF are somatic oncogene mutations in sporadic tumors, not the germline defect in Lynch syndrome.","B":"Most patients with Lynch syndrome carry mutations in the mismatch repair genes MSH2 or MLH1; BRCA1 and BRCA2 are not the genes involved."}},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"How do colon cancers in hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer typically differ from sporadic colon cancers?","choices":{"A":"They arise among hundreds of adenomas","B":"They appear at younger ages","C":"They are mostly left-sided tumors","D":"They show chromosomal instability","E":"They carry germline APC mutations"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Colon cancers in Lynch syndrome tend to occur at younger ages than sporadic cancers and are often in the right colon, driven by mismatch repair loss and microsatellite instability. Hundreds of adenomas define familial adenomatous polyposis, whereas Lynch syndrome is nonpolyposis. Lynch syndrome cancers favor the proximal, right colon rather than the left side. Chromosomal instability marks the APC pathway, while Lynch tumors show microsatellite instability. Germline APC mutations cause familial adenomatous polyposis, while Lynch syndrome involves MSH2 or MLH1. Sources: Robbins ch 17, hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome cancers arise younger, favor the right colon, and show microsatellite instability (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Colon cancer before 50 or a family with endometrial cancer prompts mismatch repair testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hundreds of adenomas define familial adenomatous polyposis,","C":"Lynch syndrome cancers favor the proximal, right","D":"Chromosomal instability marks the APC pathway, while","E":"Germline APC mutations cause familial adenomatous polyposis,"}},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which colonic region is characteristically involved in Lynch syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon","B":"Rectal ampulla","C":"Proximal colon","D":"Splenic flexure","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The objective and First Aid place Lynch syndrome in the proximal colon, and Robbins notes HNPCC colon cancers are often in the right colon. The sigmoid colon is a left-sided site. The rectum is distal, whereas Lynch syndrome cancers favor the cecum and ascending colon. The splenic flexure is not characteristic. The descending colon is left-sided, whereas Lynch syndrome favors the right colon. Sources: SDL 33 Elentra Objective 3; Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal colon is involved in Lynch syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: microsatellite unstable and CIMP tumors are frequently located in the right colon (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The sigmoid colon is a left-sided site.","B":"The rectum is distal, whereas Lynch syndrome cancers favor the cecum and ascending colon.","D":"The splenic flexure is not characteristic.","E":"The descending colon is left-sided, whereas Lynch syndrome favors the right colon."}},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"In the adenoma-carcinoma sequence, which gene is mutated in most colon cancers but uncommonly in adenomas?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"BRAF","C":"MLH1","D":"TP53","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"TP53 is mutated in most colon cancers but uncommonly in adenomas, so its loss is a late event that marks progression to carcinoma in the chromosomal instability pathway. APC loss is the early first hit that starts adenoma formation, so it is already present in adenomas. BRAF mutation belongs to the serrated, microsatellite unstable pathway, whose tumors usually keep intact TP53. MLH1 loss drives mismatch repair deficiency and is not the late step of the APC pathway. KRAS mutations are already present in many adenomas larger than 1 cm, so they are not rare in adenomas. Sources: Robbins ch 17, colonic adenocarcinoma pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic sequence is APC loss, then KRAS activation, SMAD2 and SMAD4 loss, and TP53 loss at the transition to carcinoma (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Removing adenomas before late genetic hits is why screening colonoscopy prevents cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC loss is the early first hit","B":"BRAF mutation belongs to the serrated, microsatellite","C":"MLH1 loss drives mismatch repair deficiency and","E":"KRAS mutations are already present in many"}},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"Which dietary pattern is most closely associated with increased colorectal cancer risk?","choices":{"A":"Low vegetable fiber diet","B":"High citrus fruit intake","C":"Low red meat consumption","D":"High fish oil consumption","E":"Low refined sugar intake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Low intake of unabsorbable vegetable fiber with high intake of refined carbohydrates and fat is most closely tied to colorectal cancer. Reduced stool bulk may prolong mucosal contact with bacterial oxidative byproducts. Low red meat intake is not a risk; high animal fat intake raises colorectal cancer risk. Low refined sugar intake is not a risk; high refined carbohydrate intake is. Robbins names low unabsorbable vegetable fiber and high refined carbohydrate and fat intake as the dietary factors most tied to colorectal cancer, not citrus fruit intake. First Aid lists a diet of processed meat with low fiber among colorectal cancer risk factors, not high fish oil consumption. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer risk factors include adenomatous and serrated polyps, IBD, tobacco use, and processed meat with low fiber (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: high fat intake raises hepatic bile acid synthesis, and bacteria can convert bile acids into carcinogens (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Low red meat intake is not a risk; high animal fat intake raises colorectal cancer risk.","E":"Low refined sugar intake is not a risk; high refined carbohydrate intake is.","B":"Robbins names low unabsorbable vegetable fiber and high refined carbohydrate and fat intake as the dietary factors most tied to colorectal cancer, not citrus fruit intake.","D":"First Aid lists a diet of processed meat with low fiber among colorectal cancer risk factors, not high fish oil consumption."}},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":5,"stem":"Which organ is the most common site of distant metastasis from colon cancer?","choices":{"A":"Lung","B":"Liver","C":"Bone","D":"Brain","E":"Ovary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the colon drains through the portal circulation, the liver is the most common site of distant metastases. Regional nodes, lungs, and bones can also be involved. Robbins states that, because of portal drainage, the liver is the most common site of distant colon cancer metastasis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectosigmoid is the most common colorectal cancer site, followed by ascending and descending colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: the rectum does not drain via the portal circulation, so metastatic anal carcinomas often circumvent the portal route (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Lung is not part of that answer.","C":"Bone is not part of that answer.","D":"Brain is not part of that answer.","E":"Robbins states that, because of portal drainage, the liver is the most common site of distant colon cancer metastasis."}},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"Deep tenderness two-thirds of the way from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine is known by what name?","choices":{"A":"Rovsing sign","B":"Psoas sign","C":"Murphy sign","D":"McBurney sign","E":"Obturator sign"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The McBurney sign is deep tenderness two-thirds of the distance from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine, a classic finding in acute appendicitis. Rovsing sign is severe RLQ pain on palpation of the LLQ, not tenderness at the RLQ point itself. Psoas sign is lower abdominal pain made worse by hip extension, not point tenderness on deep palpation. Murphy sign is inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation in cholecystitis, not an RLQ appendiceal finding. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute appendicitis may also elicit psoas, obturator, and Rovsing signs (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: acute appendicitis has a lifetime risk of 7% and affects males slightly more often (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rovsing sign is severe RLQ pain on palpation of the LLQ, not tenderness at the RLQ point itself.","B":"Psoas sign is lower abdominal pain made worse by hip extension, not point tenderness on deep palpation.","C":"Murphy sign is inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation in cholecystitis, not an RLQ appendiceal finding."}},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most common tumor of the appendix?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous cystadenoma","B":"Signet ring carcinoma","C":"Burkitt lymphoma","D":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E":"Neuroendocrine tumor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor is the most common appendiceal tumor. It is usually incidental, forms a bulbous swelling at the distal tip, and rarely spreads. Mucinous neoplasms occur in the appendix but are less common. A gastrointestinal stromal tumor is not this lesion. Signet-ring cell carcinoma is the diffuse pattern of gastric adenocarcinoma. Burkitt lymphoma is not an appendiceal tumor. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs such as octreotide are used to treat carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Octreotide). COMLEX: conventional adenomas and adenocarcinomas of the appendix can cause obstruction that mimics acute appendicitis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucinous neoplasms occur in the appendix but are less common.","D":"A gastrointestinal stromal tumor is not this appendiceal lesion.","B":"Signet-ring cell carcinoma is the diffuse pattern of gastric adenocarcinoma.","C":"Burkitt lymphoma is not an appendiceal tumor."}},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major risk factor for HPV-associated squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal?","choices":{"A":"Receptive anal intercourse","B":"Chronic laxative overuse","C":"Portal venous hypertension","D":"Low dietary fiber intake","E":"Untreated ulcerative colitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anal squamous cell carcinoma is frequently associated with high-risk HPV strains, and the major risk factor for these HPV-associated lesions is anal sex. Low-risk HPV strains cause condyloma acuminatum. Portal hypertension predisposes to hemorrhoids, not HPV-related anal squamous carcinoma. Low fiber intake is a colorectal adenocarcinoma risk, not a risk for HPV-driven anal squamous carcinoma. Ulcerative colitis raises colonic adenocarcinoma risk, not HPV-associated anal squamous carcinoma. Chronic anthraquinone laxative use causes melanosis coli, a brown pigmentation of the colon, and epidemiologic studies do not link it to colorectal cancer; it is not a risk factor for HPV-driven anal squamous carcinoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Anal canal). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma arises below the pectinate line, while adenocarcinoma arises above it (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Pectinate line). COMLEX: basaloid anal squamous cell carcinoma has been called cloacogenic carcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Portal hypertension predisposes to hemorrhoids, not HPV-related anal squamous carcinoma.","D":"Low fiber intake is a colorectal adenocarcinoma risk, not a risk for HPV-driven anal squamous carcinoma.","E":"Ulcerative colitis raises colonic adenocarcinoma risk, not HPV-associated anal squamous carcinoma.","B":"Chronic anthraquinone laxative use causes melanosis coli, a brown pigmentation of the colon, and epidemiologic studies do not link it to colorectal cancer; it is not a risk factor for HPV-driven anal squamous carcinoma."}},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a 3 cm sessile colonic polyp with long, slender surface projections on biopsy. Her daughter asks what best predicts whether such a growth harbors cancer. Which feature is most important?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cell number","B":"Degree of inflammation","C":"Lesion diameter","D":"Tubular gland pattern","E":"Patient sex and age"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Size is the most important characteristic correlating with malignancy in an adenoma. Villous architecture correlates with cancer partly because villous adenomas are larger, and architectural category has little significance in isolation. Inflammation, goblet cell number, and patient sex and age are not that predictor. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tubular adenomas have less malignant potential than villous adenomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: tubular adenomas tend to be small pedunculated polyps of rounded glands (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Architectural pattern has little significance on its own.","B":"Inflammation is not a predictor of cancer in an adenoma.","A":"Goblet cell number does not track with cancer in an adenoma.","E":"Patient sex and age do not track with cancer in an adenoma."}},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":5,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has three colonic adenomas removed. One shows high-grade dysplasia and the others show low-grade dysplasia. How does the high-grade lesion affect cancer risk in his remaining polyps?","choices":{"A":"Higher risk in other polyps","B":"Unchanged risk in other polyps","C":"Field cancerization of colon","D":"Germline repair defect implied","E":"Doubled risk in other polyps"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High-grade dysplasia is a risk factor for cancer in that individual polyp but does not increase cancer risk in other polyps within that patient. High-grade dysplasia raises risk only in that polyp, not in his other polyps. High-grade dysplasia does not double risk in other polyps; it affects only the polyp containing it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps progress through an adenoma-carcinoma sequence with APC loss followed by KRAS mutation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: high-grade dysplasia raises cancer risk within the individual polyp (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — chronic reflux injury and Barrett dysplasia","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"High-grade dysplasia raises risk only in that polyp, not in his other polyps.","E":"High-grade dysplasia does not double risk in other polyps; it affects only the polyp containing it."}},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman has small adenomas removed at each of several surveillance endoscopies. She asks why repeated procedures lower her colon cancer risk. Which explanation is best?","choices":{"A":"Ablation of mucosal lymphatics","B":"Reversal of APC mutations","C":"Induction of mucosal immunity","D":"Removal of precursor polyps","E":"Shrinkage of hyperplastic polyps"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adenomas are precursors of colorectal adenocarcinoma, and removed polyps do not progress to adenocarcinoma, so repeated surveillance is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Most adenomas do not progress, and no tool identifies those that will. Polypectomy does not ablate lymphatics; it lowers risk by removing adenomas before they progress. Endoscopy cannot reverse APC mutations; it removes precursor polyps. Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, so shrinking them would not lower cancer risk. First Aid describes adenomatous polyps as neoplastic precursors that progress via the adenoma-carcinoma sequence, so removing them lowers risk rather than inducing mucosal immunity. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: average-risk screening begins at age 45 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: adenomas develop in about 30% of Western adults by age 60 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Polypectomy does not ablate lymphatics; it lowers risk by removing adenomas before they progress.","B":"Endoscopy cannot reverse APC mutations.","E":"Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, so shrinking them would not lower cancer risk.","C":"First Aid describes adenomatous polyps as neoplastic precursors that progress via the adenoma-carcinoma sequence, so removing them lowers risk rather than inducing mucosal immunity."}},{"id":"sdl33_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with familial adenomatous polyposis had a prophylactic colectomy at age 20. Which site most needs continued endoscopic surveillance for adenomas?","choices":{"A":"Ampulla of Vater","B":"Distal esophagus","C":"Gallbladder fundus","D":"Pancreatic tail","E":"Hepatic hilum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colectomy prevents colorectal cancer in FAP, but patients remain at risk elsewhere. The ampulla of Vater and the stomach are common extracolonic sites of adenomas and, if untreated, cancer. The distal esophagus is not an FAP adenoma site. The gallbladder is not a recognized FAP adenoma site. The pancreatic tail is not an FAP adenoma site. The hepatic hilum is not an FAP adenoma site. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without prophylactic colectomy, 100% of FAP patients progress to colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: untreated FAP leads to colorectal adenocarcinoma often before age 30 and in nearly all untreated patients by age 50 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 4 — location-dependent presentation of pancreatic head and periampullary tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The distal esophagus is not an FAP adenoma site.","C":"The gallbladder is not a recognized FAP adenoma site.","D":"The pancreatic tail is not an FAP adenoma site.","E":"The hepatic hilum is not an FAP adenoma site."}},{"id":"sdl33_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":5,"stem":"A 42-year-old man has about 30 colonic adenomas plus several serrated and hyperplastic polyps. His parents have normal screening exams, but his brother has a similar polyp burden. APC testing is negative. Which gene is most likely mutated?","choices":{"A":"STK11","B":"SMAD4","C":"PTEN","D":"MSH6","E":"MUTYH"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MUTYH-associated polyposis is an autosomal recessive base-excision repair disorder in patients without APC loss. It has fewer than 100 polyps that appear later, with frequent sessile serrated and hyperplastic polyps. STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas with serrated polyps. SMAD4 mutations cause autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis, not recessive adenomatous polyposis. PTEN mutations cause Cowden hamartomas, not adenomas with recessive inheritance. MSH6 is a Lynch syndrome gene, which causes few polyps with dominant inheritance, not about 30 adenomas in siblings. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MUTYH-associated polyposis is also associated with duodenal adenomas and ovarian and bladder cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colon cancer development in MUTYH-associated polyposis is delayed relative to FAP (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 4 — hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas with serrated polyps.","B":"SMAD4 mutations cause autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis, not recessive adenomatous polyposis.","C":"PTEN mutations cause Cowden hamartomas, not adenomas with recessive inheritance.","D":"MSH6 is a Lynch syndrome gene, which causes few polyps with dominant inheritance, not about 30 adenomas in siblings."}},{"id":"sdl33_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with numerous colonic adenomas also has firm bony masses of the skull and a fibrous mass in the abdominal wall. Which variant best describes her disease?","choices":{"A":"Turcot syndrome","B":"Cowden syndrome","C":"Juvenile polyposis","D":"Gardner syndrome","E":"Li Fraumeni syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gardner syndrome is an APC-related FAP variant with multiple adenomas plus osteomas, thyroid and desmoid tumors, and skin cysts. Turcot syndrome adds CNS tumors such as medulloblastoma. Cowden syndrome is a PTEN hamartoma syndrome, not an APC variant with osteomas and desmoids. Juvenile polyposis features hamartomatous polyps, not adenomas with osteomas and desmoids. Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an inherited TP53 mutation with a broad tumor spectrum led by sarcomas, breast cancers, leukemias, and brain tumors, not adenomas with osteomas. Robbins lists Turcot syndrome as an APC variant with multiple adenomas plus medulloblastoma or glioblastoma, whereas osteomas and desmoid tumors define Gardner syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis) (Table 17.10). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Turcot syndrome is FAP or Lynch syndrome plus a malignant CNS tumor such as medulloblastoma or glioma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: attenuated FAP is also caused by APC mutation (Robbins Ch. 17, Table 17.10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 5 — hamartomatous polyposis syndromes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cowden syndrome is a PTEN hamartoma syndrome, not an APC variant with osteomas and desmoids.","C":"Juvenile polyposis features hamartomatous polyps, not adenomas with osteomas and desmoids.","E":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an inherited TP53 mutation with a broad tumor spectrum led by sarcomas, breast cancers, leukemias, and brain tumors, not adenomas with osteomas.","A":"Robbins lists Turcot syndrome as an APC variant with multiple adenomas plus medulloblastoma or glioblastoma, whereas osteomas and desmoid tumors define Gardner syndrome."}},{"id":"sdl33_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has endometrial carcinoma. Her mother had colon cancer at 45, and tumor staining shows loss of a mismatch repair protein. Which mutational mechanism most likely drives her tumors?","choices":{"A":"Chromosomal instability","B":"Telomere attrition crisis","C":"Oncogene amplification","D":"Whole chromosome aneuploidy","E":"Microsatellite instability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In HNPCC, loss of the second mismatch repair allele raises mutation rates up to 1000-fold, mostly in short repeats called microsatellites. Chromosomal instability is the hallmark of the APC/beta catenin pathway. Whole-chromosome aneuploidy reflects chromosomal instability of the APC pathway, not mismatch repair loss. Oncogene amplification, such as HER2 in breast carcinoma, is not the defect produced by mismatch repair loss. Telomere attrition leaves naked chromosome ends that fuse in bridge-fusion-breakage cycles, not the repeat-sequence mutations of mismatch repair loss. Robbins calls chromosomal instability the hallmark of the APC/beta-catenin pathway, whereas mismatch repair deficiency produces microsatellite instability. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer); Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome mismatch repair defects cause subsequent microsatellite instability (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: MSI can target TGFBR2 and BAX, promoting growth and survival of abnormal clones (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Whole-chromosome aneuploidy reflects chromosomal instability of the APC pathway, not mismatch repair loss.","C":"Oncogene amplification, such as HER2 in breast carcinoma, is not the defect produced by mismatch repair loss.","B":"Telomere attrition leaves naked chromosome ends that fuse in bridge-fusion-breakage cycles, not the repeat-sequence mutations of mismatch repair loss.","A":"Robbins calls chromosomal instability the hallmark of the APC/beta-catenin pathway, whereas mismatch repair deficiency produces microsatellite instability."}},{"id":"sdl33_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman with no family history of cancer has a mucinous large bowel adenocarcinoma with peritumoral lymphocytes and mismatch repair deficiency. Which finding best supports a sporadic origin rather than a hereditary syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous differentiation","B":"Advanced patient age","C":"Peritumoral lymphocytes","D":"BRAF activating mutation","E":"Repair protein loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sporadic mismatch repair–deficient cancers often arise through the CpG island hypermethylation pathway, which silences MLH1. The combination of MSI, BRAF mutation, and methylation is the signature of this pathway. Mucinous change, lymphocytes, and right-sided location occur in both. Repair protein loss occurs in both Lynch and sporadic MSI tumors, so it cannot distinguish them. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serrated polyps follow the CIMP pathway, which may silence MLH1 and lead to BRAF mutation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: KRAS and TP53 are typically intact in CIMP cancers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 4 — hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Repair protein loss occurs in both Lynch and sporadic MSI tumors, so it cannot distinguish them.","A":"Mucinous change occurs in both.","C":"Peritumoral lymphocytes do not separate sporadic from Lynch tumors."}},{"id":"sdl33_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman's brother had colon cancer at 38 and her mother had endometrial cancer at 50. Genetic testing confirms a germline mismatch repair mutation. Which other cancer is she at increased risk for?","choices":{"A":"Ovarian carcinoma","B":"Breast carcinoma","C":"Thyroid carcinoma","D":"Lung carcinoma","E":"Cervical carcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HNPCC clusters cancers of the colorectum, endometrium, stomach, ovary, ureters, brain, small bowel, hepatobiliary tract, pancreas, and skin. The objective names endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers. Thyroid cancer is linked to Gardner syndrome, not Lynch syndrome. The cancer sites of Lynch syndrome include the endometrium, ovary, stomach, and urinary tract; breast is not among them. The cancers that cluster in Lynch syndrome include the colorectum, endometrium, stomach, ovary, ureters, brain, small bowel, hepatobiliary tract, pancreas, and skin; lung is not on the list. The cancer sites of Lynch syndrome include the endometrium and ovary, but not the cervix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer); SDL 33 Elentra Objective 3. Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Lynch syndrome mnemonic CEOS stands for colon, endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: identifying germline mismatch repair mutations matters for genetic counseling and second malignancy risk (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Thyroid cancer is linked to Gardner syndrome, not Lynch syndrome.","B":"Breast is not among them.","D":"The cancers that cluster in Lynch syndrome include the colorectum, endometrium, stomach, ovary, ureters, brain, small bowel, hepatobiliary tract, pancreas, and skin; lung is not on the list.","E":"The cancer sites of Lynch syndrome include the endometrium and ovary, but not the cervix."}},{"id":"sdl33_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has fatigue. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference 12 to 16), MCV is 72 fL (reference 80 to 100), and ferritin is 8 ng/mL (reference 15 to 150). She denies visible bleeding. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Bone marrow biopsy","B":"Colonoscopy referral","C":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis","D":"Empiric oral iron","E":"Serum B12 level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Right-sided colon cancers often present with fatigue and weakness from iron deficiency anemia. Iron deficiency anemia in an older male or postmenopausal female is GI cancer until proven otherwise. Bone marrow biopsy is unnecessary when low ferritin already confirms iron deficiency, whose source must be sought by colonoscopy. Hemoglobin electrophoresis evaluates thalassemia, but a low ferritin confirms iron deficiency instead. Empiric oral iron without colonoscopy risks missing GI cancer, which iron deficiency in an older woman implies until proven otherwise. B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia, whereas her microcytic, low-ferritin anemia points to GI blood loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia in males older than 50 and postmenopausal females raises suspicion for colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: proximal colon tumors grow as polypoid masses that rarely obstruct (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Bone marrow biopsy is unnecessary when low ferritin already confirms iron deficiency, whose source must be sought by colonoscopy.","C":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis evaluates thalassemia, but a low ferritin confirms iron deficiency instead.","D":"Empiric oral iron without colonoscopy risks missing GI cancer, which iron deficiency in an older woman implies until proven otherwise.","E":"B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia, whereas her microcytic, low-ferritin anemia points to GI blood loss."}},{"id":"sdl33_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has months of narrowing stool caliber, crampy left lower quadrant pain, and bloating. Imaging shows a short constricting sigmoid mass. Which gross appearance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Sessile serrated plaque","B":"Large pedunculated villous polyp","C":"Diffuse linitis plastica","D":"Flat atrophic mucosa","E":"Annular napkin-ring lesion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Distal colon carcinomas tend to be annular napkin-ring constrictions that narrow the lumen, sometimes to obstruction. Left-sided tumors produce visible bleeding, bowel habit change, and left lower quadrant cramping. A sessile serrated plaque is a right-sided precursor lesion, not a constricting sigmoid mass. Large polypoid masses are typical of the right colon, whereas left-sided cancers are annular and constricting. Flat atrophic mucosa does not produce a constricting mass or narrowed stools. Linitis plastica is the rigid leather bottle wall of diffuse infiltrative gastric cancer, not a short constricting sigmoid mass. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: left-sided colorectal cancer is associated with hematochezia and obstruction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: invasive colon cancer elicits a desmoplastic response responsible for its firm consistency (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A sessile serrated plaque is a right-sided precursor lesion, not a constricting sigmoid mass.","B":"Large polypoid masses are typical of the right colon, whereas left-sided cancers are annular and constricting.","D":"Flat atrophic mucosa does not produce a constricting mass or narrowed stools.","C":"Linitis plastica is the rigid leather bottle wall of diffuse infiltrative gastric cancer, not a short constricting sigmoid mass."}},{"id":"sdl33_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":5,"stem":"A 55-year-old man takes daily low-dose aspirin after a myocardial infarction and asks whether it affects his colon cancer risk. Which mechanism most likely explains a protective effect?","choices":{"A":"Lipoxygenase inhibition","B":"Thromboxane receptor blockade","C":"Cyclooxygenase 2 inhibition","D":"Platelet P2Y12 blockade","E":"Phospholipase A2 inhibition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aspirin and other NSAIDs are protective, likely through inhibition of COX-2, which is highly expressed in 90% of colorectal carcinomas and 40% to 90% of adenomas. COX-2 drives prostaglandin E2, which promotes epithelial proliferation. Aspirin and NSAIDs are thought to protect against colon cancer by inhibiting COX-2, which is overexpressed in most colorectal carcinomas and drives PGE2-mediated proliferation; lipoxygenase is not the target. Aspirin lowers thromboxane A2 synthesis by irreversibly acetylating cyclooxygenase rather than blocking the thromboxane receptor. Irreversible P2Y12 ADP receptor blockade is the mechanism of clopidogrel, prasugrel, and ticlopidine, not aspirin. Phospholipase A2 is inhibited by corticosteroids through annexins (lipocortins), not by aspirin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: overexpression of COX-2 has been linked to colorectal cancer, and NSAIDs may be chemopreventive (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: some NSAIDs cause polyp regression in FAP patients whose rectum was left in place (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — NSAIDs and H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Aspirin and NSAIDs are thought to protect against colon cancer by inhibiting COX-2, which is overexpressed in most colorectal carcinomas and drives PGE2-mediated proliferation; lipoxygenase is not the target.","B":"Aspirin lowers thromboxane A2 synthesis by irreversibly acetylating cyclooxygenase rather than blocking the thromboxane receptor.","D":"Irreversible P2Y12 ADP receptor blockade is the mechanism of clopidogrel, prasugrel, and ticlopidine, not aspirin.","E":"Phospholipase A2 is inhibited by corticosteroids through annexins (lipocortins), not by aspirin."}},{"id":"sdl33_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has periumbilical pain that moved to the right lower quadrant, then nausea and low-grade fever. His resected appendix is examined. Which histologic finding is required for the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis propria neutrophils","B":"Mucosal neutrophils with ulcers","C":"Lymphoid follicle hyperplasia","D":"Submucosal eosinophil clusters","E":"Granulomas in lamina propria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mucosal neutrophils and focal superficial ulceration are not specific. Diagnosis of acute appendicitis requires neutrophilic infiltration of the muscularis propria. Lymphoid hyperplasia can obstruct the appendix in young patients, but it is a cause rather than the diagnostic finding. Granulomas in the lamina propria suggest Crohn disease or infection, not acute appendicitis. Submucosal eosinophils are not the criterion. Robbins notes mucosal neutrophils and focal superficial ulceration are often present but are not specific markers of acute appendicitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendicitis can be due to fecalith obstruction in adults or lymphoid hyperplasia in children (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: complications of appendicitis include pylephlebitis, portal venous thrombosis, abscess, and bacteremia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — bacterial enterocolitis, including Yersinia mimicking appendicitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Lymphoid hyperplasia can obstruct the appendix in young patients, but it is a cause rather than the diagnostic finding.","E":"Granulomas in the lamina propria suggest Crohn disease or infection, not acute appendicitis.","D":"Submucosal eosinophils are not the criterion.","B":"Robbins notes mucosal neutrophils and focal superficial ulceration are often present but are not specific markers of acute appendicitis."}},{"id":"sdl33_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with chronic constipation and straining notices bright red blood on toilet tissue. Anoscopy shows dilated anal venous cushions. Which condition must still be excluded?","choices":{"A":"Anal fistula","B":"Pilonidal cyst","C":"Colon carcinoma","D":"Perianal abscess","E":"Rectal prolapse"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hemorrhoids present with pain and bright red blood on toilet tissue, but colon cancer can coexist and must be excluded. Straining at defecation and venous stasis of pregnancy are the main predisposing influences. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hemorrhoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids receive visceral innervation and are not painful (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Pectinate line). COMLEX: hemorrhoidal bleeding can be treated by sclerotherapy, rubber band ligation, or infrared coagulation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has progressive abdominal distention. Surgery finds tenacious, semisolid mucin filling the peritoneal cavity and a ruptured, mucin-distended appendix with pushing tumor margins. Which primary lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Appendiceal carcinoid tumor","B":"Appendiceal mucinous neoplasm","C":"Ovarian serous carcinoma","D":"Peritoneal mesothelioma","E":"Gastric signet ring cell carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Appendiceal mucinous neoplasms occur in adults, often in the sixth decade, with pushing margins and extracellular mucin that can rupture the appendix and fill the peritoneum as pseudomyxoma peritonei. An appendiceal carcinoid is a small solid tip mass that rarely spreads. Ovarian serous carcinoma, peritoneal mesothelioma, and gastric signet-ring carcinoma are not a ruptured mucin-distended appendix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritoneal spread of appendiceal mucinous neoplasms can produce pseudomyxoma peritonei (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: GNAS, encoding Gαs, is frequently mutated in appendiceal mucinous neoplasms and is thought to drive excess mucin production (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — mucinous pancreatic cystic neoplasms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An appendiceal carcinoid is a small solid tip mass that rarely spreads, not a mucin-distended ruptured appendix.","E":"Gastric signet-ring carcinoma is not a ruptured mucin-distended appendix.","C":"Ovarian serous carcinoma is not a ruptured mucin-distended appendix.","D":"Peritoneal mesothelioma is not the mucin-producing source of this picture."}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has a 2.5 cm pedunculated sigmoid polyp removed whole by snare. Pathology shows a tubulovillous adenoma with a focus of malignant glands that have breached the muscularis mucosae and invaded the submucosa of the stalk. The resection margin is clear. Why does this finding change his management compared with carcinoma confined to the mucosa?","choices":{"A":"The stalk lacks blood vessels that tumor could reach","B":"Submucosal invasion means the polyp was a hamartoma","C":"Mucosal carcinoma spreads by lymphatics in the lamina propria","D":"Submucosa has lymphatics, so nodal spread is now possible","E":"Stalk invasion means the adenoma had no dysplasia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The colonic mucosa lacks functional lymphatic channels, so intramucosal carcinoma has little or no metastatic potential, but once malignant cells cross the muscularis mucosae into the submucosal stalk they are invasive adenocarcinoma with access to lymphatics, so nodal metastasis becomes possible and further staging is needed. The stalk lacks blood vessels is false, because the submucosa of the stalk carries both vessels and lymphatics. Submucosal invasion means the polyp was a hamartoma is wrong, since the lesion is a dysplastic adenoma that progressed to carcinoma. Mucosal carcinoma spreads by lymphatics in the lamina propria inverts the key fact that colonic mucosa has no functional lymphatics. Stalk invasion means the adenoma had no dysplasia is incorrect, because all adenomas are defined by epithelial dysplasia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas; Intramucosal carcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intramucosal carcinoma rarely metastasizes because colonic mucosa lacks lymphatics, whereas invasion into the submucosal stalk is invasive cancer (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: a malignant polyp with submucosal invasion needs margin review and staging rather than polypectomy follow-up.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The stalk lacks blood vessels is false,","B":"Submucosal invasion means the polyp was a","C":"Mucosal carcinoma spreads by lymphatics in the","E":"Stalk invasion means the adenoma had no"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl whose father had a colectomy at 30 has hundreds of small adenomas carpeting her colon and rectum. Genetic testing shows a germline truncating mutation in a tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q. Immunostaining of her adenoma epithelium is performed. Which finding is most expected in the adenoma cells?","choices":{"A":"Loss of mismatch repair protein staining","B":"Nuclear accumulation of beta-catenin","C":"Overexpression of membrane E-cadherin","D":"Loss of SMAD4 from stromal cells","E":"Absent MLH1 from promoter methylation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hundreds of adenomas in a teenager with an affected parent and a germline 5q mutation is familial adenomatous polyposis; loss of the second APC allele in each adenoma prevents beta-catenin destruction, so beta-catenin accumulates and moves to the nucleus to drive Wnt target genes such as MYC and cyclin D1. Loss of mismatch repair protein staining is the signature of Lynch syndrome tumors, not APC-driven adenomas. Overexpression of membrane E-cadherin is not the APC defect; the hallmark is free beta-catenin entering the nucleus. Loss of SMAD4 is the lesion of juvenile polyposis, a hamartomatous syndrome. Absent MLH1 from promoter methylation is the mechanism of sporadic microsatellite-unstable cancers, not FAP. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis; APC/beta-catenin pathway); First Aid 2025 GI (FAP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: In FAP, germline APC loss plus a second hit lets beta-catenin escape degradation and activate Wnt targets in the nucleus (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: pancolonic adenomas in a teenager call for prophylactic colectomy and genetic counseling of relatives.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of mismatch repair protein staining is","C":"Overexpression of membrane E-cadherin is not the","D":"Loss of SMAD4 is the lesion of","E":"Absent MLH1 from promoter methylation is the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has a right-sided colon adenocarcinoma. His mother had endometrial cancer at 48. He carries a germline MSH2 mutation. Immunohistochemistry shows absent MSH2 staining in tumor cells, but normal colonic mucosa from the same specimen stains normally for MSH2. Which event best explains the difference between the two tissues?","choices":{"A":"Gain of extra copies of the KRAS oncogene","B":"Epigenetic imprinting of the paternal MSH2 copy","C":"Chromosome 5q loss in normal colonic crypts","D":"Silencing of telomerase in tumor epithelium","E":"Loss of the remaining normal MSH2 allele"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A right-sided colon cancer in a young man with a germline mismatch repair mutation and a relative with endometrial cancer is Lynch syndrome; every cell carries one mutant MSH2 allele, but normal mucosa still makes protein from the normal allele, and the tumor lost staining because that second allele was inactivated by mutation or epigenetic silencing. Gain of extra copies of the KRAS oncogene would not remove MSH2 protein from tumor cells. Epigenetic imprinting of the paternal MSH2 copy is not how Lynch tumors lose function, and imprinting would affect normal mucosa equally. Chromosome 5q loss relates to APC, and loss in normal crypts would not explain absent tumor MSH2 staining. Silencing of telomerase in tumor epithelium is opposite to tumor biology and unrelated to mismatch repair loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome patients inherit one mutant mismatch repair allele; tumors arise after the second copy is lost, producing microsatellite instability (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: young right-sided colon cancer with endometrial cancer in the family warrants mismatch repair testing and cascade screening.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gain of extra copies of the KRAS","B":"Epigenetic imprinting of the paternal MSH2 copy","C":"Chromosome 5q loss relates to APC, and","D":"Silencing of telomerase in tumor epithelium is"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has 3 weeks of fever and fatigue and a new diastolic murmur. Three blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in chains that are nonhemolytic and identified as a gut-colonizing streptococcus formerly named for cattle. Echocardiography shows an aortic valve vegetation. Besides antibiotics, which evaluation is most important?","choices":{"A":"Dental panoramic radiography","B":"Urine culture and renal ultrasound","C":"Colonoscopy of the entire colon","D":"Transvaginal ultrasound of the pelvis","E":"Upper endoscopy for peptic ulcer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Endocarditis from Streptococcus gallolyticus, formerly S bovis, is associated with an increased incidence of colon cancer, so the patient needs colonoscopy to look for an occult colonic adenocarcinoma. Dental panoramic radiography fits viridans streptococcal endocarditis after dental procedures, not a gut-colonizing streptococcus. Urine culture and renal ultrasound look for a genitourinary source, which is more relevant to enterococcal infection. Transvaginal ultrasound of the pelvis does not apply to this man and is not linked to this organism. Upper endoscopy for peptic ulcer does not address the colonic neoplasm that this bacteremia signals. Sources: First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Streptococcus gallolyticus) and GI (colorectal cancer presentation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: S gallolyticus bacteremia or endocarditis is associated with colorectal cancer (First Aid, Streptococcus gallolyticus). COMLEX: after treating the endocarditis, arrange colonoscopy even in a patient without GI symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dental panoramic radiography fits viridans streptococcal endocarditis","B":"Urine culture and renal ultrasound look for","D":"Transvaginal ultrasound of the pelvis does not","E":"Upper endoscopy for peptic ulcer does not"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old man had a perforated appendix removed 1 week ago. He now has spiking fevers, rigors, and right upper quadrant tenderness. WBC is 21,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). CT shows several small abscesses scattered within the right lobe of the liver. By which route did infection most likely reach his liver?","choices":{"A":"Ascent from the duodenum up the bile ducts","B":"Through mesenteric veins into the portal vein","C":"Lymphatic spread through the thoracic duct","D":"Arterial seeding from the hepatic artery","E":"Direct extension through the subphrenic space"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Blood from the appendix drains through tributaries of the superior mesenteric vein into the portal vein, so bacteria and infected thrombus from a perforated appendix travel straight to the liver, which is why Robbins lists pylephlebitis, portal venous thrombosis, and liver abscess as complications of appendicitis. Ascent from the duodenum up the bile ducts is ascending cholangitis, which follows biliary obstruction rather than appendiceal perforation. Lymphatic spread through the thoracic duct empties into the systemic veins and does not carry bowel bacteria directly to the liver. Arterial seeding from the hepatic artery requires systemic bacteremia first, whereas the portal veins deliver appendiceal bacteria to the liver directly. Direct extension through the subphrenic space would form a collection above the liver rather than abscesses scattered within it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Acute appendicitis complications); course master PDF (hepatic portal vein formation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Appendicitis complications include pylephlebitis, portal vein thrombosis, liver abscess, and bacteremia, reflecting portal venous drainage of the bowel (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: fever and RUQ pain after perforated appendicitis call for imaging of the portal vein and liver.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ascent from the duodenum up the bile ducts is","C":"Lymphatic spread through the thoracic duct empties","D":"Arterial seeding from the hepatic artery requires","E":"Direct extension through the subphrenic space would"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a sigmoid adenocarcinoma resected. The pathology report lists a 5 cm moderately differentiated tumor with mucin production, invasion through the muscularis propria into pericolic fat, and metastatic tumor in 4 of 18 regional lymph nodes. No distant metastases are found. Which reported feature most worsens his prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Tumor diameter of 5 centimeters","B":"Moderate histologic differentiation","C":"Production of extracellular mucin","D":"Metastasis to regional lymph nodes","E":"Tumor location in the sigmoid colon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For colorectal adenocarcinoma the two most important prognostic factors are depth of invasion and the presence of lymph node metastases, so among the reported features his regional nodal metastases most worsen his outlook. Tumor diameter of 5 centimeters matters less than depth of invasion and nodal status in staging. Moderate histologic differentiation has less prognostic weight than invasion depth and nodal spread. Production of extracellular mucin is a histologic feature that is not one of the two leading prognostic factors. Tumor location in the sigmoid colon does not outweigh nodal metastasis in predicting survival. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Colonic adenocarcinoma, prognosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Depth of invasion and lymph node metastasis are the two most important prognostic factors in colorectal adenocarcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: node-positive colon cancer is stage III and is an indication for adjuvant chemotherapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Tumor diameter of 5 centimeters matters less","B":"Moderate histologic differentiation has less prognostic weight","C":"Production of extracellular mucin is a histologic","E":"Tumor location in the sigmoid colon does"}},{"id":"grid_sdl33_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":4,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has 2 months of fatigue. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) with microcytic indices. Colonoscopy shows a mass, and biopsy shows a mucinous adenocarcinoma with signet-ring cells and dense intraepithelial lymphocytes. Her mother had endometrial cancer at age 46, and a maternal uncle had colon cancer at age 44. She has no history of inflammatory bowel disease. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the disorder underlying this patient's cancer?","grid":{"headers":["Inheritance","Microsatellite status","Usual location"]},"choices":{"A":["Autosomal dominant","MSI-high","Proximal colon"],"B":["Autosomal dominant","Stable","Pancolonic"],"C":["Sporadic","MSI-high","Proximal colon"],"D":["Sporadic","Stable","Distal colon"],"E":["Acquired (colitis)","Stable","Pancolonic"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (autosomal dominant, MSI-high, proximal colon) is correct. Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (Lynch syndrome) is autosomal dominant and caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in DNA mismatch repair genes, most often MSH2 or MLH1; loss of the second allele causes microsatellite instability. The notes place these cancers predominantly in the proximal (right) colon at young ages, describe mucinous and signet-ring differentiation with intraepithelial lymphocytes, and list endometrial cancer as the most common extraintestinal malignancy in women. Choice B (autosomal dominant, stable, pancolonic) is familial adenomatous polyposis, an APC germline disorder with at least 100 adenomas throughout the colon beginning in adolescence and progressing through the chromosomally unstable APC/beta-catenin pathway. Choice C (sporadic, MSI-high, proximal colon) is the sporadic CIMP (serrated) pathway, in which CpG island hypermethylation silences MLH1 and BRAF is activated, arising from sessile serrated lesions without a family pattern. Choice D (sporadic, stable, distal colon) is sporadic adenocarcinoma through the classic APC, KRAS, SMAD4, TP53 sequence with chromosomal instability, which accounts for about 80% of sporadic cases. Choice E (acquired from colitis, stable, pancolonic) is colitis-associated cancer arising in long-standing ulcerative pancolitis, an acquired risk factor. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 33, sections 2–4: FAP, Lynch syndrome, colorectal cancer pathways and risk factors); Robbins Ch. 17 (colonic neoplasia). The proximal location of CIMP tumors, the distal predominance of CIN-pathway sporadic cancers, and the microsatellite-stable, pancolonic profile of colitis-associated cancer are from Robbins and are not stated in the notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome = germline mismatch repair defect (MLH1, MSH2) → MSI-high, right-sided colon cancer before 50, plus endometrial and ovarian cancer (Exam 3 notes, SDL 33). COMLEX: sporadic MSI-high cancer from MLH1 promoter hypermethylation with BRAF mutation is the main look-alike.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — FAP genetics and pancolonic polyposis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nuclear change is part of the epithelial dysplasia in a colorectal adenoma?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear pseudostratification","B":"A normal basal nucleus with abundant mucin in every cell","C":"Caseating necrosis in the nucleus","D":"Viral inclusions that fill the nucleus","E":"Loss of the nucleus in a mature squamous cell"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Adenomatous dysplasia shows nuclear hyperchromasia, elongation, and pseudostratification, with fewer goblet cells. A quiet basal nucleus and retained mucin describe normal colonocytes, and dysplasia reduces goblet cells. Caseating necrosis is a granulomatous pattern, not the nuclear change of adenoma. Viral nuclear inclusions are not the dysplasia of an adenoma. Loss of the nucleus in a squamous cell is not colonic adenomatous dysplasia.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A quiet basal nucleus and retained mucin describe normal colonocytes, and dysplasia reduces goblet cells","C":"Caseating necrosis is a granulomatous pattern, not the nuclear change of adenoma","D":"Viral nuclear inclusions are not the dysplasia of an adenoma","E":"Loss of the nucleus in a squamous cell is not colonic adenomatous dysplasia"}},{"id":"sdl33_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has fatigue. A 4 cm sessile sigmoid polyp is removed and shows villous architecture with dysplasia but no invasion through the muscularis mucosae. He takes no anticoagulant. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume is 74 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and ferritin is 9 ng/mL (reference range: 24–336 ng/mL). Which complication of the polyp itself best explains the anemia?","choices":{"A":"Metastasis to the brain","B":"A fistula into the bladder","C":"Chronic occult bleeding from the polyp surface","D":"Hemolysis from a vitamin E deficiency","E":"Hookworm infection of the polyp"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Large adenomas can ooze enough occult blood to cause iron deficiency, even before they invade. A noninvasive adenoma confined to the mucosa has essentially no metastatic potential. A bladder fistula is a complication of diverticulitis, not of a mucosal adenoma. Vitamin E deficiency can shorten red-cell survival, but this low ferritin and low mean corpuscular volume are iron loss. Hookworms cause iron deficiency from the small-bowel mucosa, not from living in a polyp.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A noninvasive adenoma confined to the mucosa has essentially no metastatic potential","B":"A bladder fistula is a complication of diverticulitis, not of a mucosal adenoma","D":"Vitamin E deficiency can shorten red-cell survival, but this low ferritin and low mean corpuscular volume are iron loss","E":"Hookworms cause iron deficiency from the small-bowel mucosa, not from living in a polyp"}},{"id":"sdl33_q33","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has hundreds of colorectal adenomas, and his father had a colectomy at 28. He now has headaches and ataxia. There is no jaw mass and no skin cysts. Hemoglobin is 13.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). MRI shows a mass in the cerebellum. Which variant pairs this polyposis with a brain tumor?","choices":{"A":"Gardner syndrome","B":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","C":"Juvenile polyposis","D":"Lynch syndrome with no excess polyps","E":"Turcot syndrome"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Turcot syndrome is familial adenomatous polyposis plus a central nervous system tumor such as medulloblastoma or glioblastoma. Gardner syndrome adds osteomas, desmoids, skin cysts, and dental abnormalities, not a cerebellar tumor. Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is hamartomas and mucocutaneous macules, with a different gene. Juvenile polyposis is hamartomatous polyps from SMAD4 or BMPR1A, not carpeting adenomas with a brain tumor. Lynch syndrome is mismatch-repair cancer with few polyps, not hundreds of adenomas.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gardner syndrome adds osteomas, desmoids, skin cysts, and dental abnormalities, not a cerebellar tumor","B":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is hamartomas and mucocutaneous macules, with a different gene","C":"Juvenile polyposis is hamartomatous polyps from SMAD4 or BMPR1A, not carpeting adenomas with a brain tumor","D":"Lynch syndrome is mismatch-repair cancer with few polyps, not hundreds of adenomas"}},{"id":"sdl33_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"A carcinoma in the cecum usually grows in which shape?","choices":{"A":"A short napkin-ring stricture","B":"An exophytic polypoid mass","C":"A flat ulcer confined to the anus","D":"A linitis plastica of the whole stomach","E":"A pedunculated worm ball"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Right-sided colon cancers grow as exophytic polypoid masses in a wide lumen and often do not obstruct early. A napkin-ring stricture is the usual shape on the left, where the lumen is narrower. A flat anal ulcer is not the growth pattern of a cecal adenocarcinoma. Linitis plastica is a diffuse gastric carcinoma, not a cecal mass. A worm ball is a complication of heavy Ascaris infection, not a carcinoma.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A napkin-ring stricture is the usual shape on the left, where the lumen is narrower","C":"A flat anal ulcer is not the growth pattern of a cecal adenocarcinoma","D":"Linitis plastica is a diffuse gastric carcinoma, not a cecal mass","E":"A worm ball is a complication of heavy Ascaris infection, not a carcinoma"}},{"id":"sdl33_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a cancer of the ascending colon. Her mother had endometrial cancer at 48, and a brother had a ureteral cancer. The tumor is mucinous, with many lymphocytes in the epithelium, and testing shows high microsatellite instability. Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and mean corpuscular volume is 76 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Which pathway produced this cancer?","choices":{"A":"Defective DNA mismatch repair","B":"Early loss of TP53 as the first event, with stable microsatellites","C":"A germline APC mutation with thousands of adenomas","D":"Only a dietary vitamin C deficiency","E":"Cholera toxin locking a G protein on"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The microsatellite-instability pathway is driven by loss of DNA mismatch repair, most often MSH2 or MLH1. TP53 loss is a late step of the chromosomal-instability pathway, and this tumor is microsatellite-unstable. A germline APC mutation with thousands of adenomas is familial adenomatous polyposis, not a mismatch-repair cancer. Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy and does not generate microsatellite instability. Cholera toxin drives secretory diarrhea and does not cause this colon cancer.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"TP53 loss is a late step of the chromosomal-instability pathway, and this tumor is microsatellite-unstable","C":"A germline APC mutation with thousands of adenomas is familial adenomatous polyposis, not a mismatch-repair cancer","D":"Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy and does not generate microsatellite instability","E":"Cholera toxin drives secretory diarrhea and does not cause this colon cancer"}}],"34":[{"id":"sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein compartment is depleted most severely in kwashiorkor?","choices":{"A":"Somatic protein compartment","B":"Bone mineral compartment","C":"Plasma lipid compartment","D":"Intracellular water compartment","E":"Visceral protein compartment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kwashiorkor results when protein deprivation is relatively greater than the reduction in total calories, and it causes marked loss of the visceral protein compartment, chiefly the liver, with resulting hypoalbuminemia. Marasmus predominantly depletes the somatic compartment of skeletal muscle. Bone mineral is not the compartment targeted in kwashiorkor, which depletes visceral protein, chiefly the liver. Plasma lipid is not a protein compartment. Intracellular water is not the depleted compartment. Robbins notes the somatic protein compartment is depleted more severely in marasmus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Protein Energy Malnutrition). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the kwashiorkor mnemonic MEALS lists malnutrition, edema, anemia, liver malfunction, and skin lesions (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: serum albumin and transferrin reflect the visceral protein compartment (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Bone mineral is not the compartment targeted in kwashiorkor, which depletes visceral protein, chiefly the liver.","C":"Plasma lipid is not a protein compartment.","D":"Intracellular water is not the depleted compartment.","A":"Robbins notes the somatic protein compartment is depleted more severely in marasmus."}},{"id":"sdl34_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":5,"stem":"Marasmus is defined when a child's weight falls to about what percentage of normal for sex, height, and age?","choices":{"A":"About 30 percent","B":"About 60 percent","C":"About 45 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins defines marasmus as weight falling to about 60% of normal for sex, height, and age, with growth retardation and loss of muscle and subcutaneous fat. In kwashiorkor, weight is typically 60% to 80% of normal, and the true loss is masked by edema. About 30 percent is far below the threshold. About 45 percent is below the defining threshold of about 60% of normal weight. About 75 percent falls within the kwashiorkor range of 60% to 80%, not the marasmus threshold. About 90 percent is near normal weight and does not define marasmus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Marasmus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of edema distinguishes marasmus from kwashiorkor (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: in marasmus the head appears large relative to the emaciated body (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"About 30 percent is far below the threshold.","C":"About 45 percent is below the defining threshold of about 60% of normal weight.","D":"About 75 percent falls within the kwashiorkor range of 60% to 80%, not the marasmus threshold.","E":"About 90 percent is near normal weight and does not define marasmus."}},{"id":"sdl34_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":5,"stem":"The enlarged fatty liver in kwashiorkor results mainly from which process?","choices":{"A":"Increased delivery of dietary fat","B":"Hepatic venous congestion","C":"Hepatocyte iron accumulation","D":"Reduced apolipoprotein synthesis","E":"Excess hepatic glycogen storage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In kwashiorkor the liver is enlarged and fatty because synthesis of the carrier protein components of lipoproteins is reduced, so triglyceride is not exported efficiently. The diet is typically carbohydrate based rather than fat rich. Iron accumulation is not the cause; the fatty liver comes from failure to make lipoprotein carrier proteins. Excess glycogen is not the problem; triglyceride accumulates because apolipoprotein synthesis falls. Hepatic venous congestion produces centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis, the mottled nutmeg liver, not a diffusely fatty liver. Robbins attributes the enlarged fatty liver of kwashiorkor to reduced synthesis of the carrier protein components of lipoproteins, not increased dietary fat delivery. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid attributes the fatty change in kwashiorkor to decreased apolipoprotein synthesis (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: superimposed fatty change can occur in marasmus as well, but it is characteristic of kwashiorkor (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Iron accumulation is not the cause; the fatty liver comes from failure to make lipoprotein carrier proteins.","E":"Excess glycogen is not the problem.","B":"Hepatic venous congestion produces centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis, the mottled nutmeg liver, not a diffusely fatty liver.","A":"Robbins attributes the enlarged fatty liver of kwashiorkor to reduced synthesis of the carrier protein components of lipoproteins, not increased dietary fat delivery."}},{"id":"sdl34_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":5,"stem":"Acute vitamin A toxicity produces headache, vomiting, and blurred vision that can mimic which condition?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial meningitis","B":"Pseudotumor cerebri","C":"Migraine with aura","D":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","E":"Hypertensive encephalopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute toxicity from a large dose of vitamin A causes headache, vomiting, and blurred vision that may be confused with a brain tumor, known as pseudotumor cerebri. Bacterial meningitis is an infection. Migraine is a primary headache, not raised intracranial pressure. Subarachnoid hemorrhage is a sudden worst headache with blood in the CSF. Hypertensive encephalopathy needs severe hypertension with end-organ damage. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin A, Toxicity). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin A excess causes idiopathic intracranial hypertension, and isotretinoin is teratogenic (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin A). COMLEX: retinoic acid stimulates osteoclast production and activity, so chronic excess raises fracture risk (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Migraine is a primary headache: unilateral, pulsating, 4-72 hours, with nausea and photophobia and sometimes an aura; it is not a state of raised intracranial pressure.","D":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage is a sudden \"worst headache of my life\" from a ruptured aneurysm or AVM, with bloody or xanthochromic CSF; vitamin A excess instead raises ICP without bleeding.","E":"Hypertensive encephalopathy needs severe hypertension (>=180/120 mm Hg) with acute end-organ damage; vitamin A toxicity raises ICP without hypertension.","A":"Bacterial meningitis is an infectious cause of headache, not isolated raised intracranial pressure from vitamin A."}},{"id":"sdl34_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Vitamin K is required for hepatic carboxylation of which group of clotting factors?","choices":{"A":"Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII","B":"Factors VIII, IX, XI, and XII","C":"Factors II, VII, IX, and X","D":"Factors II, V, VIII, and X","E":"Factors III, VII, XI, and XIII"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vitamin K is a cofactor for hepatic carboxylation of procoagulant factors II, VII, IX, and X and of proteins C and S. Deficiency produces a bleeding diathesis, especially in fat malabsorption and after broad antibiotic courses. Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; the dependent factors are II, VII, IX, and X. Factors VIII, XI, and XII are not vitamin K dependent; only IX of this set is. Factors V and VIII are not vitamin K dependent, so this set is wrong despite including II and X. Factors III, XI, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; only VII of this set is. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin K). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase and lowers functional factor levels (First Aid Hematology, Warfarin). COMLEX: vitamin K is fat soluble, so biliary and pancreatic disease reduce its absorption (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent.","B":"Factors VIII, XI, and XII are not vitamin K dependent; only IX of this set is.","D":"Factors V and VIII are not vitamin K dependent, so this set is wrong despite including II and X.","E":"Factors III, XI, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; only VII of this set is."}},{"id":"sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":5,"stem":"Which factor stimulates renal 1-alpha hydroxylase to increase active vitamin D production?","choices":{"A":"Parathyroid hormone","B":"Fibroblast growth factor 23","C":"Serum hypercalcemia","D":"Serum hyperphosphatemia","E":"Calcitriol excess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After hepatic 25-hydroxylation, the kidney converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to the active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Renal 1-alpha hydroxylase is stimulated by PTH and hypophosphatemia and inhibited by 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D and FGF23. Hypocalcemia acts indirectly by raising PTH. Hypercalcemia suppresses PTH and therefore 1-alpha hydroxylase; it is hypocalcemia that raises PTH. Hyperphosphatemia does not stimulate the enzyme; hypophosphatemia does. FGF23, made by osteoblasts and osteocytes, inhibits renal 1-alpha hydroxylase expression rather than stimulating it. 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D itself inhibits 1-alpha hydroxylase expression, so excess calcitriol shuts the enzyme down. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Metabolism). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic kidney disease reduces renal activation of vitamin D (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin D). COMLEX: 25-hydroxyvitamin D is the major circulating form and the usual marker of vitamin D status (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Hypercalcemia suppresses PTH and therefore 1-alpha hydroxylase; it is hypocalcemia that raises PTH.","D":"Hyperphosphatemia does not stimulate the enzyme; hypophosphatemia does.","B":"FGF23, made by osteoblasts and osteocytes, inhibits renal 1-alpha hydroxylase expression rather than stimulating it.","E":"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D itself inhibits 1-alpha hydroxylase expression, so excess calcitriol shuts the enzyme down."}},{"id":"sdl34_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Riboflavin functions in the body after conversion to which coenzyme?","choices":{"A":"Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide","B":"Thiamine pyrophosphate","C":"Pyridoxal phosphate","D":"Flavin adenine dinucleotide","E":"Tetrahydrofolic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Riboflavin is converted to flavin mononucleotide and flavin adenine dinucleotide, which serve as cofactors for many enzymes in intermediary metabolism. Deficiency causes cheilosis, stomatitis, glossitis, dermatitis, and corneal vascularization. NAD is the coenzyme derived from niacin, not riboflavin. Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of vitamin B1, not riboflavin. Pyridoxal phosphate is the active form of vitamin B6, not riboflavin. Tetrahydrofolic acid is derived from folate, not riboflavin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.9, Vitamins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: riboflavin deficiency causes cheilosis and corneal vascularization, the two Cs of B2 (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B2). COMLEX: niacin forms the nicotinamide coenzymes, while thiamine forms the pyrophosphate (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"NAD is the coenzyme derived from niacin, not riboflavin.","B":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of vitamin B1, not riboflavin.","C":"Pyridoxal phosphate is the active form of vitamin B6, not riboflavin.","E":"Tetrahydrofolic acid is derived from folate, not riboflavin."}},{"id":"sdl34_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Copper is an essential component of which enzyme needed for collagen cross-linking?","choices":{"A":"Lysyl oxidase","B":"Glutathione peroxidase","C":"Carbonic anhydrase","D":"Xanthine oxidase","E":"Alcohol dehydrogenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Copper is a component of cytochrome c oxidase, dopamine beta hydroxylase, tyrosinase, lysyl oxidase, and other enzymes. Deficiency causes muscle weakness, neurologic defects, and abnormal collagen cross-linking, and it may follow excess zinc intake. Glutathione peroxidase is a selenium-containing antioxidant enzyme, not the copper enzyme of collagen cross-linking. Carbonic anhydrase is a zinc enzyme, not copper-dependent lysyl oxidase. Xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid and is the target of allopurinol, not a collagen cross-linking enzyme. Alcohol dehydrogenase oxidizes ethanol to acetaldehyde, not a collagen cross-linking enzyme. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menkes disease impairs copper transport and lysyl oxidase activity (First Aid Biochemistry, Menkes disease). COMLEX: glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not copper (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Glutathione peroxidase is a selenium-containing antioxidant enzyme, not the copper enzyme of collagen cross-linking.","C":"Carbonic anhydrase is a zinc enzyme, not copper-dependent lysyl oxidase.","D":"Xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid and is the target of allopurinol, not a collagen cross-linking enzyme.","E":"Alcohol dehydrogenase oxidizes ethanol to acetaldehyde, not a collagen cross-linking enzyme."}},{"id":"sdl34_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Selenium deficiency is the cause of which endemic cardiomyopathy?","choices":{"A":"Wet beriberi","B":"Chagas disease","C":"Keshan disease","D":"Kawasaki disease","E":"Loeffler endocarditis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Selenium is a component of glutathione peroxidase, an antioxidant enzyme. Deficiency causes myopathy and congestive cardiomyopathy known as Keshan disease, described in regions with selenium-poor soil. Wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from thiamine deficiency, not selenium deficiency. Chagas disease is a myocarditis caused by Trypanosoma cruzi parasitizing myofibers, not a selenium deficiency. Kawasaki disease is an arteritis of young children that damages coronary arteries, not a nutritional cardiomyopathy. Loeffler endomyocarditis is endomyocardial fibrosis driven by eosinophil products in hypereosinophilia, not selenium deficiency. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glutathione peroxidase is a free radical scavenging enzyme (First Aid Pathology, Free radical injury). COMLEX: the antioxidant role of selenium overlaps functionally with vitamin E (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from thiamine deficiency, not selenium deficiency.","B":"Chagas disease is a myocarditis caused by Trypanosoma cruzi parasitizing myofibers, not a selenium deficiency.","D":"Kawasaki disease is an arteritis of young children that damages coronary arteries, not a nutritional cardiomyopathy.","E":"Loeffler endomyocarditis is endomyocardial fibrosis driven by eosinophil products in hypereosinophilia, not selenium deficiency."}},{"id":"sdl34_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"The central processing system that integrates peripheral signals of energy balance lies mainly in which hypothalamic nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Supraoptic nucleus","B":"Suprachiasmatic nucleus","C":"Preoptic nucleus","D":"Arcuate nucleus","E":"Mammillary nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes energy balance as an afferent system, a central processing system in the hypothalamus, and an efferent system. Within the arcuate nucleus, POMC and CART neurons promote catabolism and NPY and AgRP neurons promote anabolism. Supraoptic nuclei control renal water excretion by releasing ADH from the posterior pituitary, not energy balance. The suprachiasmatic nucleus above the optic chiasm is the circadian master clock, not the energy-balance integrator. The preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus regulates body temperature, not energy stores. The mammillary bodies partly control feeding reflexes such as lip licking and swallowing, whereas arcuate neurons integrate energy-store signals. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Regulation of Energy Balance). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a ventromedial hypothalamic lesion causes hyperphagia and obesity (First Aid Neurology, Hypothalamus). COMLEX: arcuate NPY and AgRP neurons also inhibit neighboring POMC neurons (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Supraoptic nuclei control renal water excretion by releasing ADH from the posterior pituitary, not energy balance.","B":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus above the optic chiasm is the circadian master clock, not the energy-balance integrator.","C":"The preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus regulates body temperature, not energy stores.","E":"The mammillary bodies partly control feeding reflexes such as lip licking and swallowing, whereas arcuate neurons integrate energy-store signals."}},{"id":"sdl34_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"Peptide YY and glucagon-like peptide 1 are secreted by which intestinal cell type?","choices":{"A":"K cells","B":"I cells","C":"S cells","D":"G cells","E":"L cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Peptide YY and GLP-1 are secreted by L cells in the distal ileum and colon. GIP comes from K cells in the duodenum and jejunum. These gut signals act on the hypothalamus and pancreas after a meal. I cells of the duodenum and jejunum secrete cholecystokinin, not peptide YY or GLP-1. S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin, not peptide YY or GLP-1. G cells of the stomach secrete gastrin, not peptide YY or GLP-1. K cells of the duodenal, jejunal, and ileal mucosa secrete GIP, not peptide YY or GLP-1. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP and GLP-1 are the incretin hormones of the small intestine (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Gastrointestinal regulatory substances). COMLEX: incretins act on pancreatic beta cells after enteral glucose (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"I cells of the duodenum and jejunum secrete cholecystokinin, not peptide YY or GLP-1.","C":"S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin, not peptide YY or GLP-1.","D":"G cells of the stomach secrete gastrin, not peptide YY or GLP-1.","A":"K cells of the duodenal, jejunal, and ileal mucosa secrete GIP, not peptide YY or GLP-1."}},{"id":"sdl34_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":5,"stem":"Binding of GLP-1 to its receptor on pancreatic beta cells increases which second messenger to promote insulin release?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Incretin receptors on beta cells are G protein coupled receptors that activate adenylate cyclase. The resulting rise in cyclic AMP enhances glucose-stimulated insulin secretion. Cyclic GMP is the messenger of nitric oxide signaling through guanylyl cyclase, not of the incretin receptor that activates adenylate cyclase. Nitric oxide is a diffusible gas that signals through guanylyl cyclase; it is not the second messenger raised by GLP-1 receptor activation. Inositol trisphosphate comes from phospholipase C cleavage of PIP2 and mobilizes stored calcium, not the GLP-1 messenger. Diacylglycerol is the other phospholipase C product of PIP2 breakdown, not the GLP-1 messenger. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the incretin effect explains why oral glucose releases more insulin than IV glucose (First Aid Endocrine, Insulin). COMLEX: GIP and GLP-1 are the two principal incretins (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — oral glucose, incretins, and insulin release","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Cyclic GMP is the messenger of nitric oxide signaling through guanylyl cyclase, not of the incretin receptor that activates adenylate cyclase.","E":"Nitric oxide is a diffusible gas that signals through guanylyl cyclase; it is not the second messenger raised by GLP-1 receptor activation.","C":"Inositol trisphosphate comes from phospholipase C cleavage of PIP2 and mobilizes stored calcium, not the GLP-1 messenger.","D":"Diacylglycerol is the other phospholipase C product of PIP2 breakdown, not the GLP-1 messenger."}},{"id":"sdl34_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does obesity predispose to cholesterol gallstones?","choices":{"A":"It cuts hepatic cholesterol synthesis","B":"It raises biliary cholesterol output","C":"It speeds gallbladder emptying","D":"It increases biliary bile salt pools","E":"It promotes red cell hemolysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Obesity increases total body cholesterol and cholesterol turnover, raising biliary excretion of cholesterol and making bile supersaturated with it. Cutting hepatic cholesterol synthesis would lower biliary cholesterol and protect against stones. Faster gallbladder emptying would oppose stone formation, since hypomotility is a contributor. Larger bile salt pools would keep more cholesterol in micelles and oppose crystal formation. Red cell hemolysis leads to black pigment stones, not obesity-related cholesterol stones. Sources: Robbins ch 9, obesity; Robbins ch 18, gallbladder, risk factors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obesity raises biliary cholesterol excretion and is a major risk factor for cholesterol stones (Robbins ch 9). COMLEX: Rapid weight loss after bariatric surgery also raises risk, and ursodiol can be used for prevention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — biochemical pathogenesis of cholesterol gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cutting hepatic cholesterol synthesis would lower biliary","C":"Faster gallbladder emptying would oppose stone formation,","D":"Larger bile salt pools would keep more","E":"Red cell hemolysis leads to black pigment"}},{"id":"sdl34_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Obesity raises the risk of which type of esophageal cancer?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma","B":"Small cell carcinoma","C":"Adenocarcinoma","D":"Leiomyosarcoma","E":"Mucosal melanoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma of the lower third is associated with chronic GERD, Barrett esophagus, obesity, and smoking, and Robbins lists esophageal carcinoma among obesity-related cancers. Squamous cell carcinoma is tied to alcohol, smoking, hot liquids, and achalasia rather than obesity. Small cell carcinoma is a rare esophageal tumor and is not among the obesity-associated types. Leiomyosarcoma is a rare smooth muscle tumor with no described link to obesity. Mucosal melanoma is rare in the esophagus and is not an obesity-associated cancer. Sources: First Aid 2025 GI, esophageal cancer; Robbins ch 9, obesity and cancer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obesity, GERD, and Barrett esophagus predispose to distal esophageal adenocarcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Progressive dysphagia to solids in an obese patient with long-standing reflux warrants endoscopy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma is tied to alcohol,","B":"Small cell carcinoma is a rare esophageal","D":"Leiomyosarcoma is a rare smooth muscle tumor","E":"Mucosal melanoma is rare in the esophagus"}},{"id":"sdl34_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":5,"stem":"Obesity raises free estrogen in part by reducing hepatic synthesis of which protein?","choices":{"A":"Thyroxine binding globulin","B":"Corticosteroid binding globulin","C":"Vitamin D binding protein","D":"Retinol binding protein","E":"Sex hormone binding globulin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Obesity increases estrogen synthesis from androgens in adipose tissue and reduces hepatic synthesis of sex hormone binding globulin, raising free estrogen. This mechanism helps explain the higher risk of endometrial and breast cancer. Retinol binding protein carries vitamin A from the liver and does not regulate free estrogen. Thyroxine-binding globulin carries most plasma T4 and T3, not estrogen. Corticosteroid-binding globulin (transcortin) binds most plasma cortisol, not estrogen. Vitamin D-binding protein carries vitamin D to the liver; it does not regulate free estrogen. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aromatase in adipose tissue converts androgens to estrogen (First Aid Reproductive, Estrogen). COMLEX: hyperinsulinemia also suppresses sex hormone binding globulin (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"D":"Retinol binding protein carries vitamin A from the liver and does not regulate free estrogen.","A":"Thyroxine-binding globulin carries most plasma T4 and T3, not estrogen.","B":"Corticosteroid-binding globulin (transcortin) binds most plasma cortisol, not estrogen.","C":"Vitamin D-binding protein carries vitamin D to the liver; it does not regulate free estrogen."}},{"id":"sdl34_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy in a refugee camp was weaned after his sister was born and now eats mainly maize gruel. He is apathetic and has generalized pitting edema, peeling hyperpigmented skin patches, pale banded hair, and hepatomegaly. Serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which change most directly produces his edema?","choices":{"A":"Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","B":"Increased endothelial permeability","C":"Obstructed lymphatic drainage","D":"Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","E":"Increased interstitial sodium retention"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This child has kwashiorkor. Loss of visceral protein lowers serum albumin, which reduces plasma oncotic pressure and allows fluid to leave capillaries, producing generalized edema. The flaky paint dermatosis and banded hair are characteristic accompanying findings. Obstructed lymphatics cause lymphedema, whereas kwashiorkor edema results from hypoalbuminemia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a small child with a swollen belly and peeling skin is the classic kwashiorkor picture (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: kwashiorkor is also seen with protein-losing enteropathy, nephrotic syndrome, and burns (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Obstructed lymphatics cause lymphedema, whereas kwashiorkor edema results from hypoalbuminemia.","A":"Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure is not part of that answer.","B":"Increased endothelial permeability is not part of that answer.","E":"Increased interstitial sodium retention is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl34_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":5,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl in a drought region has had little food of any kind for months. She is emaciated with wasted limbs, no subcutaneous fat, and a head that looks large for her body. There is no edema. Her weight is 55% of expected for age. Which hormonal change most likely contributes to the loss of her fat stores?","choices":{"A":"Increased cortisol secretion","B":"Increased leptin secretion","C":"Increased insulin secretion","D":"Decreased glucagon secretion","E":"Decreased epinephrine secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This child has marasmus. Low leptin levels stimulate the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis, and the resulting high cortisol contributes to lipolysis. Fat and muscle are mobilized as energy sources. Leptin falls, not rises, in marasmus as fat stores shrink, and low leptin drives the cortisol rise. Glucagon secretion rises, not falls, when blood glucose drops in starvation. Hypoglycemia stimulates sympathetic outflow and adrenal epinephrine release, so epinephrine rises rather than falls. Robbins explains that low leptin in marasmus may stimulate the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis to produce high cortisol that contributes to lipolysis, not increased insulin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Marasmus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: marasmus results from overall calorie deficiency rather than isolated protein lack (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: marasmus also causes immune deficiency, especially of T cell mediated immunity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Leptin falls, not rises, in marasmus as fat stores shrink, and low leptin drives the cortisol rise.","D":"Glucagon secretion rises, not falls, when blood glucose drops in starvation.","E":"Hypoglycemia stimulates sympathetic outflow and adrenal epinephrine release, so epinephrine rises rather than falls.","C":"Robbins explains that low leptin in marasmus may stimulate the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis to produce high cortisol that contributes to lipolysis, not increased insulin."}},{"id":"sdl34_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":5,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy is treated for protein malnutrition with generalized edema, skin desquamation, and a fatty liver. After starting full-strength milk-based feeds he develops bloating and watery diarrhea. Stool is acidic and positive for reducing substances. Which mechanism most likely explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase deficiency","B":"Mucosal disaccharidase loss","C":"Cow milk protein allergy","D":"Ileal bile acid malabsorption","E":"Bacterial enterotoxin secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In kwashiorkor, small bowel crypt mitoses decrease, causing mucosal atrophy, loss of villi and microvilli, and secondary disaccharidase deficiency. Infants may therefore respond poorly to full-strength milk diets until the mucosa recovers. Acidic stool with reducing substances points to undigested sugar. Ileal bile acid malabsorption would not produce acidic stool with reducing substances. Bacterial enterotoxin secretion causes secretory diarrhea. Pancreatic lipase deficiency and cow milk protein allergy are different problems. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactase deficiency gives osmotic diarrhea with low stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the mucosal changes of kwashiorkor reverse with treatment (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"D":"Ileal bile acid malabsorption would not produce acidic stool with reducing substances.","E":"Bacterial enterotoxin secretion causes secretory diarrhea.","A":"Pancreatic lipase deficiency is a different cause of fat malabsorption.","C":"Cow milk protein allergy is an immune reaction to milk protein."}},{"id":"sdl34_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with Crohn disease had 120 cm of distal ileum resected 3 years ago. She has greasy stools and now has trouble seeing when driving at dusk. Examination shows dry conjunctivae with small gray foamy plaques. The deficient vitamin normally serves which function?","choices":{"A":"Cofactor for gamma carboxylation","B":"Membrane lipid antioxidant","C":"Regulator of calcium absorption","D":"Component of visual pigment","E":"Cofactor for collagen hydroxylation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vitamin A deficiency develops in fat malabsorption such as Crohn disease because absorption requires bile and lipid digestion. Retinal combines with opsin to form rhodopsin, so night blindness appears first, followed by xerophthalmia and Bitot spots. Gamma carboxylation is the role of vitamin K, whose deficiency causes bleeding, not night blindness. Membrane antioxidant protection is vitamin E, whose deficiency causes ataxia and hemolysis, not xerophthalmia. Regulating calcium absorption is vitamin D, whose deficiency causes osteomalacia, not night blindness. Collagen hydroxylation needs vitamin C, whose deficiency causes scurvy, not Bitot spots. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin A, Deficiency). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bitot spots and night blindness indicate vitamin A deficiency (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin A). COMLEX: vitamin A deficiency also causes squamous metaplasia of airway and urinary epithelium (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — terminal ileal disease and bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gamma carboxylation is the role of vitamin K, whose deficiency causes bleeding, not night blindness.","B":"Membrane antioxidant protection is vitamin E, whose deficiency causes ataxia and hemolysis, not xerophthalmia.","C":"Regulating calcium absorption is vitamin D, whose deficiency causes osteomalacia, not night blindness.","E":"Collagen hydroxylation needs vitamin C, whose deficiency causes scurvy, not Bitot spots."}},{"id":"sdl34_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis rarely takes her pancreatic enzymes. She has a wide-based gait, absent ankle reflexes, and loss of vibration and position sense. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) with MCV 88 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Serum methylmalonic acid is normal. Which process most likely underlies her findings?","choices":{"A":"Myelin methylation failure","B":"Clotting factor undercarboxylation","C":"Membrane lipid peroxidation","D":"Collagen hydroxylation failure","E":"Rhodopsin regeneration failure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vitamin E is a fat-soluble antioxidant that protects membrane lipids from free radical peroxidation. Deficiency, seen with fat malabsorption such as pancreatic insufficiency, causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia. A normal methylmalonic acid argues against B12 deficiency. Undercarboxylated clotting factors cause bleeding from vitamin K deficiency, not spinocerebellar degeneration. Failed collagen hydroxylation is scurvy, which causes bleeding gums and petechiae, not ataxia. Failed rhodopsin regeneration is vitamin A deficiency, which causes night blindness, not ataxia. Robbins lists vitamin E as the major free radical scavenging antioxidant whose deficiency causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia, not a myelin methylation defect. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin E). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin E deficiency mimics B12 neuropathy without megaloblastosis or raised methylmalonic acid (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin E). COMLEX: vitamin E prevents oxidation of unsaturated fats in cell membranes (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Undercarboxylated clotting factors cause bleeding from vitamin K deficiency, not spinocerebellar degeneration.","D":"Failed collagen hydroxylation is scurvy, which causes bleeding gums and petechiae, not ataxia.","E":"Failed rhodopsin regeneration is vitamin A deficiency, which causes night blindness, not ataxia.","A":"Robbins lists vitamin E as the major free radical scavenging antioxidant whose deficiency causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia, not a myelin methylation defect."}},{"id":"sdl34_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with poorly controlled celiac disease has diffuse bone pain and proximal muscle weakness. Serum calcium is 8.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL), phosphorus 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL), alkaline phosphatase 210 U/L (reference range: 25–100 U/L), and PTH 98 pg/mL (reference range: 10–65 pg/mL). A bone biopsy would most likely show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization","B":"Mosaic pattern of lamellar bone","C":"Marrow fibrosis with brown tumors","D":"Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae","E":"Thickened seams of unmineralized osteoid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin D malabsorption leads to secondary hyperparathyroidism, which restores calcium toward normal but leaves phosphate low. Impaired mineralization in the adult produces osteomalacia, in which excess unmineralized osteoid accumulates on bone surfaces. Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization describe osteoporosis, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia. A mosaic pattern of lamellar bone is the hallmark of Paget disease, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia. Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae mark osteonecrosis, an infarction of bone, not defective mineralization. Robbins describes osteomalacia as excess persistent, inadequately mineralized osteoid seen as a thickened matrix layer, not marrow fibrosis with brown tumors. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Deficiency States). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteomalacia shows low calcium or phosphate with high PTH and alkaline phosphatase (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Osteomalacia). COMLEX: the same defect in children causes rickets with growth plate deformities (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization describe osteoporosis, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia.","B":"A mosaic pattern of lamellar bone is the hallmark of Paget disease, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia.","D":"Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae mark osteonecrosis, an infarction of bone, not defective mineralization.","C":"Robbins describes osteomalacia as excess persistent, inadequately mineralized osteoid seen as a thickened matrix layer, not marrow fibrosis with brown tumors."}},{"id":"sdl34_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with alcohol use disorder is admitted with confusion and receives an IV dextrose infusion. Hours later he has nystagmus, bilateral lateral gaze palsy, and a wide-based gait. Serum lactate is 42 mg/dL (reference range: 4.5–19.8 mg/dL). Reduced activity of which enzyme most directly explains his lactate rise?","choices":{"A":"Lactate dehydrogenase","B":"Pyruvate carboxylase","C":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase","D":"Fructose bisphosphatase","E":"Phosphofructokinase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the coenzyme for oxidative decarboxylation reactions, including pyruvate dehydrogenase. Without it, pyruvate is shunted to lactate, and a glucose load worsens the deficit. Ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and confusion indicate Wernicke encephalopathy. Lactate dehydrogenase is not thiamine-dependent and actually forms the excess lactate. Pyruvate carboxylase is a biotin-containing carboxylase that adds CO2 to pyruvate to form oxaloacetate; it is not thiamine-dependent. Phosphofructokinase-1 is the allosteric rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis, not a thiamine-dependent oxidative decarboxylation. Fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase is a gluconeogenic enzyme, not a thiamine pyrophosphate-dependent enzyme like pyruvate dehydrogenase. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.9, Vitamins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Guyton Ch. 72 (Thiamine).","boardPrep":"Step 1: give thiamine before dextrose in alcohol use disorder to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B1). COMLEX: thiamine deficiency also causes dry and wet beriberi (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactate dehydrogenase is not thiamine-dependent and actually forms the excess lactate.","B":"Pyruvate carboxylase is a biotin-containing carboxylase that adds CO2 to pyruvate to form oxaloacetate; it is not thiamine-dependent.","E":"Phosphofructokinase-1 is the allosteric rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis, not a thiamine-dependent oxidative decarboxylation.","D":"Fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase is a gluconeogenic enzyme, not a thiamine pyrophosphate-dependent enzyme like pyruvate dehydrogenase."}},{"id":"sdl34_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a metastatic midgut neuroendocrine tumor with episodic flushing and diarrhea. Over months she develops a rough dermatitis on sun-exposed skin of her neck and forearms and new forgetfulness. Diversion of which amino acid most likely explains her new findings?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine","B":"Histidine","C":"Methionine","D":"Phenylalanine","E":"Tryptophan"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Niacin can be synthesized from tryptophan. Carcinoid tumors divert tryptophan toward serotonin, reducing niacin synthesis and producing pellagra with dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia. Tyrosine is the precursor converted through dopa to the catecholamines, not the substrate diverted to serotonin. Histidine is decarboxylated to histamine, not diverted to serotonin. Phenylalanine is hydroxylated to tyrosine by phenylalanine hydroxylase, not used to make serotonin or niacin. Methionine supplies the sulfur for cysteine synthesis; niacin is synthesized from tryptophan, not methionine. Sources: First Aid Biochemistry (Vitamin B3: niacin from tryptophan; carcinoid syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pellagra occurs with carcinoid syndrome, Hartnup disease, and isoniazid use (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B3). COMLEX: niacin forms NAD and NADP for redox reactions (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Tyrosine is the precursor converted through dopa to the catecholamines, not the substrate diverted to serotonin.","B":"Histidine is decarboxylated to histamine, not diverted to serotonin.","D":"Phenylalanine is hydroxylated to tyrosine by phenylalanine hydroxylase, not used to make serotonin or niacin.","C":"Methionine supplies the sulfur for cysteine synthesis; niacin is synthesized from tryptophan, not methionine."}},{"id":"sdl34_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has received home parenteral nutrition for 6 months using a trace element mix that omitted zinc, with other trace elements supplied after extensive small bowel resection. He now has a red scaly rash around his mouth, nose, and anus, worsening diarrhea, poor wound healing, and loss of taste. Which additional finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Impaired night vision","B":"Perifollicular hemorrhages","C":"Ring sideroblastic anemia","D":"Goiter with hypothyroidism","E":"Dilated cardiomyopathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zinc deficiency from zinc-free parenteral nutrition causes a periorificial rash like acrodermatitis enteropathica, anorexia, diarrhea, poor wound healing, and impaired night vision. Zinc is a component of many enzymes, principally oxidases. Perifollicular hemorrhages indicate vitamin C deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Ring sideroblastic anemia indicates copper deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Goiter with hypothyroidism indicates iodine deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Dilated cardiomyopathy indicates selenium deficiency (Keshan disease), not zinc deficiency. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zinc deficiency causes delayed wound healing, dysgeusia, and hypogonadism (First Aid Biochemistry, Zinc). COMLEX: acrodermatitis enteropathica is the inherited form of zinc malabsorption (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Perifollicular hemorrhages indicate vitamin C deficiency, not zinc deficiency.","C":"Ring sideroblastic anemia indicates copper deficiency, not zinc deficiency.","D":"Goiter with hypothyroidism indicates iodine deficiency, not zinc deficiency.","E":"Dilated cardiomyopathy indicates selenium deficiency (Keshan disease), not zinc deficiency."}},{"id":"sdl34_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has had insatiable hunger and severe obesity since infancy. Serum leptin is undetectable, and sequencing shows a homozygous mutation in the leptin gene. She begins daily recombinant leptin injections. The expected decrease in her food intake is mediated mainly by which hypothalamic effect?","choices":{"A":"Activation of NPY and AgRP neurons","B":"Activation of lateral orexin neurons","C":"Activation of lateral MCH neurons","D":"Inhibition of paraventricular TRH neurons","E":"Activation of POMC and CART neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leptin acts in the arcuate nucleus to stimulate POMC and CART neurons, which release alpha MSH to act on melanocortin receptors and decrease food intake. It also inhibits NPY and AgRP neurons that drive feeding. Orexins A and B are orexigenic and stimulate feeding, so their activation would not reduce intake. Melanin-concentrating hormone is orexigenic and increases feeding, so activating MCH neurons would not reduce intake. Leptin inhibits NPY/AgRP neurons, the appetite gas pedal, so activating them would increase rather than reduce food intake. Second-order neurons downstream of POMC/CART produce TRH, BDNF, and CRH to reduce food intake, so inhibiting TRH neurons would oppose leptin's effect. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Leptin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin is secreted by adipose tissue and decreases appetite (First Aid Endocrine, Appetite regulation). COMLEX: mutations of leptin or its receptor cause massive obesity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Orexins A and B are orexigenic and stimulate feeding, so their activation would not reduce intake.","C":"Melanin-concentrating hormone is orexigenic and increases feeding, so activating MCH neurons would not reduce intake.","A":"Leptin inhibits NPY/AgRP neurons, the appetite gas pedal, so activating them would increase rather than reduce food intake.","D":"Second-order neurons downstream of POMC/CART produce TRH, BDNF, and CRH to reduce food intake, so inhibiting TRH neurons would oppose leptin's effect."}},{"id":"sdl34_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a BMI of 44 undergoes Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. Six months later she reports much less hunger before meals than she had with prior diets. A blunted rise of which hormone most likely contributes?","choices":{"A":"Leptin","B":"Peptide YY","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Insulin","E":"Cholecystokinin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ghrelin is an orexigenic gut hormone that increases food intake. It is produced mainly in the stomach, rises before meals, and acts on NPY and AgRP neurons. Its preprandial rise is blunted after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, which may contribute to weight loss. Leptin is a long-term fat-mass signal that suppresses appetite, not the premeal hunger hormone blunted by bypass. Peptide YY rises after meals and reduces appetite, so a blunted rise would not lessen hunger. Insulin suppresses appetite, so a blunted insulin rise would not explain reduced hunger. Cholecystokinin is released after food ingestion and suppresses further feeding, so it does not drive pre-meal hunger as fasting ghrelin does. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in Prader-Willi syndrome and with sleep deprivation (First Aid Endocrine, Appetite regulation). COMLEX: ghrelin levels are typically lower in obese individuals (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Leptin is a long-term fat-mass signal that suppresses appetite, not the premeal hunger hormone blunted by bypass.","B":"Peptide YY rises after meals and reduces appetite, so a blunted rise would not lessen hunger.","D":"Insulin suppresses appetite, so a blunted insulin rise would not explain reduced hunger.","E":"Cholecystokinin is released after food ingestion and suppresses further feeding, so it does not drive pre-meal hunger as fasting ghrelin does."}},{"id":"sdl34_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and a BMI of 34 starts a weekly injected drug that mimics a hormone of ileal and colonic endocrine cells. Over 6 months his hemoglobin A1c improves and he loses 9 kg. Which action of the drug most likely accounts for his weight loss?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption","B":"Central suppression of food intake","C":"Increased brown fat thermogenesis","D":"Increased renal glucose excretion","E":"Decreased hepatic glycogen storage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GLP-1 from intestinal L cells is an incretin that augments insulin release, and it also acts on the hypothalamus to decrease food intake and slows gastric emptying. GLP-1 receptor agonists produce weight loss mainly through reduced appetite. Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption is the action of the GI lipase inhibitor orlistat, not of a GLP-1 mimetic. Increased renal glucose excretion is the action of SGLT2 inhibitors, not of a GLP-1 receptor agonist. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Costanzo Ch. 8 (Hunger and Satiety).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 agonists decrease appetite and slow gastric emptying (First Aid Endocrine, Diabetes mellitus therapy). COMLEX: lipase inhibitors reduce fat absorption, a different weight loss mechanism (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — oral glucose, incretins, and insulin release","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption is the action of the GI lipase inhibitor orlistat, not of a GLP-1 mimetic.","D":"Increased renal glucose excretion is the action of SGLT2 inhibitors, not of a GLP-1 receptor agonist."}},{"id":"sdl34_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":5,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with a BMI of 37 has acanthosis nigricans and a waist circumference of 118 cm. Fasting glucose is 118 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), triglycerides 260 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL), and HDL 32 mg/dL (reference range: 40–60 mg/dL). Which mediator, released after inflammasome activation by excess free fatty acids, most likely drives his insulin resistance?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin 10","B":"Interleukin 4","C":"Transforming growth factor beta","D":"Interleukin 1","E":"Interferon alpha"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In obesity, excess free fatty acids activate the inflammasome, which releases interleukin 1 and promotes insulin resistance. IL-10 and TGF-beta limit immune responses. IL-4 drives allergic IgE switching. Interferon alpha is a type I interferon made in viral infection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Metabolic Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metabolic syndrome combines central obesity, hypertension, hyperglycemia, and dyslipidemia (First Aid Endocrine, Metabolic syndrome). COMLEX: obesity is associated with high triglycerides and low HDL (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that serves mainly to limit and terminate immune responses, not to drive obesity-associated inflammation.","B":"IL-4 is a signature Th2 cytokine that drives IgE isotype switching in allergic responses.","C":"TGF-beta is an anti-inflammatory and fibrogenic cytokine that limits immune responses.","E":"Interferon alpha is a type I interferon made in viral infection."}},{"id":"sdl34_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":5,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with a BMI of 38 and type 2 diabetes drinks alcohol rarely. ALT is 78 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L) and AST is 52 U/L (reference range: 12–38 U/L). Ultrasound shows a diffusely echogenic liver, and viral and autoimmune studies are negative. A reduced serum level of which adipose hormone most likely contributes to her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Adiponectin","B":"Leptin","C":"Resistin","D":"Angiotensinogen","E":"Testosterone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease. Adiponectin, which is reduced in obesity, increases fatty acid oxidation and insulin sensitivity in liver and muscle. Its decline contributes to insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, and hepatic steatosis. Leptin is elevated, not reduced, in obesity because of central leptin resistance. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Adiponectin; Obesity, Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MASLD is the hepatic manifestation of metabolic syndrome, with ALT usually above AST (First Aid Gastrointestinal, MASLD). COMLEX: obesity related steatosis may progress to steatohepatitis and fibrosis (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Leptin is elevated, not reduced, in obesity because of central leptin resistance.","C":"Resistin is not part of that answer.","D":"Angiotensinogen is not part of that answer.","E":"Testosterone is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl34_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with a BMI of 39 and fasting hyperinsulinemia is diagnosed with sigmoid adenocarcinoma. His physician explains that high insulin raises free levels of a growth factor that stimulates tumor cell proliferation. Through which signaling route does this growth factor mainly act?","choices":{"A":"Wnt and catenin pathways","B":"RAS and PI3K pathways","C":"Notch and Hedgehog pathways","D":"TGF beta and SMAD pathways","E":"JAK and STAT pathways"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hyperinsulinemia raises free insulin-like growth factor 1, a mitogen whose receptor activates the RAS and PI3K AKT pathways and promotes growth of tumor cells. This is one proposed link between obesity and colorectal and other cancers. Wnt and beta-catenin signaling is the APC pathway, not the target of insulin-like growth factor 1. TGF-beta and SMAD signaling is a growth-inhibitory pathway lost in colon cancer, not the IGF-1 mitogenic route. JAK/STAT signaling is activated by JAK2 point mutations in myeloproliferative neoplasms, not the main IGF-1 receptor route. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer risk factors include obesity, red meat, and a low fiber diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: excess adipose estrogen production is another obesity cancer link (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma risk factors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Wnt and beta-catenin signaling is the APC pathway, not the target of insulin-like growth factor 1.","D":"TGF-beta and SMAD signaling is a growth-inhibitory pathway lost in colon cancer, not the IGF-1 mitogenic route.","E":"JAK/STAT signaling is activated by JAK2 point mutations in myeloproliferative neoplasms, not the main IGF-1 receptor route."}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 14-month-old girl in the United States has been fed only a rice beverage since weaning. She has generalized edema, flaky skin, and a fatty liver. Which tissue is relatively spared?","choices":{"A":"Subcutaneous fat stores","B":"Hepatic protein stores","C":"Plasma albumin pool","D":"Thymic lymphoid tissue","E":"Intestinal villous mucosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In kwashiorkor, the visceral compartment is markedly depleted while loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle mass is relatively mild and masked by edema. Thymic and lymphoid atrophy and small bowel villous loss are prominent. Robbins notes cases in developed countries from milk replacement with rice-based beverages. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: kwashiorkor follows a diet deficient in protein despite adequate calories (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: marasmus depletes subcutaneous fat and muscle more severely (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has taken no food by mouth for 2 weeks while receiving broad-spectrum antibiotics. He now oozes from IV sites. PT is 24 s (reference range: 11–15 s). He takes no anticoagulants. What is the main cause?","choices":{"A":"Increased hepatic vitamin breakdown","B":"Increased renal vitamin loss","C":"Blocked epoxide reductase activity","D":"Loss of gut bacterial synthesis","E":"Impaired platelet granule release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vitamin K comes from the diet and from intestinal bacteria. Without oral intake and with gut flora suppressed by antibiotics, vitamin K dependent factors fall and the PT rises. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin K). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Guyton Ch. 72 (Vitamin K).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin K deficiency prolongs PT and aPTT with a normal bleeding time (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin K). COMLEX: vitamin K is a cofactor for hepatic carboxylation of clotting factors (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old widower eats mostly tea and toast. He has bleeding gums, corkscrew hairs, and perifollicular hemorrhages. The deficient vitamin is a cofactor for which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Gamma glutamyl carboxylase","B":"Prolyl hydroxylase","C":"Pyruvate carboxylase","D":"Glutathione peroxidase","E":"Transketolase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vitamin C activates prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases needed for collagen hydroxylation. Deficiency, seen in older adults with poor diets, causes scurvy with gum bleeding and perifollicular hemorrhages. Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver uses vitamin K as its cofactor. Glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not vitamin C. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin C). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: scurvy causes swollen gums, bruising, and corkscrew hair (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin C). COMLEX: vitamin C excess can cause oxalate stones and iron overload (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver uses vitamin K as its cofactor.","D":"Glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not vitamin C."}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with a BMI of 41 has a high serum leptin level. She remains hungry despite large fat stores. What best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Hypothalamic leptin resistance","B":"Rapid renal leptin clearance","C":"Circulating antileptin antibodies","D":"Reduced adipocyte leptin release","E":"Excess hepatic leptin uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leptin is secreted in proportion to fat stores, so obese people usually have high leptin. Most are resistant to its central effects, so the satiety signal fails despite abundant hormone. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Leptin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin acts on the ventromedial hypothalamus to promote satiety (First Aid Neurology, Hypothalamus). COMLEX: leptin gene mutations are a rare cause of massive obesity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with a BMI of 46 had a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and has lost 30 kg over 5 months. She now has episodes of postprandial right upper quadrant pain. Ultrasound shows multiple mobile gallstones. Bilirubin, hemoglobin, and reticulocyte count are normal, and she has no history of biliary infection. Which mechanism best explains her stones?","choices":{"A":"Excess unconjugated bilirubin from hemolysis","B":"Bacterial beta-glucuronidase acting in bile","C":"Cholesterol-supersaturated bile with stasis","D":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption from bypass","E":"Reduced hepatic secretion of cholesterol into bile"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Obesity and rapid weight reduction are risk factors for cholesterol gallstones, which form when bile becomes supersaturated with cholesterol beyond the solubilizing capacity of bile salts and lecithin and the gallbladder empties poorly, letting crystals nucleate and grow. Excess unconjugated bilirubin from hemolysis produces pigment stones, and her hemoglobin and reticulocyte count are normal. Bacterial beta-glucuronidase acting in bile forms pigment stones in biliary infection, which she does not have. Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption occurs with ileal disease or resection, and gastric bypass leaves the ileum in continuity. Reduced hepatic secretion of cholesterol into bile would lower stone risk rather than raise it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18 (Cholelithiasis, risk factors and pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cholesterol stones need supersaturated bile, gallbladder hypomotility, crystal nucleation, and mucus hypersecretion; obesity and rapid weight loss are risk factors (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: counsel patients after bariatric surgery about symptomatic gallstones during rapid weight loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Excess unconjugated bilirubin from hemolysis produces pigment","B":"Bacterial beta-glucuronidase acting in bile forms pigment","D":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption occurs","E":"Reduced hepatic secretion of cholesterol into bile"}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":5,"stem":"A 10-month-old breastfed infant living in a northern city has soft skull bones and bowed legs. Which chest finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Sternal pectus excavatum","B":"Costochondral beading","C":"Clavicular callus formation","D":"Inferior rib notching","E":"Scapular winging"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vitamin D deficiency in children causes rickets. Overgrowth of cartilage at costochondral junctions produces the rachitic rosary, along with craniotabes and leg bowing. Breastfed infants with little sun exposure are at risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Rickets). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rickets shows rachitic rosary, bowed legs, and craniotabes (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin D). COMLEX: the chest may also show a pigeon breast deformity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"grid_sdl34_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":4,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who drinks 10 beers daily and eats little besides crackers has fatigue and a sore, smooth red tongue. Neurologic examination, including vibration and position sense, is normal. Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and a peripheral smear shows hypersegmented neutrophils. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["MCV","Serum methylmalonic acid","Serum homocysteine"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↑"],"B":["↓","↔","↔"],"C":["↓","↔","↑"],"D":["↔","↑","↑"],"E":["↑","↔","↑"]},"correct":"E","explanation":"Choice E (↑ MCV, ↔ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is correct. Poor intake with heavy alcohol use causes folate deficiency; the notes describe folate as essential for one-carbon transfers in purine and thymidylate synthesis, so deficiency causes a megaloblastic anemia with hypersegmented neutrophils but no neurologic deficits. Without methyl-tetrahydrofolate, homocysteine is not remethylated and rises, while methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, which needs B12 but not folate, keeps working. Choice A (↑ MCV, ↑ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is vitamin B12 deficiency, as after ileal resection or in atrophic gastritis, which also causes subacute combined degeneration with loss of vibration and position sense. Choice B (↓ MCV, ↔ serum methylmalonic acid, ↔ serum homocysteine) is iron deficiency, a microcytic anemia. Choice C (↓ MCV, ↔ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is vitamin B6 deficiency, which causes a microcytic sideroblastic anemia and impairs the B6-dependent conversion of homocysteine to cystathionine. Choice D (↔ MCV, ↑ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is the anemia of chronic kidney disease, a normocytic anemia from low erythropoietin in which reduced renal clearance raises both metabolites. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 34, section on water-soluble vitamins: B12, folate); First Aid 2025 Biochemistry (folate, B12, and B6 deficiency; methylmalonic acid and homocysteine) and Hematology (microcytic and macrocytic anemias). The laboratory arrows are from First Aid because the notes do not list them; the raised metabolites in chronic kidney disease are from general clinical chemistry and are inferred.","boardPrep":"Step 1: folate and B12 deficiency both raise homocysteine, but only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid and causes neurologic deficits (First Aid 2025 Biochemistry). COMLEX: replacing folate in B12 deficiency can correct the anemia while the neurologic damage progresses.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — intrinsic factor and B12 absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein compartment is depleted most in marasmus?","choices":{"A":"Visceral protein, with muscle spared","B":"Somatic protein in skeletal muscle","C":"Only plasma immunoglobulins, with a fatty liver","D":"Surfactant protein in the lung","E":"Collagen in bone, with edema as the main sign"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Marasmus burns the somatic protein compartment in skeletal muscle, while the visceral compartment is relatively spared. Visceral protein loss with relative sparing of muscle is the kwashiorkor pattern. A fatty liver with visceral protein loss is kwashiorkor, not marasmus. Lung surfactant is not the compartment marasmus consumes. Edema is absent in marasmus, and bone collagen is not the compartment that is mobilized.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Visceral protein loss with relative sparing of muscle is the kwashiorkor pattern","C":"A fatty liver with visceral protein loss is kwashiorkor, not marasmus","D":"Lung surfactant is not the compartment marasmus consumes","E":"Edema is absent in marasmus, and bone collagen is not the compartment that is mobilized"}},{"id":"sdl34_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Serum albumin in marasmus is usually which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Extremely low, and the cause of severe edema","B":"Undetectable in every case","C":"High, from dehydration only","D":"Normal or only slightly reduced","E":"Replaced by a monoclonal spike"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Albumin stays normal or only slightly reduced in marasmus because visceral protein synthesis is relatively preserved. Very low albumin with edema is the kwashiorkor laboratory picture. Albumin is not undetectable in marasmus. Marasmus does not raise albumin; it leaves it near normal. A monoclonal spike is a plasma-cell disorder, not marasmus.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Very low albumin with edema is the kwashiorkor laboratory picture","B":"Albumin is not undetectable in marasmus","C":"Marasmus does not raise albumin; it leaves it near normal","E":"A monoclonal spike is a plasma-cell disorder, not marasmus"}},{"id":"sdl34_q33","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy was weaned onto maize gruel when his sister was born. He is apathetic, with swelling of the face and legs, sparse reddish hair, and patches of peeling hyperpigmented skin. The abdomen is protuberant and the liver is enlarged. Albumin is 1.8 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL) and glucose is 70 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). Which skin change is characteristic?","choices":{"A":"Rose spots on the trunk","B":"Dark macules on the lips","C":"Bronze skin with diabetes","D":"A serpiginous track on the sole","E":"Flaky-paint desquamation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kwashiorkor produces flaky-paint skin, with zones of hyperpigmentation, peeling, and hypopigmentation, plus edema and a fatty liver. Rose spots are the rash of typhoid fever. Dark lip macules are the pigment of Peutz-Jeghers syndrome. Bronze skin with diabetes suggests hereditary hemochromatosis. A serpiginous sole track is cutaneous larva migrans.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rose spots are the rash of typhoid fever","B":"Dark lip macules are the pigment of Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","C":"Bronze skin with diabetes suggests hereditary hemochromatosis","D":"A serpiginous sole track is cutaneous larva migrans"}},{"id":"sdl34_q34","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl in a drought region has had almost no food for months. Her limbs are wasted, subcutaneous fat is gone, the head looks large for the body, and there is no edema. Weight is far below the standard for her age. She has oral thrush. Albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL) and glucose is 68 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). Which immune problem fits this state?","choices":{"A":"Selective IgA deficiency as the cause of the wasting","B":"A high eosinophil count from a luminal tapeworm","C":"T-cell immune deficiency","D":"Autoantibodies against parietal cells","E":"Complement consumption from a liver abscess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Marasmus includes T-cell immune deficiency, so these children develop infections while albumin stays near normal and edema is absent. Selective IgA deficiency is not the mechanism of this calorie-starved state. A luminal tapeworm does not produce this total loss of fat and muscle with thrush. Parietal-cell antibodies cause pernicious anemia, not this picture of starvation. Complement consumption from an amebic abscess is a different illness.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Selective IgA deficiency is not the mechanism of this calorie-starved state","B":"A luminal tapeworm does not produce this total loss of fat and muscle with thrush","D":"Parietal-cell antibodies cause pernicious anemia, not this picture of starvation","E":"Complement consumption from an amebic abscess is a different illness"}},{"id":"sdl34_q35","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Vitamin A is stored in which hepatic cell?","choices":{"A":"Stellate cell","B":"Kupffer cell","C":"Cholangiocyte","D":"Endothelial cell of the portal vein","E":"Gallbladder epithelial cell"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vitamin A is stored in hepatic stellate cells and travels in blood bound to retinol-binding protein. Kupffer cells are the sinusoidal macrophages and are not the vitamin A store. Cholangiocytes line bile ducts and do not store vitamin A. Portal-vein endothelium is not the vitamin A reservoir. Gallbladder epithelium concentrates bile and does not store vitamin A.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Kupffer cells are the sinusoidal macrophages and are not the vitamin A store","C":"Cholangiocytes line bile ducts and do not store vitamin A","D":"Portal-vein endothelium is not the vitamin A reservoir","E":"Gallbladder epithelium concentrates bile and does not store vitamin A"}},{"id":"sdl34_q36","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Active vitamin D increases intestinal calcium absorption by inducing which enterocyte protein?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"Apolipoprotein B","C":"Calbindin","D":"Trypsinogen","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D induces calbindin, which ferries calcium across the enterocyte. Intrinsic factor is made by gastric parietal cells for vitamin B12, not by vitamin D in the enterocyte. Apolipoprotein B is required to export chylomicrons, not to absorb calcium. Trypsinogen is a pancreatic zymogen. Pepsinogen is the gastric protease precursor.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intrinsic factor is made by gastric parietal cells for vitamin B12, not by vitamin D in the enterocyte","B":"Apolipoprotein B is required to export chylomicrons, not to absorb calcium","D":"Trypsinogen is a pancreatic zymogen","E":"Pepsinogen is the gastric protease precursor"}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy with long-standing cholestasis has an unsteady gait and absent ankle reflexes. He rarely takes the prescribed enzymes. Hemoglobin has fallen to 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL), bilirubin is 2.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL), and the smear shows no schistocytes from a microangiopathy. The neurologic findings fit a fat-soluble vitamin problem. Which blood consequence does that same deficiency produce?","choices":{"A":"Iron-deficiency microcytosis from hookworms","B":"A high neutrophil count from an abscess","C":"Sickle cells","D":"Megaloblastic anemia from folate lack, with no nerve damage","E":"Hemolysis from a shortened red-cell life span"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin E deficiency causes spinocerebellar degeneration and also shortens red-cell survival, leading to hemolytic anemia. Hookworm iron deficiency is a separate cause of anemia and does not produce this ataxia. Neutrophilia from an abscess is not the hematologic result of vitamin E deficiency. Sickle cells are a hemoglobinopathy, not vitamin E deficiency. Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia without the spinal-cord picture, and vitamin E deficiency does damage nerves.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hookworm iron deficiency is a separate cause of anemia and does not produce this ataxia","B":"Neutrophilia from an abscess is not the hematologic result of vitamin E deficiency","C":"Sickle cells are a hemoglobinopathy, not vitamin E deficiency","D":"Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia without the spinal-cord picture, and vitamin E deficiency does damage nerves"}},{"id":"sdl34_q37","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"Agouti-related peptide increases food intake by blocking which receptor?","choices":{"A":"The insulin receptor on muscle","B":"The epidermal growth factor receptor","C":"The vitamin D receptor","D":"The melanocortin-4 receptor","E":"The bile-salt export pump"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Agouti-related peptide is a competitive antagonist at the melanocortin-4 receptor, which removes the satiety signal of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone. The insulin receptor is not the target Agouti-related peptide blocks to drive hunger. The epidermal growth factor receptor is a growth-factor target, not this appetite receptor. The vitamin D receptor mediates calbindin induction, not the orexigenic block. The bile-salt export pump moves bile acids out of hepatocytes and is not an appetite receptor.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The insulin receptor is not the target Agouti-related peptide blocks to drive hunger","B":"The epidermal growth factor receptor is a growth-factor target, not this appetite receptor","C":"The vitamin D receptor mediates calbindin induction, not the orexigenic block","E":"The bile-salt export pump moves bile acids out of hepatocytes and is not an appetite receptor"}},{"id":"sdl34_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Intestinal-type gastric cancer is linked to which dietary exposure?","choices":{"A":"Salted, smoked, or preserved foods","B":"A diet of only fresh fruit","C":"Parenteral vitamin B12","D":"Pasteurized milk alone","E":"A gluten-free diet"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma is linked to dietary N-nitroso compounds and benzo[a]pyrene in salted, smoked, or preserved foods. Fresh fruit is not the exposure linked to intestinal-type gastric cancer. Parenteral vitamin B12 treats deficiency and is not a gastric carcinogen. Pasteurized milk is not the preserved-food exposure named for this cancer. Avoiding gluten treats celiac disease and is not the risk named for intestinal-type gastric cancer.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Fresh fruit is not the exposure linked to intestinal-type gastric cancer","C":"Parenteral vitamin B12 treats deficiency and is not a gastric carcinogen","D":"Pasteurized milk is not the preserved-food exposure named for this cancer","E":"Avoiding gluten treats celiac disease and is not the risk named for intestinal-type gastric cancer"}},{"id":"sdl34_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with a body mass index of 36 has taken a combined oral contraceptive for eight years. She now has episodic right-upper-quadrant pain after fatty meals. She drinks little alcohol. The abdomen is soft and she is not jaundiced. Alkaline phosphatase is 110 U/L (reference range: 40–129 U/L) and alanine aminotransferase is 28 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L). Ultrasound shows gallbladder stones that shadow. Estrogen promoted the stones by stimulating which hepatic enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Alcohol dehydrogenase","B":"Pepsin","C":"Enterokinase","D":"Thymidylate synthase","E":"HMG-CoA reductase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Estrogen stimulates hepatic HMG-CoA reductase and increases biliary cholesterol secretion, which favors cholesterol stones. Alcohol dehydrogenase metabolizes ethanol and is not the estrogen target that raises biliary cholesterol. Pepsin is the gastric protease. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen. Thymidylate synthase is the target of fluoropyrimidines, not of estrogen in the liver.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Alcohol dehydrogenase metabolizes ethanol and is not the estrogen target that raises biliary cholesterol","B":"Pepsin is the gastric protease","C":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen","D":"Thymidylate synthase is the target of fluoropyrimidines, not of estrogen in the liver"}},{"id":"sdl34_q40","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 41 has had heartburn for years. Endoscopy shows salmon-colored mucosa in the distal esophagus and a mass at the gastroesophageal junction. He does not smoke. Hemoglobin is 11.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which mechanical effect of his central fat started this sequence?","choices":{"A":"Portal-vein thrombosis","B":"Higher intra-abdominal pressure with reflux","C":"Occlusion of the inferior mesenteric artery","D":"A volvulus around the superior mesenteric artery","E":"Failure of the anal membrane to open"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Central obesity raises intra-abdominal pressure, which promotes reflux and Barrett esophagus and thereby esophageal adenocarcinoma. Portal-vein thrombosis causes portal hypertension and is not the mechanical start of this reflux sequence. Inferior mesenteric occlusion causes hindgut ischemia, not Barrett esophagus. Midgut volvulus is an embryologic accident, not the result of central fat. Failure of the anal membrane to break down causes imperforate anus.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal-vein thrombosis causes portal hypertension and is not the mechanical start of this reflux sequence","C":"Inferior mesenteric occlusion causes hindgut ischemia, not Barrett esophagus","D":"Midgut volvulus is an embryologic accident, not the result of central fat","E":"Failure of the anal membrane to break down causes imperforate anus"}}]};
